 horror mr'),(4850,1,47979,8619,'gardiner readily promised every assistance in his power'),(4851,1,2133,6608,'elizabeth though expecting no less thanked him with tears of gratitude and all three being actuated by one spirit everything relating to their journey was speedily settled'),(4852,1,1056,346,'they were to be off as soon as possible'),(4853,1,295,3453,'but what is to be done about pemberley'),(4854,1,4916,273,'cried mrs'),(4855,1,1015,636,'john told us mr'),(4856,1,2154,1051,'darcy was here when you sent for us--was it so'),(4857,1,9487,18050,'yes and i told him we should not be able to keep our engagement'),(4858,1,163,6608,'that is all settled'),(4859,1,994,6608,'what is all settled'),(4860,1,12410,31712,'repeated the other as she ran into her room to prepare'),(4861,1,1,4788,'and are they upon such terms as for her to disclose the real truth'),(4862,1,5037,473,'oh that i knew how it was'),(4863,1,295,8903,'but wishes were vain or at best could serve only to amuse her in the hurry and confusion of the following hour'),(4864,1,259,42453,'had elizabeth been at leisure to be idle she would have remained certain that all employment was impossible to one so wretched as herself but she had her share of business as well as her aunt and amongst the rest there were notes to be written to all their friends at lambton with false excuses for their sudden departure'),(4865,1,573,636,'an hour however saw the whole completed and mr'),(4866,1,47979,7709,'gardiner meanwhile having settled his account at the inn nothing remained to be done but to go and elizabeth after all the misery of the morning found herself in a shorter space of time than she could have supposed seated in the carriage and on the road to longbourn'),(4867,1,4305,91860,'illustration the first pleasing earnest of their welcome chapter xlvii'),(4868,1,4305,6358,'illustration i have been thinking it over again elizabeth said her uncle as they drove from the town and really upon serious consideration i am much more inclined than i was to judge as your eldest sister does of the matter'),(4869,1,67,82,'it appears to me so very unlikely that any young man should form such a design against a girl who is by no means unprotected or friendless and who was actually staying in his colonels family that i am strongly inclined to hope the best'),(4870,1,575,16209,'could he expect that her friends would not step forward'),(4871,1,575,11302,'could he expect to be noticed again by the regiment after such an affront to colonel forster'),(4872,1,892,38172,'his temptation is not adequate to the risk'),(4873,1,513,1051,'do you really think so'),(4874,1,4916,2845,'cried elizabeth brightening up for a moment'),(4875,1,787,273,'upon my word said mrs'),(4876,1,47979,5810,'gardiner i begin to be of your uncles opinion'),(4877,1,67,67,'it is really too great a violation of decency honour and interest for him to be guilty of it'),(4878,1,279,588,'i cannot think so very ill of wickham'),(4879,1,850,67,'can you yourself lizzie so wholly give him up as to believe him capable of it'),(4880,1,55,198,'not perhaps of neglecting his own interest'),(4881,1,295,28005,'but of every other neglect i can believe him capable'),(4882,1,257,1051,'if indeed it should be so'),(4883,1,295,67,'but i dare not hope it'),(4884,1,4949,52,'why should they not go on to scotland if that had been the case'),(4885,1,50,636,'in the first place replied mr'),(4886,1,47979,89079,'gardiner there is no absolute proof that they are not gone to scotland'),(4887,1,5037,92127,'oh but their removing from the chaise into a hackney coach is such a presumption'),(4888,1,1,53024,'and besides no traces of them were to be found on the barnet road'),(4889,1,43,9635,'well then--supposing them to be in london--they may be there though for the purpose of concealment for no more exceptionable purpose'),(4890,1,67,89079,'it is not likely that money should be very abundant on either side and it might strike them that they could be more economically