d to dispose of her time in the most idle and frivolous manner and to adopt any opinions that came in her way'),(4908,1,5543,1999,'since the ----shire were first quartered in meryton nothing but love flirtation and officers have been in her head'),(4909,1,729,67,'she has been doing everything in her power by thinking and talking on the subject to give greater--what shall i call it'),(4910,1,92567,566,'--susceptibility to her feelings which are naturally lively enough'),(4911,1,1,1506,'and we all know that wickham has every charm of person and address that can captivate a woman'),(4912,1,295,656,'but you see that jane said her aunt does not think so ill of wickham as to believe him capable of the attempt'),(4913,1,53,5886,'of whom does jane ever think ill'),(4914,1,1,112,'and who is there whatever might be their former conduct that she would believe capable of such an attempt till it were proved against them'),(4915,1,295,75,'but jane knows as well as i do what wickham really is'),(4916,1,1649,92692,'we both know that he has been profligate in every sense of the word that he has neither integrity nor honour that he is as false and deceitful as he is insinuating'),(4917,1,1,37,'and do you really know all this'),(4918,1,4916,273,'cried mrs'),(4919,1,47979,2362,'gardiner whose curiosity as to the mode of her intelligence was all alive'),(4920,1,279,46421,'i do indeed replied elizabeth colouring'),(4921,1,279,636,'i told you the other day of his infamous behaviour to mr'),(4922,1,2154,247,'darcy and you yourself when last at longbourn heard in what manner he spoke of the man who had behaved with such forbearance and liberality towards him'),(4923,1,1,92787,'and there are other circumstances which i am not at liberty--which it is not worth while to relate but his lies about the whole pemberley family are endless'),(4924,1,344,1873,'from what he said of miss darcy i was thoroughly prepared to see a proud reserved disagreeable girl'),(4925,1,119,852,'yet he knew to the contrary himself'),(4926,1,258,382,'he must know that she was as amiable and unpretending as we have found her'),(4927,1,295,37,'but does lydia know nothing of this'),(4928,1,850,5591,'can she be ignorant of what you and jane seem so well to understand'),(4929,1,5037,9487,'oh yes'),(4930,1,92851,172,'--that that is the worst of all'),(4931,1,505,636,'till i was in kent and saw so much both of mr'),(4932,1,2154,1718,'darcy and his relation colonel fitzwilliam i was ignorant of the truth myself'),(4933,1,1,369,'and when i returned home the ----shire was to leave meryton in a week or fortnights time'),(4934,1,42,78193,'as that was the case neither jane to whom i related the whole nor i thought it necessary to make our knowledge public for of what use could it apparently be to anyone that the good opinion which all the neighbourhood had of him should then be overthrown'),(4935,1,1,273,'and even when it was settled that lydia should go with mrs'),(4936,1,11302,424,'forster the necessity of opening her eyes to his character never occurred to me'),(4937,1,163,1999,'that she could be in any danger from the deception never entered my head'),(4938,1,163,2791,'that such a consequence as this should ensue you may easily believe was far enough from my thoughts'),(4939,1,483,409,'when they all removed to brighton therefore you had no reason i suppose to believe them fond of each other'),(4940,1,55,4096,'not the slightest'),(4941,1,279,4025,'i can remember no symptom of affection on either side and had anything of the kind been perceptible you must be aware that ours is not a family on which it could be thrown away'),(4942,1,483,798,'when first he entered the corps she was ready enough to admire him but so we all were'),(4943,1,138,33842,'every girl in or near meryton was out of her senses about him for the first two months but he never distinguished her by any particular attention and consequently after a moderate period of extravagant and wild admiration her fancy for him gave way a