991,'i must confess that he did not speak so well of wickham as he formerly did'),(5040,1,258,742,'he believed him to be imprudent and extravagant and since this sad affair has taken place it is said that he left meryton greatly in debt but i hope this may be false'),(5041,1,5037,4729,'oh jane had we been less secret had we told what we knew of him this could not have happened'),(5042,1,290,8194,'perhaps it would have been better replied her sister'),(5043,1,295,95105,'but to expose the former faults of any person without knowing what their present feelings were seemed unjustifiable'),(5044,1,1649,63204,'we acted with the best intentions'),(5045,1,575,1340,'could colonel forster repeat the particulars of lydias note to his wife'),(5046,1,258,4334,'he brought it with him for us to see'),(5047,1,4,2133,'jane then took it from her pocket-book and gave it to elizabeth'),(5048,1,2457,89214,'these were the contents-- nind my dear harriet you will laugh when you know where i am gone and i cannot help laughing myself at your surprise to-morrow morning as soon as i am missed'),(5049,1,279,9115,'i am going to gretna green and if you cannot guess with who i shall think you a simpleton for there is but one man in the world i love and he is an angel'),(5050,1,279,891,'i should never be happy without him so think it no harm to be off'),(5051,1,2074,588,'you need not send them word at longbourn of my going if you do not like it for it will make the surprise the greater when i write to them and sign my name lydia wickham'),(5052,1,994,104,'what a good joke it will be'),(5053,1,279,37476,'i can hardly write for laughing'),(5054,1,12426,95294,'pray make my excuses to pratt for not keeping my engagement and dancing with him to-night'),(5055,1,4923,4212,'tell him i hope he will excuse me when he knows all and tell him i will dance with him at the next ball we meet with great pleasure'),(5056,1,279,1274,'i shall send for my clothes when i get to longbourn but i wish you would tell sally