d in the distance'),(8045,2,74,76777,'there was an offensive tone in it perhaps and yet in the main it struck me as mere riotous outcry the noisy reception of one who amused and interested them rather than of one they disliked or despised'),(8046,2,128876,140168,'challenger smiled with weary and tolerant contempt as a kindly man would meet the yapping of a litter of puppies'),(8047,2,258,247,'he sat slowly down blew out his chest passed his hand caressingly down his beard and looked with drooping eyelids and supercilious eyes at the crowded hall before him'),(8048,2,51,636,'the uproar of his advent had not yet died away when professor ronald murray the chairman and mr'),(8049,2,138597,5847,'waldron the lecturer threaded their way to the front and the proceedings began'),(8050,2,128875,140251,'professor murray will i am sure excuse me if i say that he has the common fault of most englishmen of being inaudible'),(8051,2,4949,534,'why on earth people who have something to say which is worth hearing should not take the slight trouble to learn how to make it heard is one of the strange mysteries of modern life'),(8052,2,81,12842,'their methods are as reasonable as to try to pour some precious stuff from the spring to the reservoir through a non-conducting pipe which could by the least effort be opened'),(8053,2,128875,106,'professor murray made several profound remarks to his white tie and to the water-carafe upon the table with a humorous twinkling aside to the silver candlestick upon his right'),(8054,2,1502,636,'then he sat down and mr'),(8055,2,138597,140364,'waldron the famous popular lecturer rose amid a general murmur of applause'),(8056,2,258,247,'he was a stern gaunt man with a harsh voice and an aggressive manner but he had the merit of knowing how to assimilate the ideas of other men and to pass them on in a way which was intelligible and even interesting to the lay public with a happy knack of being funny about the most unlikely objects so that the precession