red back at me with hard angry eyes but i refused to give way'),(8220,2,279,636,'i will go mr'),(8221,2,130618,3373,'chairman i kept repeating over and over again'),(8222,2,4916,37116,'cried the audience'),(8223,2,413,129000,'my name is edward dunn malone'),(8224,2,279,128799,'i am the reporter of the daily gazette'),(8225,2,279,54978,'i claim to be an absolutely unprejudiced witness'),(8226,2,994,5275,'what is your name sir'),(8227,2,51,85616,'the chairman asked of my tall rival'),(8228,2,279,143090,'i am lord john roxton'),(8229,2,279,143109,'i have already been up the amazon i know all the ground and have special qualifications for this investigation'),(8230,2,22823,134841,'lord john roxton\'s reputation as a sportsman and a traveler is of course world-famous said the chairman at the same time it would certainly be as well to have a member of the press upon such an expedition'),(8231,2,1502,143182,'then i move said professor challenger that both these gentlemen be elected as representatives of this meeting to accompany professor summerlee upon his journey to investigate and to report upon the truth of my statements'),(8232,2,1,67,'and so amid shouting and cheering our fate was decided and i found myself borne away in the human current which swirled towards the door with my mind half stunned by the vast new project which had risen so suddenly before it'),(8233,2,42,112,'as i emerged from the hall i was conscious for a moment of a rush of laughing students--down the pavement and of an arm wielding a heavy umbrella which rose and fell in the midst of them'),(8234,2,1502,24280,'then amid a mixture of groans and cheers professor challenger\'s electric brougham slid from the curb and i found myself walking under the silvery lights of regent street full of thoughts of gladys and of wonder as to my future'),(8235,2,5725,134439,'suddenly there was a touch at my elbow'),(8236,2,279,20912,'i turned and found myself looking into the humorous masterful eyes of the tall thin man who had volunteered to be my companion on this strange quest'),(8237,2,129000,258,'malone i understand said he'),(8238,2,1649,143346,'we are to be companions--what'),(8239,2,413,143356,'my rooms are just over the road in the albany'),(8240,2,290,2074,'perhaps you would have the kindness to spare me half an hour for there are one or two things that i badly want to say to you'),(8241,2,4328,143412,'chapter vi i was the flail of the lord lord john roxton and i turned down vigo street together and through the dingy portals of the famous aristocratic rookery'),(8242,2,308,143433,'at the end of a long drab passage my new acquaintance pushed open a door and turned on an electric switch'),(8243,2,23,143452,'a number of lamps shining through tinted shades bathed the whole great room before us in a ruddy radiance'),(8244,2,7265,143477,'standing in the doorway and glancing round me i had a general impression of extraordinary comfort and elegance combined with an atmosphere of masculine virility'),(8245,2,8458,143496,'everywhere there were mingled the luxury of the wealthy man of taste and the careless untidiness of the bachelor'),(8246,2,2582,143511,'rich furs and strange iridescent mats from some oriental bazaar were scattered upon the floor'),(8247,2,81275,143533,'pictures and prints which even my unpractised eyes could recognize as being of great price and rarity hung thick upon the walls'),(8248,2,135513,143553,'sketches of boxers of ballet-girls and of racehorses alternated with a sensuous fragonard a martial girardet and a dreamy turner'),(8249,2,295,2045,'but amid these varied ornaments there were scattered the trophies which brought back strongly to my recollection the fact that lord john roxton was one of the great all-round sportsmen and athletes of his day'),(8250,2,23,2163,'a dark-blue oar crossed with a cherry-pink one above his mantel-piece spoke of the old oxonian and leander man while the foils and boxing-gloves above and below them were the tools of a man who had won supremacy with each'),(8251,2,591,172,'like a dado round the room was the jutting line of splendid heavy game-heads the best of their sort from every quarter of the world with the rare white rhinoceros of the lado enclave drooping its supercilious lip above them all'),(8252,2,50,143700,'in the center of the rich red carpet was a black and gold louis quinze table a lovely antique now sacrilegiously desecrated with marks of glasses and the scars of cigar-stumps'),(8253,2,407,143695,'on it stood a silver tray of smokables and a burnished spirit-stand from which and an adjacent siphon my silent host proceeded to charge two high glasses'),(8254,2,6999,143746,'having indicated an arm-chair to me and placed my refreshment near it he handed me a long smooth havana'),(8255,2,1502,135185,'then seating himself opposite to me he looked at me long and fixedly with his strange twinkling reckless eyes--eyes of a cold light blue the color of a glacier lake'),(8256,2,2751,73001,'through the thin haze of my cigar-smoke i noted the details of a face which was already familiar to me from many photographs--the strongly-curved nose the hollow worn cheeks the dark ruddy hair thin at the top the crisp virile moustaches the small aggressive tuft upon his projecting chin'),(8257,2,707,6463,'something there was of napoleon iii'),(8258,2,707,13462,'something of don quixote and yet again something which was the essence of the english country gentleman the keen alert open-air lover of dogs and of horses'),(8259,2,892,54262,'his skin was of a rich flower-pot red from sun and wind'),(8260,2,892,90669,'his eyebrows were tufted and overhanging which gave those naturally cold eyes an almost ferocious aspect an impression which was increased by his strong and furrowed brow'),(8261,2,50,143922,'in figure he was spare but very strongly built--indeed he had often proved that there were few men in england capable of such sustained exertions'),(8262,2,892,125325,'his height was a little over six feet but he seemed shorter on account of a peculiar rounding of the shoulders'),(8263,2,392,8550,'such was the famous lord john roxton as he sat opposite to me biting hard upon his cigar and watching me steadily in a long and embarrassing silence'),(8264,2,43,49341,'well said he at last we\'ve gone and done it young fellah my lad'),(8265,2,37,143997,'this curious phrase he pronounced as if it were all one word--young-fellah-me-lad'),(8266,2,9487,424,'yes we\'ve taken a jump you an\' me'),(8267,2,279,144022,'i suppose now when you went into that room there was no such notion in your head--what'),(8268,2,655,67,'no thought of it'),(8269,2,51,454,'the same here'),(8270,2,655,67,'no thought of it'),(8271,2,1,144044,'and here we are up to our necks in the tureen'),(8272,2,4949,172,'why i\'ve only been back three weeks from uganda and taken a place in scotland and signed the lease and all'),(8273,2,1963,144068,'pretty goin\'s on--what'),(8274,2,1338,2074,'how does it hit you'),(8275,2,43,661,'well it is all in the main line of my business'),(8276,2,279,128799,'i am a journalist on the gazette'),(8277,2,53,407,'of course--you said so when you took it on'),(8278,2,3,424,'by the way i\'ve got a small job for you if you\'ll help me'),(8279,2,34,4212,'with pleasure'),(8280,2,127385,2074,'don\'t mind takin\' a risk do you'),(8281,2,994,38172,'what is the risk'),(8282,2,43,38172,'well it\'s ballinger--he\'s the risk'),(8283,2,127522,247,'you\'ve heard of him'),(8284,2,4949,7714,'why young fellah where have you lived'),(8285,2,5275,1878,'sir john ballinger is the best gentleman jock in the north country'),(8286,2,279,22259,'i could hold him on the flat at my best but over jumps he\'s my master'),(8287,2,43,67,'well it\'s an open secret that when he\'s out of trainin\' he drinks hard--strikin\' an average he calls it'),(8288,2,258,5543,'he got delirium on toosday and has been ragin\' like a devil ever since'),(8289,2,892,37,'his room is above this'),(8290,2,51,144265,'the doctors