 do nothing to make it worse'),(8401,2,23,146136,'a third attempt later in the day provoked a terrific crash and a subsequent message from the central exchange that professor challenger\'s receiver had been shattered'),(8402,2,171,22199,'after that we abandoned all attempt at communication'),(8403,2,1,16111,'and now my patient readers i can address you directly no longer'),(8404,2,344,10836,'from now onwards if indeed any continuation of this narrative should ever reach you it can only be through the paper which i represent'),(8405,2,50,482,'in the hands of the editor i leave this account of the events which have led up to one of the most remarkable expeditions of all time so that if i never return to england there shall be some record as to how the affair came about'),(8406,2,279,636,'i am writing these last lines in the saloon of the booth liner francisca and they will go back by the pilot to the keeping of mr'),(8407,2,2792,424,'let me draw one last picture before i close the notebook--a picture which is the last memory of the old country which i bear away with me'),(8408,2,67,3363,'it is a wet foggy morning in the late spring a thin cold rain is falling'),(8409,2,1794,6791,'three shining mackintoshed figures are walking down the quay making for the gang-plank of the great liner from which the blue-peter is flying'),(8410,2,50,146336,'in front of them a porter pushes a trolley piled high with trunks wraps and gun-cases'),(8411,2,128875,852,'professor summerlee a long melancholy figure walks with dragging steps and drooping head as one who is already profoundly sorry for himself'),(8412,2,22823,146376,'lord john roxton steps briskly and his thin eager face beams forth between his hunting-cap and his muffler'),(8413,2,42,52397,'as for myself i am glad to have got the bustling days of preparation and the pangs of leave-taking behind me and i have no doubt that i show it in my bearing'),(8414,2,5725,2793,'suddenly just as we reach the vessel there is a shout behind us'),(8415,2,67,891,'it is professor challenger who had promised to see us off'),(8416,2,258,7012,'he runs after us a puffing red-faced irascible figure'),(8417,2,655,146455,'no thank you says he i should much prefer not to go aboard'),(8418,2,279,60,'i have only a few words to say to you and they can very well be said where we are'),(8419,2,279,1965,'i beg you not to imagine that i am in any way indebted to you for making this journey'),(8420,2,279,63678,'i would have you to understand that it is a matter of perfect indifference to me and i refuse to entertain the most remote sense of personal obligation'),(8421,2,4788,7105,'truth is truth and nothing which you can report can affect it in any way though it may excite the emotions and allay the curiosity of a number of very ineffectual people'),(8422,2,413,40950,'my directions for your instruction and guidance are in this sealed envelope'),(8423,2,2074,2899,'you will open it when you reach a town upon the amazon which is called manaos but not until the date and hour which is marked upon the outside'),(8424,2,254,11120,'have i made myself clear'),(8425,2,279,130215,'i leave the strict observance of my conditions entirely to your honor'),(8426,2,655,636,'no mr'),(8427,2,129000,6135,'malone i will place no restriction upon your correspondence since the ventilation of the facts is the object of your journey but i demand that you shall give no particulars as to your exact destination and that nothing be actually published until your return'),(8428,2,131110,5275,'good-bye sir'),(8429,2,2074,33041,'you have done something to mitigate my feelings for the loathsome profession to which you unhappily belong'),(8430,2,131110,1015,'good-bye lord john'),(8431,2,11882,2074,'science is as i understand a sealed book to you but you may congratulate yourself upon the hunting-field which awaits you'),(8432,2,2074,137777,'you will no doubt have the opportunity of describing in the field how you brought down the rocketing dimorphodon'),(8433,2,1,142538,'and good-bye to you also professor summerlee'),(8434,2,257,1278,'if you are still capable of self-improvement of which i am frankly unconvinced you will surely return to london a wiser man'),(8435,2,1051,35120,'so he turned upon his heel and a minute later from the deck i could see his short squat figure bobbing about in the distance as he made his way back to his train'),(8436,2,43,2530,'well we are well down channel now'),(8437,2,129216,131573,'there\'s the last bell for letters and it\'s good-bye to the pilot'),(8438,2,130769,407,'we\'ll be down hull-down on the old trail from now on'),(8439,2,46460,1886,'god bless all we leave behind us and send us safely back'),(8440,2,4328,135321,'chapter vii to-morrow we disappear into the unknown i will not bore those whom this narrative may reach by an account of our luxurious voyage upon the booth liner nor will i tell of our week\'s stay at para save that i should wish to acknowledge the great kindness of the pereira da pinta company in helping us to get together our equipment'),(8441,2,279,146920,'i will also allude very briefly to our river journey up a wide slow-moving clay-tinted stream in a steamer which was little smaller than that which had carried us across the atlantic'),(8442,2,146921,146582,'eventually we found ourselves through the narrows of obidos and reached the town of manaos'),(8443,2,454,636,'here we were rescued from the limited attractions of the local inn by mr'),(8444,2,146950,1028,'shortman the representative of the british and brazilian trading company'),(8445,2,50,128876,'in his hospitable fazenda we spent our time until the day when we were empowered to open the letter of instructions given to us by professor challenger'),(8446,2,509,129699,'before i reach the surprising events of that date i would desire to give a clearer sketch of my comrades in this enterprise and of the associates whom we had already gathered together in south america'),(8447,2,279,636,'i speak freely and i leave the use of my material to your own discretion mr'),(8448,2,128880,378,'mcardle since it is through your hands that this report must pass before it reaches the world'),(8449,2,51,112,'the scientific attainments of professor summerlee are too well known for me to trouble to recapitulate them'),(8450,2,258,6660,'he is better equipped for a rough expedition of this sort than one would imagine at first sight'),(8451,2,892,147116,'his tall gaunt stringy figure is insensible to fatigue and his dry half-sarcastic and often wholly unsympathetic manner is uninfluenced by any change in his surroundings'),(8452,2,384,53630,'though in his sixty-sixth year i have never heard him express any dissatisfaction at the occasional hardships which we have had to encounter'),(8453,2,279,764,'i had regarded his presence as an encumbrance to the expedition but as a matter of fact i am now well convinced that his power of endurance is as great as my own'),(8454,2,50,139448,'in temper he is naturally acid and sceptical'),(8455,2,344,4973,'from the beginning he has never concealed his belief that professor challenger is an absolute fraud that we are all embarked upon an absurd wild-goose chase and that we are likely to reap nothing but disappointment and danger in south america and corresponding ridicule in england'),(8456,2,392,146582,'such are the views which with much passionate distortion of his thin features and wagging of his thin goat-like beard he poured into our ears all the way from southampton to manaos'),(8457,2,5543,11882,'since landing from the boat he has obtained some consolation from the beauty and variety of the insect and bird life around him for he is absolutely whole-hearted in his devotion to science'),(8458,2,258,8716,'he spends his days flitting through the woods with his shot-gun and his butterfly-net and his evenings in mounting the many specimens he has acquired'),(8459,2,5663,2725,'among his minor peculiarities are that he is careless as to his attire unclean in his person exceedingly absent-minded in his habits and addicted to smoking a short briar pipe which is seldom out of his mouth'),(8460,2,258,247,'he has been upon several scientific expeditions in his youth he was with robertson in papua and the life of the camp and the canoe is nothing fresh to him'),(8461,2,22823,409,'lord john roxton has some points in common with professor summerlee and others in which they are the very antithesis to each other'),(8462,2,258,147420,'he is twenty years younger but has something of the same spare scraggy physique'),(8463,2,42,6833,'as to his appearance i have as i recollect described it in that portion of my narrative which i have left behind me in london'),(8464,2,258,2045,'he is exceedingly neat and prim in his ways dresses always with great care in white drill suits and high brown mosquito-boots and shaves at least once a day'),(8465,2,591,652,'like most men of action he is laconic in speech and sinks readily into his own thoughts but he is always quick to answer a question or join in a conversation talking in a queer jerky half-humorous fashion'),(8466,2,892,142538,'his knowledge of the world and very especially of south america is surprising and he has a whole-hearted belief in the possibilities of our journey which is not to be dashed by the sneers of professor summerlee'),(8467,2,258,138704,'he has a gentle voice and a quiet manner but behind his twinkling blue eyes there lurks a capacity for furious wrath and implacable resolution the more dangerous because they are held in leash'),(8468,2,258,147624,'he spoke little of his own exploits in brazil and peru but it was a revelation to me to find the excitement which was caused by his presence among the riverine natives who looked upon him as their champion and protector'),(8469,2,51,566,'the exploits of the red chief as they called him had become legends among them but the real facts as far as i could learn them were amazing enough'),(8470,2,2457,147679,'these were that lord john had found himself some years before in that no-man\'s-land which is formed by the half-defined frontiers between peru brazil and columbia'),(8471,2,50,145384,'in this great district the wild rubber tree flourishes and has become as in the congo a curse to the natives which can only be compared to their forced labor under the spaniards upon the old silver mines of darien'),(8472,2,23,144789,'a handful of villainous half-breeds dominated the country armed such indians as would support them and turned the rest into slaves terrorizing them with the most inhuman tortures in order to force them to gather the india-rubber which was then floated down the river to para'),(8473,2,22823,3013,'lord john roxton expostulated on behalf of the wretched victims and received nothing but threats and insults for his pains'),(8474,2,258,25727,'he then formally declared war against pedro lopez the leader of the slave-drivers enrolled a band of runaway slaves in his service armed them and conducted a campaign which ended by his killing with his own hands the notorious half-breed and breaking down the system which he represented'),(8475,2,655,112,'no wonder that the ginger-headed man with the silky voice and the free and easy manners was now looked upon with deep interest upon the banks of the great south american river though the feelings he inspired were naturally mixed since the gratitude of the natives was equaled by the resentment of those who desired to exploit them'),(8476,2,139,147592,'one useful result of his former experiences was that he could talk fluently in the lingoa geral which is the peculiar talk one-third portuguese and two-thirds indian which is current all over brazil'),(8477,2,279,147936,'i have said before that lord john roxton was a south americomaniac'),(8478,2,258,7748,'he could not speak of that great country without ardor and this ardor was infectious for ignorant as i was he fixed my attention and stimulated my curiosity'),(8479,2,1338,8548,'how i wish i could reproduce the glamour of his discourses the peculiar mixture of accurate knowledge and of racy imagination which gave them their fascination until even the professor\'s cynical and sceptical smile would gradually vanish from his thin face as he listened'),(8480,2,258,80110,'he would tell the history of the mighty river so rapidly explored for some of the first conquerors of peru actually crossed the entire continent upon its waters and yet so unknown in regard to all that lay behind its ever-changing banks'),(8481,2,994,74,'what is there'),(8482,2,258,4971,'he would cry pointing to the north'),(8483,2,79998,148066,'wood and marsh and unpenetrated jungle'),(8484,2,59,148072,'who knows what it may shelter'),(8485,2,1,102297,'and there to the south'),(8486,2,23,260,'a wilderness of swampy forest where no white man has ever been'),(8487,2,51,1473,'the unknown is up against us on every side'),(8488,2,2899,515,'outside the narrow lines of the rivers what does anyone know'),(8489,2,59,1878,'who will say what is possible in such a country'),(8490,2,4949,106,'why should old man challenger not be right'),(8491,2,308,140309,'at which direct defiance the stubborn sneer would reappear upon professor summerlee\'s face and he would sit shaking his sardonic head in unsympathetic silence behind the cloud of his briar-root pipe'),(8492,2,1051,148185,'so much for the moment for my two white companions whose characters and limitations will be further exposed as surely as my own as this narrative proceeds'),(8493,2,295,3479,'but already we have enrolled certain retainers who may play no small part in what is to come'),(8494,2,51,1385,'the first is a gigantic negro named zambo who is a black hercules as willing as any horse and about as intelligent'),(8495,2,247,1436,'him we enlisted at para on the recommendation of the steamship company on whose vessels he had learned to speak a halting english'),(8496,2,67,148273,'it was at para also that we engaged gomez and manuel two half-breeds from up the river just come down with a cargo of redwood'),(8497,2,1056,148286,'they were swarthy fellows bearded and fierce as active and wiry as panthers'),(8498,2,1191,112,'both of them had spent their lives in those upper waters of the amazon which we were about to explore and it was this recommendation which had caused lord john to engage them'),(8499,2,139,1436,'one of them gomez had the further advantage that he could speak excellent english'),(8500,2,2457,19004,'these men were willing to act as our personal servants to cook to row or to make themselves useful in any way at a payment of fifteen dollars a month'),(8501,2,16049,136964,'besides these we had engaged three mojo indians from bolivia who are the most skilful at fishing and boat work of all the river tribes'),(8502,2,51,148407,'the chief of these we called mojo after his tribe and the others are known as jose and fernando'),(8503,2,1794,20912,'three white men then two half-breeds one negro and three indians made up the personnel of the little expedition which lay waiting for its instructions at manaos before starting upon its singular quest'),(8504,2,308,8903,'at last after a weary week the day had come and the hour'),(8505,2,279,9388,'i ask you to picture the shaded sitting-room of the fazenda st'),(8506,2,148466,146582,'ignatio two miles inland from the town of manaos'),(8507,2,2899,2601,'outside lay the yellow brassy glare of the sunshine with the shadows of the palm trees as black and definite as the trees themselves'),(8508,2,51,148530,'the air was calm full of the eternal hum of insects a tropical chorus of many octaves from the deep drone of the bee to the high keen pipe of the mosquito'),(8509,2,1653,11189,'beyond the veranda was a small cleared garden bounded with cactus hedges and adorned with clumps of flowering shrubs round which the great blue butterflies and the tiny humming-birds fluttered and darted in crescents of sparkling light'),(8510,2,6991,40950,'within we were seated round the cane table on which lay a sealed envelope'),(8511,2,148582,283,'inscribed upon it in the jagged handwriting of professor challenger were the words-- instructions to lord john roxton and party'),(8512,2,40,21677,'to be opened at manaos upon july th at o\'clock precisely'),(8513,2,22823,247,'lord john had placed his watch upon the table beside him'),(8514,2,1649,258,'we have seven more minutes said he'),(8515,2,51,148636,'the old dear is very precise'),(8516,2,128875,410,'professor summerlee gave an acid smile as he picked up the envelope in his gaunt hand'),(8517,2,994,6645,'what can it possibly matter whether we open it now or in seven minutes'),(8518,2,2257,258,'said he'),(8519,2,67,132158,'it is all part and parcel of the same system of quackery and nonsense for which i regret to say that the writer is notorious'),(8520,2,5037,1015,'oh come we must play the game accordin\' to rules said lord john'),(8521,2,127528,1317,'it\'s old man challenger\'s show and we are here by his good will so it would be rotten bad form if we didn\'t follow his instructions to the letter'),(8522,2,23,75,'a pretty business it is'),(8523,2,4916,23063,'cried the professor bitterly'),(8524,2,67,5984,'it struck me as preposterous in london but i\'m bound to say that it seems even more so upon closer acquaintance'),(8525,2,279,144789,'i don\'t know what is inside this envelope but unless it is something pretty definite i shall be much tempted to take the next down-river boat and catch the bolivia at para'),(8526,2,171,148818,'after all i have some more responsible work in the world than to run about disproving the assertions of a lunatic'),(8527,2,2530,369,'now roxton surely it is time'),(8528,2,369,1015,'time it is said lord john'),(8529,2,2074,148835,'you can blow the whistle'),(8530,2,258,148846,'he took up the envelope and cut it with his penknife'),(8531,2,344,40958,'from it he drew a folded sheet of paper'),(8532,2,37,8843,'this he carefully opened out and flattened on the table'),(8533,2,67,40953,'it was a blank sheet'),(8534,2,258,554,'he turned it over'),(8535,2,3373,129205,'again it was blank'),(8536,2,1649,142538,'we looked at each other in a bewildered silence which was broken by a discordant burst of derisive laughter from professor summerlee'),(8537,2,67,4916,'it is an open admission he cried'),(8538,2,994,299,'what more do you want'),(8539,2,51,148918,'the fellow is a self-confessed humbug'),(8540,2,1649,75,'we have only to return home and report him as the brazen imposter that he is'),(8541,2,148935,148936,'invisible ink'),(8542,2,279,22375,'i suggested'),(8543,2,279,693,'i don\'t think'),(8544,2,2257,11189,'said lord roxton holding the paper to the light'),(8545,2,655,7312,'no young fellah my lad there is no use deceiving yourself'),(8546,2,127845,40958,'i\'ll go bail for it that nothing has ever been written upon this paper'),(8547,2,465,50,'may i come in'),(8548,2,134043,148533,'boomed a voice from the veranda'),(8549,2,51,148998,'the shadow of a squat figure had stolen across the patch of sunlight'),(8550,2,163,4517,'that voice'),(8551,2,163,149005,'that monstrous breadth of shoulder'),(8552,2,1649,2793,'we sprang to our feet with a gasp of astonishment as challenger in a round boyish straw-hat with a colored ribbon--challenger with his hands in his jacket-pockets and his canvas shoes daintily pointing as he walked--appeared in the open space before us'),(8553,2,258,917,'he threw back his head and there he stood in the golden glow with all his old assyrian luxuriance of beard all his native insolence of drooping eyelids and intolerant eyes'),(8554,2,279,2055,'i fear said he taking out his watch that i am a few minutes too late'),(8555,2,483,8903,'when i gave you this envelope i must confess that i had never intended that you should open it for it had been my fixed intention to be with you before the hour'),(8556,2,51,135574,'the unfortunate delay can be apportioned between a blundering pilot and an intrusive sandbank'),(8557,2,279,149154,'i fear that it has given my colleague professor summerlee occasion to blaspheme'),(8558,2,279,1690,'i am bound to say sir said lord john with some sternness of voice that your turning up is a considerable relief to us for our mission seemed to have come to a premature end'),(8559,2,1232,3728,'even now i can\'t for the life of me understand why you should have worked it in so extraordinary a manner'),(8560,2,6884,19414,'instead of answering professor challenger entered shook hands with myself and lord john bowed with ponderous insolence to professor summerlee and sank back into a basket-chair which creaked and swayed beneath his weight'),(8561,2,75,1965,'is all ready for your journey'),(8562,2,258,7867,'he asked'),(8563,2,1649,5906,'we can start to-morrow'),(8564,2,1502,5767,'then so you shall'),(8565,2,2074,25535,'you need no chart of directions now since you will have the inestimable advantage of my own guidance'),(8566,2,344,143109,'from the first i had determined that i would myself preside over your investigation'),(8567,2,51,7565,'the most elaborate charts would as you will readily admit be a poor substitute for my own intelligence and advice'),(8568,2,42,2074,'as to the small ruse which i played upon you in the matter of the envelope it is clear that had i told you all my intentions i should have been forced to resist unwelcome pressure to travel out with you'),(8569,2,55,5275,'not from me sir'),(8570,2,25251,15179,'exclaimed professor summerlee heartily'),(8571,2,1051,146920,'so long as there was another ship upon the atlantic'),(8572,2,128876,410,'challenger waved him away with his great hairy hand'),(8573,2,1373,45498,'your common sense will i am sure sustain my objection and realize that it was better that i should direct my own movements and appear only at the exact moment when my presence was needed'),(8574,2,163,6733,'that moment has now arrived'),(8575,2,2074,22498,'you are in safe hands'),(8576,2,2074,146649,'you will not now fail to reach your destination'),(8577,2,344,6169,'from henceforth i take command of this expedition and i must ask you to complete your preparations to-night so that we may be able to make an early start in the morning'),(8578,2,413,764,'my time is of value and the same thing may be said no doubt in a lesser degree of your own'),(8579,2,279,4334,'i propose therefore that we push on as rapidly as possible until i have demonstrated what you have come to see'),(8580,2,22823,4669,'lord john roxton has chartered a large steam launch the esmeralda which was to carry us up the river'),(8581,2,1051,149560,'so far as climate goes it was immaterial what time we chose for our expedition as the temperature ranges from seventy-five to ninety degrees both summer and winter with no appreciable difference in heat'),(8582,2,50,43671,'in moisture however it is otherwise from december to may is the period of the rains and during this time the river slowly rises until it attains a height of nearly forty feet above its low-water mark'),(8583,2,67,149635,'it floods the banks extends in great lagoons over a monstrous waste of country and forms a huge district called locally the gapo which is for the most part too marshy for foot-travel and too shallow for boating'),(8584,2,482,23509,'about june the waters begin to fall and are at their lowest at october or november'),(8585,2,3155,64567,'thus our expedition was at the time of the dry season when the great river and its tributaries were more or less in a normal condition'),(8586,2,51,9325,'the current of the river is a slight one the drop being not greater than eight inches in a mile'),(8587,2,655,143202,'no stream could be more convenient for navigation since the prevailing wind is south-east and sailing boats may make a continuous progress to the peruvian frontier dropping down again with the current'),(8588,2,50,135185,'in our own case the excellent engines of the esmeralda could disregard the sluggish flow of the stream and we made as rapid progress as if we were navigating a stagnant lake'),(8589,2,284,149797,'for three days we steamed north-westwards up a stream which even here a thousand miles from its mouth was still so enormous that from its center the two banks were mere shadows upon the distant skyline'),(8590,2,407,80095,'on the fourth day after leaving manaos we turned into a tributary which at its mouth was little smaller than the main stream'),(8591,2,67,146582,'it narrowed rapidly however and after two more days\' steaming we reached an indian village where the professor insisted that we should land and that the esmeralda should be sent back to manaos'),(8592,2,1649,2310,'we should soon come upon rapids he explained which would make its further use impossible'),(8593,2,258,104,'he added privately that we were now approaching the door of the unknown country and that the fewer whom we took into our confidence the better it would be'),(8594,2,40,26565,'to this end also he made each of us give our word of honor that we would publish or say nothing which would give any exact clue as to the whereabouts of our travels while the servants were all solemnly sworn to the same effect'),(8595,2,67,1878,'it is for this reason that i am compelled to be vague in my narrative and i would warn my readers that in any map or diagram which i may give the relation of places to each other may be correct but the points of the compass are carefully confused so that in no way can it be taken as an actual guide to the country'),(8596,2,128875,2793,'professor challenger\'s reasons for secrecy may be valid or not but we had no choice but to adopt them for he was prepared to abandon the whole expedition rather than modify the conditions upon which he would guide us'),(8597,2,67,149518,'it was august nd when we snapped our last link with the outer world by bidding farewell to the esmeralda'),(8598,2,5543,150106,'since then four days have passed during which we have engaged two large canoes from the indians made of so light a material skins over a bamboo framework that we should be able to carry them round any obstacle'),(8599,2,2457,149705,'these we have loaded with all our effects and have engaged two additional indians to help us in the navigation'),(8600,2,279,1965,'i understand that they are the very two--ataca and ipetu by name--who accompanied professor challenger upon his previous journey'),(8601,2,1056,6358,'they appeared to be terrified at the prospect of repeating it but the chief has patriarchal powers in these countries and if the bargain is good in his eyes the clansman has little choice in the matter'),(8602,2,1051,12850,'so to-morrow we disappear into the unknown'),(8603,2,37,76246,'this account i am transmitting down the river by canoe and it may be our last word to those who are interested in our fate'),(8604,2,279,636,'i have according to our arrangement addressed it to you my dear mr'),(8605,2,128880,67,'mcardle and i leave it to your discretion to delete alter or do what you like with it'),(8606,2,344,127940,'from the assurance of professor challenger\'s manner--and in spite of the continued scepticism of professor summerlee--i have no doubt that our leader will make good his statement and that we are really on the eve of some most remarkable experiences'),(8607,2,4328,150328,'chapter viii the outlying pickets of the new world our friends at home may well rejoice with us for we are at our goal and up to a point at least we have shown that the statement of professor challenger can be verified'),(8608,2,1649,137229,'we have not it is true ascended the plateau but it lies before us and even professor summerlee is in a more chastened mood'),(8609,2,55,8550,'not that he will for an instant admit that his rival could be right but he is less persistent in his incessant objections and has sunk for the most part into an observant silence'),(8610,2,279,67,'i must hark back however and continue my narrative from where i dropped it'),(8611,2,1649,410,'we are sending home one of our local indians who is injured and i am committing this letter to his charge with considerable doubts in my mind as to whether it will ever come to hand'),(8612,2,483,149518,'when i wrote last we were about to leave the indian village where we had been deposited by the esmeralda'),(8613,2,279,146035,'i have to begin my report by bad news for the first serious personal trouble i pass over the incessant bickerings between the professors occurred this evening and might have had a tragic ending'),(8614,2,279,46,'i have spoken of our english-speaking half-breed gomez--a fine worker and a willing fellow but afflicted i fancy with the vice of curiosity which is common enough among such men'),(8615,2,407,21512,'on the last evening he seems to have hid himself near the hut in which we were discussing our plans and being observed by our huge negro zambo who is as faithful as a dog and has the hatred which all his race bear to the half-breeds he was dragged out and carried into our presence'),(8616,2,148257,247,'gomez whipped out his knife however and but for the huge strength of his captor which enabled him to disarm him with one hand he would certainly have stabbed him'),(8617,2,51,43,'the matter has ended in reprimands the opponents have been compelled to shake hands and there is every hope that all will be well'),(8618,2,42,16200,'as to the feuds of the two learned men they are continuous and bitter'),(8619,2,67,1594,'it must be admitted that challenger is provocative in the last degree but summerlee has an acid tongue which makes matters worse'),(8620,2,4196,150309,'last night challenger said that he never cared to walk on the thames embankment and look up the river as it was always sad to see one\'s own eventual goal'),(8621,2,258,152,'he is convinced of course that he is destined for westminster abbey'),(8622,2,142538,4332,'summerlee rejoined however with a sour smile by saying that he understood that millbank prison had been pulled down'),(8623,2,129903,12311,'challenger\'s conceit is too colossal to allow him to be really annoyed'),(8624,2,258,2236,'he only smiled in his beard and repeated really'),(8625,2,50,17601,'in the pitying tone one would use to a child'),(8626,2,330,3942,'indeed they are children both--the one wizened and cantankerous the other formidable and overbearing yet each with a brain which has put him in the front rank of his scientific age'),(8627,2,78515,2163,'brain character soul--only as one sees more of life does one understand how distinct is each'),(8628,2,51,134841,'the very next day we did actually make our start upon this remarkable expedition'),(8629,2,1649,147378,'we found that all our possessions fitted very easily into the two canoes and we divided our personnel six in each taking the obvious precaution in the interests of peace of putting one professor into each canoe'),(8630,2,150857,696,'personally i was with challenger who was in a beatific humor moving about as one in a silent ecstasy and beaming benevolence from every feature'),(8631,2,279,137884,'i have had some experience of him in other moods however and shall be the less surprised when the thunderstorms suddenly come up amidst the sunshine'),(8632,2,257,1583,'if it is impossible to be at your ease it is equally impossible to be dull in his company for one is always in a state of half-tremulous doubt as to what sudden turn his formidable temper may take'),(8633,2,284,55421,'for two days we made our way up a good-sized river some hundreds of yards broad and dark in color but transparent so that one could usually see the bottom'),(8634,2,51,12534,'the affluents of the amazon are half of them of this nature while the other half are whitish and opaque the difference depending upon the class of country through which they have flowed'),(8635,2,51,151021,'the dark indicate vegetable decay while the others point to clayey soil'),(8636,2,7629,112,'twice we came across rapids and in each case made a portage of half a mile or so to avoid them'),(8637,2,51,112,'the woods on either side were primeval which are more easily penetrated than woods of the second growth and we had no great difficulty in carrying our canoes through them'),(8638,2,1338,67,'how shall i ever forget the solemn mystery of it'),(8639,2,51,151166,'the height of the trees and the thickness of the boles exceeded anything which i in my town-bred life could have imagined shooting upwards in magnificent columns until at an enormous distance above our heads we could dimly discern the spot where they threw out their side-branches into gothic upward curves which coalesced to form one great matted roof of verdure through which only an occasional golden ray of sunshine shot downwards to trace a thin dazzling line of light amidst the majestic obscurity'),(8640,2,42,15065,'as we walked noiselessly amid the thick soft carpet of decaying vegetation the hush fell upon our souls which comes upon us in the twilight of the abbey and even professor challenger\'s full-chested notes sank into a whisper'),(8641,2,8177,534,'alone i should have been ignorant of the names of these giant growths but our men of science pointed out the cedars the great silk cotton trees and the redwood trees with all that profusion of various plants which has made this continent the chief supplier to the human race of those gifts of nature which depend upon the vegetable world while it is the most backward in those products which come from animal life'),(8642,2,151280,151324,'vivid orchids and wonderful colored lichens smoldered upon the swarthy tree-trunks and where a wandering shaft of light fell full upon the golden allamanda the scarlet star-clusters of the tacsonia or the rich deep blue of ipomaea the effect was as a dream of fairyland'),(8643,2,50,11189,'in these great wastes of forest life which abhors darkness struggles ever upwards to the light'),(8644,2,138,21146,'every plant even the smaller ones curls and writhes to the green surface twining itself round its stronger and taller brethren in the effort'),(8645,2,129627,151418,'climbing plants are monstrous and luxuriant but others which have never been known to climb elsewhere learn the art as an escape from that somber shadow so that the common nettle the jasmine and even the jacitara palm tree can be seen circling the stems of the cedars and striving to reach their crowns'),(8646,2,53,112,'of animal life there was no movement amid the majestic vaulted aisles which stretched from us as we walked but a constant movement far above our heads told of that multitudinous world of snake and monkey bird and sloth which lived in the sunshine and looked down in wonder at our tiny dark stumbling figures in the obscure depths immeasurably below them'),(8647,2,308,50,'at dawn and at sunset the howler monkeys screamed together and the parrakeets broke into shrill chatter but during the hot hours of the day only the full drone of insects like the beat of a distant surf filled the ear while nothing moved amid the solemn vistas of stupendous trunks fading away into the darkness which held us in'),(8648,2,855,148486,'once some bandy-legged lurching creature an ant-eater or a bear scuttled clumsily amid the shadows'),(8649,2,67,145548,'it was the only sign of earth life which i saw in this great amazonian forest'),(8650,2,1,151590,'and yet there were indications that even human life itself was not far from us in those mysterious recesses'),(8651,2,407,6169,'on the third day out we were aware of a singular deep throbbing in the air rhythmic and solemn coming and going fitfully throughout the morning'),(8652,2,51,93284,'the two boats were paddling within a few yards of each other when first we heard it and our indians remained motionless as if they had been turned to bronze listening intently with expressions of terror upon their faces'),(8653,2,994,1502,'what is it then'),(8654,2,279,7867,'i asked'),(8655,2,151662,151662,'drums said lord john carelessly war drums'),(8656,2,279,509,'i have heard them before'),(8657,2,9487,147825,'yes sir war drums said gomez the half-breed'),(8658,2,14852,850,'wild indians bravos not mansos they watch us every mile of the way kill us if they can'),(8659,2,1338,2793,'how can they watch us'),(8660,2,279,120416,'i asked gazing into the dark motionless void'),(8661,2,51,125325,'the half-breed shrugged his broad shoulders'),(8662,2,51,515,'the indians know'),(8663,2,1056,608,'they have their own way'),(8664,2,1056,2793,'they watch us'),(8665,2,1056,409,'they talk the drum talk to each other'),(8666,2,103880,850,'kill us if they can'),(8667,2,3,35297,'by the afternoon of that day--my pocket diary shows me that it was tuesday august th--at least six or seven drums were throbbing from various points'),(8668,2,841,4971,'sometimes they beat quickly sometimes slowly sometimes in obvious question and answer one far to the east breaking out in a high staccato rattle and being followed after a pause by a deep roll from the north'),(8669,2,74,850,'there was something indescribably nerve-shaking and menacing in that constant mutter which seemed to shape itself into the very syllables of the half-breed endlessly repeated we will kill you if we can'),(8670,2,1649,850,'we will kill you if we can'),(8671,2,655,79733,'no one ever moved in the silent woods'),(8672,2,172,151880,'all the peace and soothing of quiet nature lay in that dark curtain of vegetation but away from behind there came ever the one message from our fellow-man'),(8673,2,1649,138056,'we will kill you if we can said the men in the east'),(8674,2,1649,4971,'we will kill you if we can said the men in the north'),(8675,2,172,47604,'all day the drums rumbled and whispered while their menace reflected itself in the faces of our colored companions'),(8676,2,1232,151932,'even the hardy swaggering half-breed seemed cowed'),(8677,2,279,2129,'i learned however that day once for all that both summerlee and challenger possessed that highest type of bravery the bravery of the scientific mind'),(8678,2,13464,151977,'theirs was the spirit which upheld darwin among the gauchos of the argentine or wallace among the head-hunters of malaya'),(8679,2,67,106780,'it is decreed by a merciful nature that the human brain cannot think of two things simultaneously so that if it be steeped in curiosity as to science it has no room for merely personal considerations'),(8680,2,172,9388,'all day amid that incessant and mysterious menace our two professors watched every bird upon the wing and every shrub upon the bank with many a sharp wordy contention when the snarl of summerlee came quick upon the deep growl of challenger but with no more sense of danger and no more reference to drum-beating indians than if they were seated together in the smoking-room of the royal society\'s club in st'),(8681,2,152086,26198,'james\'s street'),(8682,2,855,112,'once only did they condescend to discuss them'),(8683,2,152096,79998,'miranha or amajuaca cannibals said challenger jerking his thumb towards the reverberating wood'),(8684,2,655,13526,'no doubt sir summerlee answered'),(8685,2,591,3658,'like all such tribes i shall expect to find them of poly-synthetic speech and of mongolian type'),(8686,2,152131,136528,'polysynthetic certainly said challenger indulgently'),(8687,2,279,2564,'i am not aware that any other type of language exists in this continent and i have notes of more than a hundred'),(8688,2,51,31929,'the mongolian theory i regard with deep suspicion'),(8689,2,279,23063,'i should have thought that even a limited knowledge of comparative anatomy would have helped to verify it said summerlee bitterly'),(8690,2,128876,152200,'challenger thrust out his aggressive chin until he was all beard and hat-rim'),(8691,2,655,26565,'no doubt sir a limited knowledge would have that effect'),(8692,2,483,132236,'when one\'s knowledge is exhaustive one comes to other conclusions'),(8693,2,1056,850,'they glared at each other in mutual defiance while all round rose the distant whisper we will kill you--we will kill you if we can'),(8694,2,163,18033,'that night we moored our canoes with heavy stones for anchors in the center of the stream and made every preparation for a possible attack'),(8695,2,1155,2793,'nothing came however and with the dawn we pushed upon our way the drum-beating dying out behind us'),(8696,2,482,1965,'about three o\'clock in the afternoon we came to a very steep rapid more than a mile long--the very one in which professor challenger had suffered disaster upon his first journey'),(8697,2,279,582,'i confess that the sight of it consoled me for it was really the first direct corroboration slight as it was of the truth of his story'),(8698,2,51,79733,'the indians carried first our canoes and then our stores through the brushwood which is very thick at this point while we four whites our rifles on our shoulders walked between them and any danger coming from the woods'),(8699,2,509,9700,'before evening we had successfully passed the rapids and made our way some ten miles above them where we anchored for the night'),(8700,2,308,80095,'at this point i reckoned that we had come not less than a hundred miles up the tributary from the main stream'),(8701,2,67,42453,'it was in the early forenoon of the next day that we made the great departure'),(8702,2,5543,4669,'since dawn professor challenger had been acutely uneasy continually scanning each bank of the river'),(8703,2,5725,80095,'suddenly he gave an exclamation of satisfaction and pointed to a single tree which projected at a peculiar angle over the side of the stream'),(8704,2,994,163,'what do you make of that'),(8705,2,258,7867,'he asked'),(8706,2,67,142538,'it is surely an assai palm said summerlee'),(8707,2,67,152513,'it was an assai palm which i took for my landmark'),(8708,2,51,4669,'the secret opening is half a mile onwards upon the other side of the river'),(8709,2,74,53546,'there is no break in the trees'),(8710,2,163,67,'that is the wonder and the mystery of it'),(8711,2,74,12850,'there where you see light-green rushes instead of dark-green undergrowth there between the great cotton woods that is my private gate into the unknown'),(8712,2,1287,5591,'push through and you will understand'),(8713,2,67,461,'it was indeed a wonderful place'),(8714,2,6999,55421,'having reached the spot marked by a line of light-green rushes we poled out two canoes through them for some hundreds of yards and eventually emerged into a placid and shallow stream running clear and transparent over a sandy bottom'),(8715,2,67,135705,'it may have been twenty yards across and was banked in on each side by most luxuriant vegetation'),(8716,2,655,1653,'no one who had not observed that for a short distance reeds had taken the place of shrubs could possibly have guessed the existence of such a stream or dreamed of the fairyland beyond'),(8717,2,284,9113,'for a fairyland it was--the most wonderful that the imagination of man could conceive'),(8718,2,51,3136,'the thick vegetation met overhead interlacing into a natural pergola and through this tunnel of verdure in a golden twilight flowed the green pellucid river beautiful in itself but marvelous from the strange tints thrown by the vivid light from above filtered and tempered in its fall'),(8719,2,11120,136638,'clear as crystal motionless as a sheet of glass green as the edge of an iceberg it stretched in front of us under its leafy archway every stroke of our paddles sending a thousand ripples across its shining surface'),(8720,2,67,52145,'it was a fitting avenue to a land of wonders'),(8721,2,172,152810,'all sign of the indians had passed away but animal life was more frequent and the tameness of the creatures showed that they knew nothing of the hunter'),(8722,2,152811,7605,'fuzzy little black-velvet monkeys with snow-white teeth and gleaming mocking eyes chattered at us as we passed'),(8723,2,34,144647,'with a dull heavy splash an occasional cayman plunged in from the bank'),(8724,2,855,149005,'once a dark clumsy tapir stared at us from a gap in the bushes and then lumbered away through the forest once too the yellow sinuous form of a great puma whisked amid the brushwood and its green baleful eyes glared hatred at us over its tawny shoulder'),(8725,2,137407,135719,'bird life was abundant especially the wading birds stork heron and ibis gathering in little groups blue scarlet and white upon every log which jutted from the bank while beneath us the crystal water was alive with fish of every shape and color'),(8726,2,284,137884,'for three days we made our way up this tunnel of hazy green sunshine'),(8727,2,407,5847,'on the longer stretches one could hardly tell as one looked ahead where the distant green water ended and the distant green archway began'),(8728,2,51,1278,'the deep peace of this strange waterway was unbroken by any sign of man'),(8729,2,655,454,'no indian here'),(8730,2,813,6416,'too much afraid'),(8731,2,136971,148257,'curupuri said gomez'),(8732,2,136971,3588,'curupuri is the spirit of the woods lord john explained'),(8733,2,127528,130263,'it\'s a name for any kind of devil'),(8734,2,51,67,'the poor beggars think that there is something fearsome in this direction and therefore they avoid it'),(8735,2,407,149633,'on the third day it became evident that our journey in the canoes could not last much longer for the stream was rapidly growing more shallow'),(8736,2,7629,55421,'twice in as many hours we stuck upon the bottom'),(8737,2,27627,4669,'finally we pulled the boats up among the brushwood and spent the night on the bank of the river'),(8738,2,50,1874,'in the morning lord john and i made our way for a couple of miles through the forest keeping parallel with the stream but as it grew ever shallower we returned and reported what professor challenger had already suspected that we had reached the highest point to which the canoes could be brought'),(8739,2,1649,3373,'we drew them up therefore and concealed them among the bushes blazing a tree with our axes so that we should find them again'),(8740,2,1502,1965,'then we distributed the various burdens among us--guns ammunition food a tent blankets and the rest--and shouldering our packages we set forth upon the more laborious stage of our journey'),(8741,2,573,858,'an unfortunate quarrel between our pepper-pots marked the outset of our new stage'),(8742,2,128876,142538,'challenger had from the moment of joining us issued directions to the whole party much to the evident discontent of summerlee'),(8743,2,2530,1999,'now upon his assigning some duty to his fellow-professor it was only the carrying of an aneroid barometer the matter suddenly came to a head'),(8744,2,465,13783,'may i ask sir said summerlee with vicious calm in what capacity you take it upon yourself to issue these orders'),(8745,2,128876,153273,'challenger glared and bristled'),(8746,2,279,134841,'i do it professor summerlee as leader of this expedition'),(8747,2,279,16154,'i am compelled to tell you sir that i do not recognize you in that capacity'),(8748,2,128876,153304,'challenger bowed with unwieldy sarcasm'),(8749,2,290,47212,'perhaps you would define my exact position'),(8750,2,9487,5275,'yes sir'),(8751,2,2074,67,'you are a man whose veracity is upon trial and this committee is here to try it'),(8752,2,2074,153336,'you walk sir with your judges'),(8753,2,2783,424,'dear me'),(8754,2,2257,150081,'said challenger seating himself on the side of one of the canoes'),(8755,2,50,10708,'in that case you will of course go on your way and i will follow at my leisure'),(8756,2,257,19643,'if i am not the leader you cannot expect me to lead'),(8757,2,18463,6833,'thank heaven that there were two sane men--lord john roxton and myself--to prevent the petulance and folly of our learned professors from sending us back empty-handed to london'),(8758,2,392,153420,'such arguing and pleading and explaining before we could get them mollified'),(8759,2,1502,171,'then at last summerlee with his sneer and his pipe would move forwards and challenger would come rolling and grumbling after'),(8760,2,3,153461,'by some good fortune we discovered about this time that both our savants had the very poorest opinion of dr'),(8761,2,153462,129537,'illingworth of edinburgh'),(8762,2,153465,85616,'thenceforward that was our one safety and every strained situation was relieved by our introducing the name of the scotch zoologist when both our professors would form a temporary alliance and friendship in their detestation and abuse of this common rival'),(8763,2,132955,90159,'advancing in single file along the bank of the stream we soon found that it narrowed down to a mere brook and finally that it lost itself in a great green morass of sponge-like mosses into which we sank up to our knees'),(8764,2,51,534,'the place was horribly haunted by clouds of mosquitoes and every form of flying pest so we were glad to find solid ground again and to make a circuit among the trees which enabled us to outflank this pestilent morass which droned like an organ in the distance so loud was it with insect life'),(8765,2,407,2127,'on the second day after leaving our canoes we found that the whole character of the country changed'),(8766,2,2043,149066,'our road was persistently upwards and as we ascended the woods became thinner and lost their tropical luxuriance'),(8767,2,51,28,'the huge trees of the alluvial amazonian plain gave place to the phoenix and coco palms growing in scattered clumps with thick brushwood between'),(8768,2,50,153676,'in the damper hollows the mauritia palms threw out their graceful drooping fronds'),(8769,2,1649,153724,'we traveled entirely by compass and once or twice there were differences of opinion between challenger and the two indians when to quote the professor\'s indignant words the whole party agreed to trust the fallacious instincts of undeveloped savages rather than the highest product of modern european culture'),(8770,2,163,153766,'that we were justified in doing so was shown upon the third day when challenger admitted that he recognized several landmarks of his former journey and in one spot we actually came upon four fire-blackened stones which must have marked a camping-place'),(8771,2,51,153782,'the road still ascended and we crossed a rock-studded slope which took two days to traverse'),(8772,2,51,153822,'the vegetation had again changed and only the vegetable ivory tree remained with a great profusion of wonderful orchids among which i learned to recognize the rare nuttonia vexillaria and the glorious pink and scarlet blossoms of cattleya and odontoglossum'),(8773,2,4273,16434,'occasional brooks with pebbly bottoms and fern-draped banks gurgled down the shallow gorges in the hill and offered good camping-grounds every evening on the banks of some rock-studded pool where swarms of little blue-backed fish about the size and shape of english trout gave us a delicious supper'),(8774,2,407,148549,'on the ninth day after leaving the canoes having done as i reckon about a hundred and twenty miles we began to emerge from the trees which had grown smaller until they were mere shrubs'),(8775,2,81,134990,'their place was taken by an immense wilderness of bamboo which grew so thickly that we could only penetrate it by cutting a pathway with the machetes and billhooks of the indians'),(8776,2,67,150106,'it took us a long day traveling from seven in the morning till eight at night with only two breaks of one hour each to get through this obstacle'),(8777,2,2241,1473,'anything more monotonous and wearying could not be imagined for even at the most open places i could not see more than ten or twelve yards while usually my vision was limited to the back of lord john\'s cotton jacket in front of me and to the yellow wall within a foot of me on either side'),(8778,2,344,135772,'from above came one thin knife-edge of sunshine and fifteen feet over our heads one saw the tops of the reeds swaying against the deep blue sky'),(8779,2,279,2793,'i do not know what kind of creatures inhabit such a thicket but several times we heard the plunging of large heavy animals quite close to us'),(8780,2,344,154091,'from their sounds lord john judged them to be some form of wild cattle'),(8781,2,26,2045,'just as night fell we cleared the belt of bamboos and at once formed our camp exhausted by the interminable day'),(8782,2,480,3373,'early next morning we were again afoot and found that the character of the country had changed once again'),(8783,2,7459,4669,'behind us was the wall of bamboo as definite as if it marked the course of a river'),(8784,2,50,80086,'in front was an open plain sloping slightly upwards and dotted with clumps of tree-ferns the whole curving before us until it ended in a long whale-backed ridge'),(8785,2,37,154203,'this we reached about midday only to find a shallow valley beyond rising once again into a gentle incline which led to a low rounded sky-line'),(8786,2,67,17468,'it was here while we crossed the first of these hills that an incident occurred which may or may not have been important'),(8787,2,128875,106,'professor challenger who with the two local indians was in the van of the party stopped suddenly and pointed excitedly to the right'),(8788,2,42,154164,'as he did so we saw at the distance of a mile or so something which appeared to be a huge gray bird flap slowly up from the ground and skim smoothly off flying very low and straight until it was lost among the tree-ferns'),(8789,2,991,67,'did you see it'),(8790,2,4916,34423,'cried challenger in exultation'),(8791,2,142538,67,'summerlee did you see it'),(8792,2,892,137531,'his colleague was staring at the spot where the creature had disappeared'),(8793,2,994,473,'what do you claim that it was'),(8794,2,258,7867,'he asked'),(8795,2,40,137779,'to the best of my belief a pterodactyl'),(8796,2,142538,154343,'summerlee burst into derisive laughter a pter-fiddlestick'),(8797,2,2257,258,'said he'),(8798,2,67,139,'it was a stork if ever i saw one'),(8799,2,128876,4634,'challenger was too furious to speak'),(8800,2,258,50152,'he simply swung his pack upon his back and continued upon his march'),(8801,2,22823,122422,'lord john came abreast of me however and his face was more grave than was his wont'),(8802,2,258,410,'he had his zeiss glasses in his hand'),(8803,2,279,258,'i focused it before it got over the trees said he'),(8804,2,279,534,'i won\'t undertake to say what it was but i\'ll risk my reputation as a sportsman that it wasn\'t any bird that ever i clapped eyes on in my life'),(8805,2,1051,24717,'so there the matter stands'),(8806,2,60,9785,'are we really just at the edge of the unknown encountering the outlying pickets of this lost world of which our leader speaks'),(8807,2,279,513,'i give you the incident as it occurred and you will know as much as i do'),(8808,2,67,1783,'it stands alone for we saw nothing more which could be called remarkable'),(8809,2,1,154164,'and now my readers if ever i have any i have brought you up the broad river and through the screen of rushes and down the green tunnel and up the long slope of palm trees and through the bamboo brake and across the plain of tree-ferns'),(8810,2,308,2793,'at last our destination lay in full sight of us'),(8811,2,483,2109,'when we had crossed the second ridge we saw before us an irregular palm-studded plain and then the line of high red cliffs which i have seen in the picture'),(8812,2,74,648,'there it lies even as i write and there can be no question that it is the same'),(8813,2,308,4334,'at the nearest point it is about seven miles from our present camp and it curves away stretching as far as i can see'),(8814,2,128876,139448,'challenger struts about like a prize peacock and summerlee is silent but still sceptical'),(8815,2,1351,1690,'another day should bring some of our doubts to an end'),(8816,2,39737,410,'meanwhile as jose whose arm was pierced by a broken bamboo insists upon returning i send this letter back in his charge and only hope that it may eventually come to hand'),(8817,2,279,154691,'i will write again as the occasion serves'),(8818,2,279,5591,'i have enclosed with this a rough chart of our journey which may have the effect of making the account rather easier to understand'),(8819,2,4328,67,'chapter ix who could have foreseen it'),(8820,2,23,2793,'a dreadful thing has happened to us'),(8821,2,59,67,'who could have foreseen it'),(8822,2,279,141303,'i cannot foresee any end to our troubles'),(8823,2,67,461,'it may be that we are condemned to spend our whole lives in this strange inaccessible place'),(8824,2,279,24280,'i am still so confused that i can hardly think clearly of the facts of the present or of the chances of the future'),(8825,2,40,9700,'to my astounded senses the one seems most terrible and the other as black as night'),(8826,2,655,283,'no men have ever found themselves in a worse position nor is there any use in disclosing to you our exact geographical situation and asking our friends for a relief party'),(8827,2,1232,129699,'even if they could send one our fate will in all human probability be decided long before it could arrive in south america'),(8828,2,1649,154870,'we are in truth as far from any human aid as if we were in the moon'),(8829,2,257,2793,'if we are to win through it is only our own qualities which can save us'),(8830,2,279,46322,'i have as companions three remarkable men men of great brain-power and of unshaken courage'),(8831,2,74,2068,'there lies our one and only hope'),(8832,2,67,145505,'it is only when i look upon the untroubled faces of my comrades that i see some glimmer through the darkness'),(8833,2,4087,1056,'outwardly i trust that i appear as unconcerned as they'),(8834,2,154940,43140,'inwardly i am filled with apprehension'),(8835,2,2792,213,'let me give you with as much detail as i can the sequence of events which have led us to this catastrophe'),(8836,2,483,4513,'when i finished my last letter i stated that we were within seven miles from an enormous line of ruddy cliffs which encircled beyond all doubt the plateau of which professor challenger spoke'),(8837,2,81,155043,'their height as we approached them seemed to me in some places to be greater than he had stated--running up in parts to at least a thousand feet--and they were curiously striated in a manner which is i believe characteristic of basaltic upheavals'),(8838,2,707,129537,'something of the sort is to be seen in salisbury crags at edinburgh'),(8839,2,51,53546,'the summit showed every sign of a luxuriant vegetation with bushes near the edge and farther back many high trees'),(8840,2,74,4334,'there was no indication of any life that we could see'),(8841,2,163,26336,'that night we pitched our camp immediately under the cliff--a most wild and desolate spot'),(8842,2,51,9590,'the crags above us were not merely perpendicular but curved outwards at the top so that ascent was out of the question'),(8843,2,9704,67843,'close to us was the high thin pinnacle of rock which i believe i mentioned earlier in this narrative'),(8844,2,67,28,'it is like a broad red church spire the top of it being level with the plateau but a great chasm gaping between'),(8845,2,407,135753,'on the summit of it there grew one high tree'),(8846,2,1191,693,'both pinnacle and cliff were comparatively low--some five or six hundred feet i should think'),(8847,2,67,155207,'it was on that said professor challenger pointing to this tree that the pterodactyl was perched'),(8848,2,279,247,'i climbed half-way up the rock before i shot him'),(8849,2,279,1051,'i am inclined to think that a good mountaineer like myself could ascend the rock to the top though he would of course be no nearer to the plateau when he had done so'),(8850,2,42,53335,'as challenger spoke of his pterodactyl i glanced at professor summerlee and for the first time i seemed to see some signs of a dawning credulity and repentance'),(8851,2,74,32476,'there was no sneer upon his thin lips but on the contrary a gray drawn look of excitement and amazement'),(8852,2,128876,17184,'challenger saw it too and reveled in the first taste of victory'),(8853,2,53,155356,'of course said he with his clumsy and ponderous sarcasm professor summerlee will understand that when i speak of a pterodactyl i mean a stork--only it is the kind of stork which has no feathers a leathery skin membranous wings and teeth in its jaws'),(8854,2,258,4025,'he grinned and blinked and bowed until his colleague turned and walked away'),(8855,2,50,2793,'in the morning after a frugal breakfast of coffee and manioc--we had to be economical of our stores--we held a council of war as to the best method of ascending to the plateau above us'),(8856,2,128876,155420,'challenger presided with a solemnity as if he were the lord chief justice on the bench'),(8857,2,2109,149402,'picture him seated upon a rock his absurd boyish straw hat tilted on the back of his head his supercilious eyes dominating us from under his drooping lids his great black beard wagging as he slowly defined our present situation and our future movements'),(8858,2,1480,27678,'beneath him you might have seen the three of us--myself sunburnt young and vigorous after our open-air tramp summerlee solemn but still critical behind his eternal pipe lord john as keen as a razor-edge with his supple alert figure leaning upon his rifle and his eager eyes fixed eagerly upon the speaker'),(8859,2,7459,150309,'behind us were grouped the two swarthy half-breeds and the little knot of indians while in front and above us towered those huge ruddy ribs of rocks which kept us from our goal'),(8860,2,279,155226,'i need not say said our leader that on the occasion of my last visit i exhausted every means of climbing the cliff and where i failed i do not think that anyone else is likely to succeed for i am something of a mountaineer'),(8861,2,279,2530,'i had none of the appliances of a rock-climber with me but i have taken the precaution to bring them now'),(8862,2,34,163,'with their aid i am positive i could climb that detached pinnacle to the summit but so long as the main cliff overhangs it is vain to attempt ascending that'),(8863,2,279,155666,'i was hurried upon my last visit by the approach of the rainy season and by the exhaustion of my supplies'),(8864,2,2457,1274,'these considerations limited my time and i can only claim that i have surveyed about six miles of the cliff to the east of us finding no possible way up'),(8865,2,994,513,'what then shall we now do'),(8866,2,74,142538,'there seems to be only one reasonable course said professor summerlee'),(8867,2,257,155119,'if you have explored the east we should travel along the base of the cliff to the west and seek for a practicable point for our ascent'),(8868,2,128237,1015,'that\'s it said lord john'),(8869,2,51,24407,'the odds are that this plateau is of no great size and we shall travel round it until we either find an easy way up it or come back to the point from which we started'),(8870,2,279,155857,'i have already explained to our young friend here said challenger he has a way of alluding to me as if i were a school child ten years old that it is quite impossible that there should be an easy way up anywhere for the simple reason that if there were the summit would not be isolated and those conditions would not obtain which have effected so singular an interference with the general laws of survival'),(8871,2,119,155887,'yet i admit that there may very well be places where an expert human climber may reach the summit and yet a cumbrous and heavy animal be unable to descend'),(8872,2,67,346,'it is certain that there is a point where an ascent is possible'),(8873,2,1338,5275,'how do you know that sir'),(8874,2,7867,145213,'asked summerlee sharply'),(8875,2,99,155119,'because my predecessor the american maple white actually made such an ascent'),(8876,2,1338,133346,'how otherwise could he have seen the monster which he sketched in his notebook'),(8877,2,74,142538,'there you reason somewhat ahead of the proved facts said the stubborn summerlee'),(8878,2,279,15005,'i admit your plateau because i have seen it but i have not as yet satisfied myself that it contains any form of life whatever'),(8879,2,994,9343,'what you admit sir or what you do not admit is really of inconceivably small importance'),(8880,2,279,6527,'i am glad to perceive that the plateau itself has actually obtruded itself upon your intelligence'),(8881,2,258,6950,'he glanced up at it and then to our amazement he sprang from his rock and seizing summerlee by the neck he tilted his face into the air'),(8882,2,2530,5275,'now sir'),(8883,2,258,147605,'he shouted hoarse with excitement'),(8884,2,513,534,'do i help you to realize that the plateau contains some animal life'),(8885,2,279,135785,'i have said that a thick fringe of green overhung the edge of the cliff'),(8886,2,976,9198,'out of this there had emerged a black glistening object'),(8887,2,42,1999,'as it came slowly forth and overhung the chasm we saw that it was a very large snake with a peculiar flat spade-like head'),(8888,2,67,156120,'it wavered and quivered above us for a minute the morning sun gleaming upon its sleek sinuous coils'),(8889,2,1502,137531,'then it slowly drew inwards and disappeared'),(8890,2,142538,6950,'summerlee had been so interested that he had stood unresisting while challenger tilted his head into the air'),(8891,2,2530,32453,'now he shook his colleague off and came back to his dignity'),(8892,2,279,73001,'i should be glad professor challenger said he if you could see your way to make any remarks which may occur to you without seizing me by the chin'),(8893,2,1232,8771,'even the appearance of a very ordinary rock python does not appear to justify such a liberty'),(8894,2,295,44875,'but there is life upon the plateau all the same his colleague replied in triumph'),(8895,2,1,155119,'and now having demonstrated this important conclusion so that it is clear to anyone however prejudiced or obtuse i am of opinion that we cannot do better than break up our camp and travel to westward until we find some means of ascent'),(8896,2,51,2733,'the ground at the foot of the cliff was rocky and broken so that the going was slow and difficult'),(8897,2,5725,156291,'suddenly we came however upon something which cheered our hearts'),(8898,2,67,156319,'it was the site of an old encampment with several empty chicago meat tins a bottle labeled brandy a broken tin-opener and a quantity of other travelers\' debris'),(8899,2,23,156335,'a crumpled disintegrated newspaper revealed itself as the chicago democrat though the date had been obliterated'),(8900,2,55,128876,'not mine said challenger'),(8901,2,67,156344,'it must be maple white\'s'),(8902,2,22823,156299,'lord john had been gazing curiously at a great tree-fern which overshadowed the encampment'),(8903,2,279,258,'i say look at this said he'),(8904,2,279,156373,'i believe it is meant for a sign-post'),(8905,2,23,156254,'a slip of hard wood had been nailed to the tree in such a way as to point to the westward'),(8906,2,387,128876,'most certainly a sign-post said challenger'),(8907,2,994,2224,'what else'),(8908,2,14072,255,'finding himself upon a dangerous errand our pioneer has left this sign so that any party which follows him may know the way he has taken'),(8909,2,290,2839,'perhaps we shall come upon some other indications as we proceed'),(8910,2,1649,1212,'we did indeed but they were of a terrible and most unexpected nature'),(8911,2,5002,1965,'immediately beneath the cliff there grew a considerable patch of high bamboo like that which we had traversed in our journey'),(8912,2,2455,156495,'many of these stems were twenty feet high with sharp strong tops so that even as they stood they made formidable spears'),(8913,2,1649,67,'we were passing along the edge of this cover when my eye was caught by the gleam of something white within it'),(8914,2,156518,129381,'thrusting in my head between the stems i found myself gazing at a fleshless skull'),(8915,2,51,2679,'the whole skeleton was there but the skull had detached itself and lay some feet nearer to the open'),(8916,2,34,156576,'with a few blows from the machetes of our indians we cleared the spot and were able to study the details of this old tragedy'),(8917,2,241,135538,'only a few shreds of clothes could still be distinguished but there were the remains of boots upon the bony feet and it was very clear that the dead man was a european'),(8918,2,23,139569,'a gold watch by hudson of new york and a chain which held a stylographic pen lay among the bones'),(8919,2,74,156637,'there was also a silver cigarette-case with j'),(8920,2,344,23,'from a'),(8921,2,787,156642,'upon the lid'),(8922,2,51,509,'the state of the metal seemed to show that the catastrophe had occurred no great time before'),(8923,2,59,104,'who can he be'),(8924,2,7867,1015,'asked lord john'),(8925,2,3081,130263,'poor devil'),(8926,2,138,44639,'every bone in his body seems to be broken'),(8927,2,1,142538,'and the bamboo grows through his smashed ribs said summerlee'),(8928,2,67,12828,'it is a fast-growing plant but it is surely inconceivable that this body could have been here while the canes grew to be twenty feet in length'),(8929,2,42,543,'as to the man\'s identity said professor challenger i have no doubt whatever upon that point'),(8930,2,42,1894,'as i made my way up the river before i reached you at the fazenda i instituted very particular inquiries about maple white'),(8931,2,308,1155,'at para they knew nothing'),(8932,2,26320,135547,'fortunately i had a definite clew for there was a particular picture in his sketch-book which showed him taking lunch with a certain ecclesiastic at rosario'),(8933,2,37,5609,'this priest i was able to find and though he proved a very argumentative fellow who took it absurdly amiss that i should point out to him the corrosive effect which modern science must have upon his beliefs he none the less gave me some positive information'),(8934,2,155915,133885,'maple white passed rosario four years ago or two years before i saw his dead body'),(8935,2,258,135528,'he was not alone at the time but there was a friend an american named james colver who remained in the boat and did not meet this ecclesiastic'),(8936,2,279,135497,'i think therefore that there can be no doubt that we are now looking upon the remains of this james colver'),(8937,2,5398,511,'nor said lord john is there much doubt as to how he met his death'),(8938,2,258,156924,'he has fallen or been chucked from the top and so been impaled'),(8939,2,1338,84985,'how else could he come by his broken bones and how could he have been stuck through by these canes with their points so high above our heads'),(8940,2,23,2458,'a hush came over us as we stood round these shattered remains and realized the truth of lord john roxton\'s words'),(8941,2,51,156983,'the beetling head of the cliff projected over the cane-brake'),(8942,2,10553,203,'undoubtedly he had fallen from above'),(8943,2,295,24022,'but had he fallen'),(8944,2,259,30654,'had it been an accident'),(8945,2,156999,118264,'or--already ominous and terrible possibilities began to form round that unknown land'),(8946,2,1649,146416,'we moved off in silence and continued to coast round the line of cliffs which were as even and unbroken as some of those monstrous antarctic ice-fields which i have seen depicted as stretching from horizon to horizon and towering high above the mast-heads of the exploring vessel'),(8947,2,50,32511,'in five miles we saw no rift or break'),(8948,2,1,2068,'and then suddenly we perceived something which filled us with new hope'),(8949,2,50,157101,'in a hollow of the rock protected from rain there was drawn a rough arrow in chalk pointing still to the westwards'),(8950,2,155915,128876,'maple white again said professor challenger'),(8951,2,258,247,'he had some presentiment that worthy footsteps would follow close behind him'),(8952,2,258,1502,'he had chalk then'),(8953,2,23,135165,'a box of colored chalks was among the effects i found in his knapsack'),(8954,2,279,157148,'i remember that the white one was worn to a stump'),(8955,2,163,142538,'that is certainly good evidence said summerlee'),(8956,2,1649,156254,'we can only accept his guidance and follow on to the westward'),(8957,2,1649,52871,'we had proceeded some five more miles when again we saw a white arrow upon the rocks'),(8958,2,67,135761,'it was at a point where the face of the cliff was for the first time split into a narrow cleft'),(8959,2,148771,40255,'inside the cleft was a second guidance mark which pointed right up it with the tip somewhat elevated as if the spot indicated were above the level of the ground'),(8960,2,67,55421,'it was a solemn place for the walls were so gigantic and the slit of blue sky so narrow and so obscured by a double fringe of verdure that only a dim and shadowy light penetrated to the bottom'),(8961,2,1649,157303,'we had had no food for many hours and were very weary with the stony and irregular journey but our nerves were too strung to allow us to halt'),(8962,2,1649,157329,'we ordered the camp to be pitched however and leaving the indians to arrange it we four with the two half-breeds proceeded up the narrow gorge'),(8963,2,67,155119,'it was not more than forty feet across at the mouth but it rapidly closed until it ended in an acute angle too straight and smooth for an ascent'),(8964,2,829,141660,'certainly it was not this which our pioneer had attempted to indicate'),(8965,2,1649,4162,'we made our way back--the whole gorge was not more than a quarter of a mile deep--and then suddenly the quick eyes of lord john fell upon what we were seeking'),(8966,2,5476,47715,'high up above our heads amid the dark shadows there was one circle of deeper gloom'),(8967,2,5320,157427,'surely it could only be the opening of a cave'),(8968,2,51,1274,'the base of the cliff was heaped with loose stones at the spot and it was not difficult to clamber up'),(8969,2,483,9316,'when we reached it all doubt was removed'),(8970,2,55,157094,'not only was it an opening into the rock but on the side of it there was marked once again the sign of the arrow'),(8971,2,454,155119,'here was the point and this the means by which maple white and his ill-fated comrade had made their ascent'),(8972,2,1649,855,'we were too excited to return to the camp but must make our first exploration at once'),(8973,2,22823,11189,'lord john had an electric torch in his knapsack and this had to serve us as light'),(8974,2,258,157556,'he advanced throwing his little clear circlet of yellow radiance before him while in single file we followed at his heels'),(8975,2,51,152254,'the cave had evidently been water-worn the sides being smooth and the floor covered with rounded stones'),(8976,2,67,127625,'it was of such a size that a single man could just fit through by stooping'),(8977,2,284,157608,'for fifty yards it ran almost straight into the rock and then it ascended at an angle of forty-five'),(8978,2,728,2793,'presently this incline became even steeper and we found ourselves climbing upon hands and knees among loose rubble which slid from beneath us'),(8979,2,5725,143090,'suddenly an exclamation broke from lord roxton'),(8980,2,127528,157640,'it\'s blocked'),(8981,2,2257,258,'said he'),(8982,2,157643,157663,'clustering behind him we saw in the yellow field of light a wall of broken basalt which extended to the ceiling'),(8983,2,51,50,'the roof has fallen in'),(8984,2,50,5872,'in vain we dragged out some of the pieces');
INSERT INTO `sentence` VALUES (8985,2,51,2793,'the only effect was that the larger ones became detached and threatened to roll down the gradient and crush us'),(8986,2,67,67,'it was evident that the obstacle was far beyond any efforts which we could make to remove it'),(8987,2,51,157727,'the road by which maple white had ascended was no longer available'),(8988,2,813,77102,'too much cast down to speak we stumbled down the dark tunnel and made our way back to the camp'),(8989,2,139,6712,'one incident occurred however before we left the gorge which is of importance in view of what came afterwards'),(8990,2,1649,27369,'we had gathered in a little group at the bottom of the chasm some forty feet beneath the mouth of the cave when a huge rock rolled suddenly downwards--and shot past us with tremendous force'),(8991,2,67,2793,'it was the narrowest escape for one or all of us'),(8992,2,1649,135781,'we could not ourselves see whence the rock had come but our half-breed servants who were still at the opening of the cave said that it had flown past them and must therefore have fallen from the summit'),(8993,2,4586,135785,'looking upwards we could see no sign of movement above us amidst the green jungle which topped the cliff'),(8994,2,74,138041,'there could be little doubt however that the stone was aimed at us so the incident surely pointed to humanity--and malevolent humanity--upon the plateau'),(8995,2,1649,28339,'we withdrew hurriedly from the chasm our minds full of this new development and its bearing upon our plans'),(8996,2,51,139,'the situation was difficult enough before but if the obstructions of nature were increased by the deliberate opposition of man then our case was indeed a hopeless one'),(8997,2,1,151477,'and yet as we looked up at that beautiful fringe of verdure only a few hundreds of feet above our heads there was not one of us who could conceive the idea of returning to london until we had explored it to its depths'),(8998,2,407,80036,'on discussing the situation we determined that our best course was to continue to coast round the plateau in the hope of finding some other means of reaching the top'),(8999,2,51,1108,'the line of cliffs which had decreased considerably in height had already begun to trend from west to north and if we could take this as representing the arc of a circle the whole circumference could not be very great'),(9000,2,308,158069,'at the worst then we should be back in a few days at our starting-point'),(9001,2,1649,43022,'we made a march that day which totaled some two-and-twenty miles without any change in our prospects'),(9002,2,279,158120,'i may mention that our aneroid shows us that in the continual incline which we have ascended since we abandoned our canoes we have risen to no less than three thousand feet above sea-level'),(9003,2,75081,135705,'hence there is a considerable change both in the temperature and in the vegetation'),(9004,2,1649,145498,'we have shaken off some of that horrible insect life which is the bane of tropical travel'),(9005,2,23,7459,'a few palms still survive and many tree-ferns but the amazonian trees have been all left behind'),(9006,2,67,52871,'it was pleasant to see the convolvulus the passion-flower and the begonia all reminding me of home here among these inhospitable rocks'),(9007,2,74,158222,'there was a red begonia just the same color as one that is kept in a pot in the window of a certain villa in streatham--but i am drifting into private reminiscence'),(9008,2,163,2793,'that night--i am still speaking of the first day of our circumnavigation of the plateau--a great experience awaited us and one which for ever set at rest any doubt which we could have had as to the wonders so near us'),(9009,2,2074,636,'you will realize as you read it my dear mr'),(9010,2,128880,67,'mcardle and possibly for the first time that the paper has not sent me on a wild-goose chase and that there is inconceivably fine copy waiting for the world whenever we have the professor\'s leave to make use of it'),(9011,2,279,369,'i shall not dare to publish these articles unless i can bring back my proofs to england or i shall be hailed as the journalistic munchausen of all time'),(9012,2,279,146074,'i have no doubt that you feel the same way yourself and that you would not care to stake the whole credit of the gazette upon this adventure until we can meet the chorus of criticism and scepticism which such articles must of necessity elicit'),(9013,2,1051,129516,'so this wonderful incident which would make such a headline for the old paper must still wait its turn in the editorial drawer'),(9014,2,1,158431,'and yet it was all over in a flash and there was no sequel to it save in our own convictions'),(9015,2,994,37,'what occurred was this'),(9016,2,22823,2140,'lord john had shot an ajouti--which is a small pig-like animal--and half of it having been given to the indians we were cooking the other half upon our fire'),(9017,2,74,158482,'there is a chill in the air after dark and we had all drawn close to the blaze'),(9018,2,51,11635,'the night was moonless but there were some stars and one could see for a little distance across the plain'),(9019,2,43,158521,'well suddenly out of the darkness out of the night there swooped something with a swish like an aeroplane'),(9020,2,51,88510,'the whole group of us were covered for an instant by a canopy of leathery wings and i had a momentary vision of a long snake-like neck a fierce red greedy eye and a great snapping beak filled to my amazement with little gleaming teeth'),(9021,2,51,6451,'the next instant it was gone--and so was our dinner'),(9022,2,23,2793,'a huge black shadow twenty feet across skimmed up into the air for an instant the monster wings blotted out the stars and then it vanished over the brow of the cliff above us'),(9023,2,1649,112,'we all sat in amazed silence round the fire like the heroes of virgil when the harpies came down upon them'),(9024,2,67,4634,'it was summerlee who was the first to 