o we fairly took to our heels and ran until we were exhausted'),(9039,2,279,152650,'i shall always remember as we looked back how far behind we could see the heads and necks of our horrible pursuers rising and falling amid the reeds'),(9040,2,158834,159010,'jaracaca swamp we named it in the map which we are constructing'),(9041,2,51,54921,'the cliffs upon the farther side had lost their ruddy tint being chocolate-brown in color the vegetation was more scattered along the top of them and they had sunk to three or four hundred feet in height but in no place did we find any point where they could be ascended'),(9042,2,257,112,'if anything they were more impossible than at the first point where we had met them'),(9043,2,81,64385,'their absolute steepness is indicated in the photograph which i took over the stony desert'),(9044,2,5320,9844,'surely said i as we discussed the situation the rain must find its way down somehow'),(9045,2,74,52871,'there are bound to be water-channels in the rocks'),(9046,2,2043,149005,'our young friend has glimpses of lucidity said professor challenger patting me upon the shoulder'),(9047,2,51,12410,'the rain must go somewhere i repeated'),(9048,2,258,159146,'he keeps a firm grip upon actuality'),(9049,2,51,52871,'the only drawback is that we have conclusively proved by ocular demonstration that there are no water channels down the rocks'),(9050,2,4187,351,'where then does it go'),(9051,2,279,38859,'i persisted'),(9052,2,279,156125,'i think it may be fairly assumed that if it does not come outwards it must run inwards'),(9053,2,1502,133141,'then there is a lake in the center'),(9054,2,1051,302,'so i should suppose'),(9055,2,67,142538,'it is more than likely that the lake may be an old crater said summerlee'),(9056,2,51,138253,'the whole formation is of course highly volcanic'),(9057,2,295,158819,'but however that may be i should expect to find the surface of the plateau slope inwards with a considerable sheet of water in the center which may drain off by some subterranean channel into the marshes of the jaracaca swamp'),(9058,2,123,130686,'or evaporation might preserve an equilibrium remarked challenger and the two learned men wandered off into one of their usual scientific arguments which were as comprehensible as chinese to the layman'),(9059,2,407,135748,'on the sixth day we completed our first circuit of the cliffs and found ourselves back at the first camp beside the isolated pinnacle of rock'),(9060,2,1649,135785,'we were a disconsolate party for nothing could have been more minute than our investigation and it was absolutely certain that there was no single point where the most active human being could possibly hope to scale the cliff'),(9061,2,51,101605,'the place which maple white\'s chalk-marks had indicated as his own means of access was now entirely impassable'),(9062,2,994,2530,'what were we to do now'),(9063,2,2043,159410,'our stores of provisions supplemented by our guns were holding out well but the day must come when they would need replenishment'),(9064,2,50,77102,'in a couple of months the rains might be expected and we should be washed out of our camp'),(9065,2,51,1014,'the rock was harder than marble and any attempt at cutting a path for so great a height was more than our time or resources would admit'),(9066,2,655,91127,'no wonder that we looked gloomily at each other that night and sought our blankets with hardly a word exchanged'),(9067,2,279,247,'i remember that as i dropped off to sleep my last recollection was that challenger was squatting like a monstrous bull-frog by the fire his huge head in his hands sunk apparently in the deepest thought and entirely oblivious to the good-night which i wished him'),(9068,2,295,6978,'but it was a very different challenger who greeted us in the morning--a challenger with contentment and self-congratulation shining from his whole person'),(9069,2,258,67,'he faced us as we assembled for breakfast with a deprecating false modesty in his eyes as who should say i know that i deserve all that you can say but i pray you to spare my blushes by not saying it'),(9070,2,892,131574,'his beard bristled exultantly his chest was thrown out and his hand was thrust into the front of his jacket'),(9071,2,1051,77070,'so in his fancy may he see himself sometimes gracing the vacant pedestal in trafalgar square and adding one more to the horrors of the london streets'),(9072,2,258,132050,'he cried his teeth shining through his beard'),(9073,2,4570,409,'gentlemen you may congratulate me and we may congratulate each other'),(9074,2,51,159657,'the problem is solved'),(9075,2,2074,1274,'you have found a way up'),(9076,2,279,1051,'i venture to think so'),(9077,2,1,4187,'and where'),(9078,2,284,106,'for answer he pointed to the spire-like pinnacle upon our right'),(9079,2,2043,67,'our faces--or mine at least--fell as we surveyed it'),(9080,2,163,17987,'that it could be climbed we had our companion\'s assurance'),(9081,2,295,138041,'but a horrible abyss lay between it and the plateau'),(9082,2,1649,159717,'we can never get across i gasped'),(9083,2,1649,258,'we can at least all reach the summit said he'),(9084,2,483,1629,'when we are up i may be able to show you that the resources of an inventive mind are not yet exhausted'),(9085,2,171,159764,'after breakfast we unpacked the bundle in which our leader had brought his climbing accessories'),(9086,2,344,159790,'from it he took a coil of the strongest and lightest rope a hundred and fifty feet in length with climbing irons clamps and other devices'),(9087,2,22823,4005,'lord john was an experienced mountaineer and summerlee had done some rough climbing at various times so that i was really the novice at rock-work of the party but my strength and activity may have made up for my want of experience'),(9088,2,67,1999,'it was not in reality a very stiff task though there were moments which made my hair bristle upon my head'),(9089,2,51,135748,'the first half was perfectly easy but from there upwards it became continually steeper until for the last fifty feet we were literally clinging with our fingers and toes to tiny ledges and crevices in the rock'),(9090,2,279,74,'i could not have accomplished it nor could summerlee if challenger had not gained the summit it was extraordinary to see such activity in so unwieldy a creature and there fixed the rope round the trunk of the considerable tree which grew there'),(9091,2,34,135781,'with this as our support we were soon able to scramble up the jagged wall until we found ourselves upon the small grassy platform some twenty-five feet each way which formed the summit'),(9092,2,51,156470,'the first impression which i received when i had recovered my breath was of the extraordinary view over the country which we had traversed'),(9093,2,51,154203,'the whole brazilian plain seemed to lie beneath us extending away and away until it ended in dim blue mists upon the farthest sky-line'),(9094,2,50,1653,'in the foreground was the long slope strewn with rocks and dotted with tree-ferns farther off in the middle distance looking over the saddle-back hill i could just see the yellow and green mass of bamboos through which we had passed and then gradually the vegetation increased until it formed the huge forest which extended as far as the eyes could reach and for a good two thousand miles beyond'),(9095,2,279,149005,'i was still drinking in this wonderful panorama when the heavy hand of the professor fell upon my shoulder'),(9096,2,37,160113,'this way my young friend said he vestigia nulla retrorsum'),(9097,2,2027,150309,'never look rearwards but always to our glorious goal'),(9098,2,51,7571,'the level of the plateau when i turned was exactly that on which we stood and the green bank of bushes with occasional trees was so near that it was difficult to realize how inaccessible it remained'),(9099,2,308,13944,'at a rough guess the gulf was forty feet across but so far as i could see it might as well have been forty miles'),(9100,2,279,159704,'i placed one arm round the trunk of the tree and leaned over the abyss'),(9101,2,987,2793,'far down were the small dark figures of our servants looking up at us'),(9102,2,51,424,'the wall was absolutely precipitous as was that which faced me'),(9103,2,37,142538,'this is indeed curious said the creaking voice of professor summerlee'),(9104,2,279,160252,'i turned and found that he was examining with great interest the tree to which i clung'),(9105,2,163,917,'that smooth bark and those small ribbed leaves seemed familiar to my eyes'),(9106,2,4949,160271,'why i cried it\'s a beech'),(9107,2,2575,142538,'exactly said summerlee'),(9108,2,23,118264,'a fellow-countryman in a far land'),(9109,2,55,10299,'not only a fellow-countryman my good sir said challenger but also if i may be allowed to enlarge your simile an ally of the first value'),(9110,2,37,160313,'this beech tree will be our saviour'),(9111,2,3,4303,'by george'),(9112,2,4916,80175,'cried lord john a bridge'),(9113,2,2575,80175,'exactly my friends a bridge'),(9114,2,67,8908,'it is not for nothing that i expended an hour last night in focusing my mind upon the situation'),(9115,2,279,132867,'i have some recollection of once remarking to our young friend here that g'),(9116,2,75,132350,'is at his best when his back is to the wall'),(9117,2,4196,132350,'last night you will admit that all our backs were to the wall'),(9118,2,295,976,'but where will-power and intellect go together there is always a way out'),(9119,2,23,159704,'a drawbridge had to be found which could be dropped across the abyss'),(9120,2,128790,67,'behold it'),(9121,2,67,3968,'it was certainly a brilliant idea'),(9122,2,51,155164,'the tree was a good sixty feet in height and if it only fell the right way it would easily cross the chasm'),(9123,2,128876,54921,'challenger had slung the camp axe over his shoulder when he ascended'),(9124,2,2530,424,'now he handed it to me'),(9125,2,2043,258,'our young friend has the thews and sinews said he'),(9126,2,279,159841,'i think he will be the most useful at this task'),(9127,2,279,957,'i must beg however that you will kindly refrain from thinking for yourself and that you will do exactly what you are told'),(9128,2,3470,12385,'under his direction i cut such gashes in the sides of the trees as would ensure that it should fall as we desired'),(9129,2,67,2733,'it had already a strong natural tilt in the direction of the plateau so that the matter was not difficult'),(9130,2,27627,1015,'finally i set to work in earnest upon the trunk taking turn and turn with lord john'),(9131,2,50,1473,'in a little over an hour there was a loud crack the tree swayed forward and then crashed over burying its branches among the bushes on the farther side'),(9132,2,51,554,'the severed trunk rolled to the very edge of our platform and for one terrible second we all thought it was over'),(9133,2,67,12850,'it balanced itself however a few inches from the edge and there was our bridge to the unknown'),(9134,2,172,11455,'all of us without a word shook hands with professor challenger who raised his straw hat and bowed deeply to each in turn'),(9135,2,279,1695,'i claim the honor said he to be the first to cross to the unknown land--a fitting subject no doubt for some future historical painting'),(9136,2,258,6700,'he had approached the bridge when lord john laid his hand upon his coat'),(9137,2,413,67,'my dear chap said he i really cannot allow it'),(9138,2,400,5275,'cannot allow it sir'),(9139,2,51,16209,'the head went back and the beard forward'),(9140,2,483,11882,'when it is a matter of science don\'t you know i follow your lead because you are by way of bein\' a man of science'),(9141,2,295,129546,'but it\'s up to you to follow me when you come into my department'),(9142,2,1373,5275,'your department sir'),(9143,2,1649,9989,'we all have our professions and soldierin\' is mine'),(9144,2,1649,160786,'we are accordin\' to my ideas invadin\' a new country which may or may not be chock-full of enemies of sorts'),(9145,2,40,55671,'to barge blindly into it for want of a little common sense and patience isn\'t my notion of management'),(9146,2,51,28766,'the remonstrance was too reasonable to be disregarded'),(9147,2,128876,125325,'challenger tossed his head and shrugged his heavy shoulders'),(9148,2,43,453,'well sir what do you propose'),(9149,2,284,80175,'for all i know there may be a tribe of cannibals waitin\' for lunch-time among those very bushes said lord john looking across the bridge'),(9150,2,127528,798,'it\'s better to learn wisdom before you get into a cookin\'-pot so we will content ourselves with hopin\' that there is no trouble waitin\' for us and at the same time we will act as if there were'),(9151,2,129000,409,'malone and i will go down again therefore and we will fetch up the four rifles together with gomez and the other'),(9152,2,139,12535,'one man can then go across and the rest will cover him with guns until he sees that it is safe for the whole crowd to come along'),(9153,2,128876,9590,'challenger sat down upon the cut stump and groaned his impatience but summerlee and i were of one mind that lord john was our leader when such practical details were in question'),(9154,2,51,155119,'the climb was a more simple thing now that the rope dangled down the face of the worst part of the ascent'),(9155,2,6991,147302,'within an hour we had brought up the rifles and a shot-gun'),(9156,2,51,139,'the half-breeds had ascended also and under lord john\'s orders they had carried up a bale of provisions in case our first exploration should be a long one'),(9157,2,1649,135318,'we had each bandoliers of cartridges'),(9158,2,2530,622,'now challenger if you really insist upon being the first man in said lord john when every preparation was complete'),(9159,2,279,2587,'i am much indebted to you for your gracious permission said the angry professor for never was a man so intolerant of every form of authority'),(9160,2,5543,5138,'since you are good