the base of the cliff i saw as i looked over a tangled mass of branches and splintered trunk'),(9182,2,67,135753,'it was our beech tree'),(9183,2,259,2751,'had the edge of the platform crumbled and let it through'),(9184,2,284,4835,'for a moment this explanation was in all our minds'),(9185,2,51,134282,'the next from the farther side of the rocky pinnacle before us a swarthy face the face of gomez the half-breed was slowly protruded'),(9186,2,9487,3355,'yes it was gomez but no longer the gomez of the demure smile and the mask-like expression'),(9187,2,454,29120,'here was a face with flashing eyes and distorted features a face convulsed with hatred and with the mad joy of gratified revenge'),(9188,2,22823,143090,'lord roxton'),(9189,2,258,142149,'he shouted'),(9190,2,22823,143090,'lord john roxton'),(9191,2,43,1711,'well said our companion here i am'),(9192,2,23,159704,'a shriek of laughter came across the abyss'),(9193,2,9487,12784,'yes there you are you english dog and there you will remain'),(9194,2,279,6023,'i have waited and waited and now has come my chance'),(9195,2,2074,4332,'you found it hard to get up you will find it harder to get down'),(9196,2,2074,2074,'you cursed fools you are trapped every one of you'),(9197,2,1649,4634,'we were too astounded to speak'),(9198,2,1649,32476,'we could only stand there staring in amazement'),(9199,2,23,80175,'a great broken bough upon the grass showed whence he had gained his leverage to tilt over our bridge'),(9200,2,51,509,'the face had vanished but presently it was up again more frantic than before'),(9201,2,1649,949,'we nearly killed you with a stone at the cave he cried but this is better'),(9202,2,67,130739,'it is slower and more terrible'),(9203,2,1373,112,'your bones will whiten up there and none will know where you lie or come to cover them'),(9204,2,42,4669,'as you lie dying think of lopez whom you shot five years ago on the putomayo river'),(9205,2,279,161696,'i am his brother and come what will i will die happy now for his memory has been avenged'),(9206,2,23,53992,'a furious hand was shaken at us and then all was quiet'),(9207,2,259,247,'had the half-breed simply wrought his vengeance and then escaped all might have been well with him'),(9208,2,67,161740,'it was that foolish irresistible latin impulse to be dramatic which brought his own downfall'),(9209,2,143090,161766,'roxton the man who had earned himself the name of the flail of the lord through three countries was not one who could be safely taunted'),(9210,2,51,1278,'the half-breed was descending on the farther side of the pinnacle but before he could reach the ground lord john had run along the edge of the plateau and gained a point from which he could see his man'),(9211,2,74,133885,'there was a single crack of his rifle and though we saw nothing we heard the scream and then the distant thud of the falling body'),(9212,2,143090,138233,'roxton came back to us with a face of granite'),(9213,2,279,22537,'i have been a blind simpleton said he bitterly it\'s my folly that has brought you all into this trouble'),(9214,2,279,10398,'i should have remembered that these people have long memories for blood-feuds and have been more upon my guard'),(9215,2,994,139,'what about the other one'),(9216,2,67,55120,'it took two of them to lever that tree over the edge'),(9217,2,279,351,'i could have shot him but i let him go'),(9218,2,258,67,'he may have had no part in it'),(9219,2,290,410,'perhaps it would have been better if i had killed him for he must as you say have lent a hand'),(9220,2,2530,12082,'now that we had the clue to his action each of us could cast back and remember some sinister act upon the part of the half-breed--his constant desire to know our plans his arrest outside our tent when he was over-hearing them the furtive looks of hatred which from time to time one or other of us had surprised'),(9221,2,1649,6970,'we were still discussing it endeavoring to adjust our minds to these new conditions when a singular scene in the plain below arrested our attention'),(9222,2,23,162043,'a man in white clothes who could only be the surviving half-breed was running as one does run when death is the pacemaker'),(9223,2,7459,148209,'behind him only a few yards in his rear bounded the huge ebony figure of zambo our devoted negro'),(9224,2,1232,156026,'even as we looked he sprang upon the back of the fugitive and flung his arms round his neck'),(9225,2,1056,10658,'they rolled on the ground together'),(9226,2,573,53540,'an instant afterwards zambo rose looked at the prostrate man and then waving his hand joyously to us came running in our direction'),(9227,2,51,11635,'the white figure lay motionless in the middle of the great plain'),(9228,2,2043,112,'our two traitors had been destroyed but the mischief that they had done lived after them'),(9229,2,3,137350,'by no possible means could we get back to the pinnacle'),(9230,2,1649,138041,'we had been natives of the world now we were natives of the plateau'),(9231,2,51,133284,'the two things were separate and apart'),(9232,2,74,150081,'there was the plain which led to the canoes'),(9233,2,162180,162193,'yonder beyond the violet hazy horizon was the stream which led back to civilization'),(9234,2,295,70053,'but the link between was missing'),(9235,2,655,13329,'no human ingenuity could suggest a means of bridging the chasm which yawned between ourselves and our past lives'),(9236,2,139,30136,'one instant had altered the whole conditions of our existence'),(9237,2,67,35667,'it was at such a moment that i learned the stuff of which my three comrades were composed'),(9238,2,1056,63062,'they were grave it is true and thoughtful but of an invincible serenity'),(9239,2,284,148211,'for the moment we could only sit among the bushes in patience and wait the coming of zambo'),(9240,2,728,137350,'presently his honest black face topped the rocks and his herculean figure emerged upon the top of the pinnacle'),(9241,2,994,2530,'what i do now'),(9242,2,258,4916,'he cried'),(9243,2,2074,67,'you tell me and i do it'),(9244,2,67,4905,'it was a question which it was easier to ask than to answer'),(9245,2,139,11120,'one thing only was clear'),(9246,2,258,378,'he was our one trusty link with the outside world'),(9247,2,407,2793,'on no account must he leave us'),(9248,2,655,655,'no no'),(9249,2,258,4916,'he cried'),(9250,2,279,2074,'i not leave you'),(9251,2,15005,454,'whatever come you always find me here'),(9252,2,295,134990,'but no able to keep indians'),(9253,2,2915,8866,'already they say too much curupuri live on this place and they go home'),(9254,2,2530,112,'now you leave them me no able to keep them'),(9255,2,67,6135,'it was a fact that our indians had shown in many ways of late that they were weary of their journey and anxious to return'),(9256,2,1649,112,'we realized that zambo spoke the truth and that it would be impossible for him to keep them'),(9257,2,1665,112,'make them wait till to-morrow zambo i shouted then i can send letter back by them'),(9258,2,56,162451,'very good sarr'),(9259,2,279,148209,'i promise they wait till to-morrow said the negro'),(9260,2,295,2530,'but what i do for you now'),(9261,2,74,67,'there was plenty for him to do and admirably the faithful fellow did it'),(9262,2,183,2793,'first of all under our directions he undid the rope from the tree-stump and threw one end of it across to us'),(9263,2,67,513,'it was not thicker than a clothes-line but it was of great strength and though we could not make a bridge of it we might well find it invaluable if we had any climbing to do'),(9264,2,258,26307,'he then fastened his end of the rope to the package of supplies which had been carried up and we were able to drag it across'),(9265,2,37,2224,'this gave us the means of life for at least a week even if we found nothing else'),(9266,2,27627,1886,'finally he descended and carried up two other packets of mixed goods--a box of ammunition and a number of other things all of which we got across by throwing our rope to him and hauling it back'),(9267,2,67,6169,'it was evening when he at last climbed down with a final assurance that he would keep the indians till next morning'),(9268,2,1,162672,'and so it is that i have spent nearly the whole of this our first night upon the plateau writing up our experiences by the light of a single candle-lantern'),(9269,2,1649,5221,'we supped and camped at the very edge of the cliff quenching our thirst with two bottles of apollinaris which were in one of the cases'),(9270,2,67,12850,'it is vital to us to find water but i think even lord john himself had had adventures enough for one day and none of us felt inclined to make the first push into the unknown'),(9271,2,1649,11034,'we forbore to light a fire or to make any unnecessary sound'),(9272,2,5906,118264,'to-morrow or to-day rather for it is already dawn as i write we shall make our first venture into this strange land'),(9273,2,483,55,'when i shall be able to write again--or if i ever shall write again--i know not'),(9274,2,39737,1317,'meanwhile i can see that the indians are still in their place and i am sure that the faithful zambo will be here presently to get my letter'),(9275,2,279,410,'i only trust that it will come to hand'),(9276,2,116361,867,'--the more i think the more desperate does our position seem'),(9277,2,279,6135,'i see no possible hope of our return'),(9278,2,257,81979,'if there were a high tree near the edge of the plateau we might drop a return bridge across but there is none within fifty yards'),(9279,2,2043,9635,'our united strength could not carry a trunk which would serve our purpose'),(9280,2,51,67,'the rope of course is far too short that we could descend by it'),(9281,2,655,162898,'no our position is hopeless--hopeless'),(9282,2,4328,2793,'chapter x the most wonderful things have happened the most wonderful things have happened and are continually happening to us'),(9283,2,172,2793,'all the paper that i possess consists of five old note-books and a lot of scraps and i have only the one stylographic pencil but so long as i can move my hand i will continue to set down our experiences and impressions for since we are the only men of the whole human race to see such things it is of enormous importance that i should record them whilst they are fresh in my memory and before that fate which seems to be constantly impending does actually overtake us'),(9284,2,516,15068,'whether zambo can at last take these letters to the river or whether i shall myself in some miraculous way carry them back with me or finally whether some daring explorer coming upon our tracks with the advantage perhaps of a perfected monoplane should find this bundle of manuscript in any case i can see that what i am writing is destined to immortality as a classic of true adventure'),(9285,2,407,127940,'on the morning after our being trapped upon the plateau by the villainous gomez we began a new stage in our experiences'),(9286,2,51,33351,'the first incident in it was not such as to give me a very favorable opinion of the place to which we had wandered'),(9287,2,42,132818,'as i roused myself from a short nap after day had dawned my eyes fell upon a most singular appearance upon my own leg'),(9288,2,413,163163,'my trouser had slipped up exposing a few inches of my skin above my sock'),(9289,2,407,163171,'on this there rested a large purplish grape'),(9290,2,12976,53540,'astonished at the sight i leaned forward to pick it off when to my horror it burst between my finger and thumb squirting blood in every direction'),(9291,2,413,1473,'my cry of disgust had brought the two professors to my side'),(9292,2,387,163218,'most interesting said summerlee bending over my shin'),(9293,2,573,163226,'an enormous blood-tick as yet i believe unclassified'),(9294,2,51,652,'the first-fruits of our labors said challenger in his booming pedantic fashion'),(9295,2,1649,163247,'we cannot do less than call it ixodes maloni'),(9296,2,51,134942,'the very small inconvenience of being bitten my young friend cannot i am sure weigh with you as against the glorious privilege of having your name inscribed in the deathless roll of zoology'),(9297,2,24913,163292,'unhappily you have crushed this fine specimen at the moment of satiation'),(9298,2,163293,132829,'filthy vermin'),(9299,2,279,4916,'i cried'),(9300,2,128875,149005,'professor challenger raised his great eyebrows in protest and placed a soothing paw upon my shoulder'),(9301,2,2074,258,'you should cultivate the scientific eye and the detached scientific mind said he'),(9302,2,40,163360,'to a man of philosophic temperament like myself the blood-tick with its lancet-like proboscis and its distending stomach is as beautiful a work of nature as the peacock or for that matter the aurora borealis'),(9303,2,67,652,'it pains me to hear you speak of it in so unappreciative a fashion'),(9304,2,655,136282,'no doubt with due diligence we can secure some other specimen'),(9305,2,74,163403,'there can be no doubt of that said summerlee grimly for one has just disappeared behind your shirt-collar'),(9306,2,128876,891,'challenger sprang into the air bellowing like a bull and tore frantically at his coat and shirt to get them off'),(9307,2,142538,247,'summerlee and i laughed so that we could hardly help him'),(9308,2,308,163448,'at last we exposed that monstrous torso fifty-four inches by the tailor\'s tape'),(9309,2,892,247,'his body was all matted with black hair out of which jungle we picked the wandering tick before it had bitten him'),(9310,2,295,77102,'but the bushes round were full of the horrible pests and it was clear that we must shift our camp'),(9311,2,295,2793,'but first of all it was necessary to make our arrangements with the faithful negro who appeared presently on the pinnacle with a number of tins of cocoa and biscuits which he tossed over to us'),(9312,2,53,36932,'of the stores which remained below he was ordered to retain as much as would keep him for two months'),(9313,2,51,134889,'the indians were to have the remainder as a reward for their services and as payment for taking our letters back to the amazon'),(9314,2,144,3479,'some hours later we saw them in single file far out upon the plain each with a bundle on his head making their way back along the path we had come'),(9315,2,148211,15363,'zambo occupied our little tent at the base of the pinnacle and there he remained our one link with the world below'),(9316,2,1,149402,'and now we had to decide upon our immediate movements'),(9317,2,1649,68646,'we shifted our position from among the tick-laden bushes until we came to a small clearing thickly surrounded by trees upon all sides'),(9318,2,74,1878,'there were some flat slabs of rock in the center with an excellent well close by and there we sat in cleanly comfort while we made our first plans for the invasion of this new country'),(9319,2,103882,534,'birds were calling among the foliage--especially one with a peculiar whooping cry which was new to us--but beyond these sounds there were no signs of life'),(9320,2,2043,787,'our first care was to make some sort of list of our own stores so that we might know what we had to rely upon'),(9321,2,994,12764,'what with the things we had ourselves brought up and those which zambo had sent across on the rope we were fairly well supplied'),(9322,2,387,135318,'most important of all in view of the dangers which might surround us we had our four rifles and one thousand three hundred rounds also a shot-gun but not more than a hundred and fifty medium pellet cartridges'),(9323,2,50,163836,'in the matter of provisions we had enough to last for several weeks with a sufficiency of tobacco and a few scientific implements including a large telescope and a good field-glass'),(9324,2,172,163875,'all these things we collected together in the clearing and as a first precaution we cut down with our hatchet and knives a number of thorny bushes which we piled round in a circle some fifteen yards in diameter'),(9325,2,37,137978,'this was to be our headquarters for the time--our place of refuge against sudden danger and the guard-house for our stores'),(9326,2,163897,67,'fort challenger we called it'),(9327,2,67,163934,'it was midday before we had made ourselves secure but the heat was not oppressive and the general character of the plateau both in its temperature and in its vegetation was almost temperate'),(9328,2,51,50,'the beech the oak and even the birch were to be found among the tangle of trees which girt us in'),(9329,2,139,163977,'one huge gingko tree topping all the others shot its great limbs and maidenhair foliage over the fort which we had constructed'),(9330,2,50,4814,'in its shade we continued our discussion while lord john who had quickly taken command in the hour of action gave us his views'),(9331,2,1051,258,'so long as neither man nor beast has seen or heard us we are safe said he'),(9332,2,344,10508,'from the time they know we are here our troubles begin'),(9333,2,74,119,'there are no signs that they have found us out as yet'),(9334,2,1051,118264,'so our game surely is to lie low for a time and spy out the land'),(9335,2,1649,23264,'we want to have a good look at our neighbors before we get on visitin\' terms'),(9336,2,295,3198,'but we must advance i ventured to remark'),(9337,2,3,10170,'by all means sonny my boy'),(9338,2,1649,10981,'we will advance'),(9339,2,295,286,'but with common sense'),(9340,2,1649,138116,'we must never go so far that we can\'t get back to our base'),(9341,2,203,159396,'above all we must never unless it is life or death fire off our guns'),(9342,2,295,142538,'but you fired yesterday said summerlee'),(9343,2,43,128305,'well it couldn\'t be helped'),(9344,2,303,155113,'however the wind was strong and blew outwards'),(9345,2,67,138041,'it is not likely that the sound could have traveled far into the plateau'),(9346,2,3,461,'by the way what shall we call this place'),(9347,2,279,5031,'i suppose it is up to us to give it a name'),(9348,2,74,3776,'there were several suggestions more or less happy but challenger\'s was final'),(9349,2,67,258,'it can only have one name said he'),(9350,2,67,67,'it is called after the pioneer who discovered it'),(9351,2,67,118264,'it is maple white land'),(9352,2,155915,159841,'maple white land it became and so it is named in that chart which has become my special task'),(9353,2,1051,24280,'so it will i trust appear in the atlas of the future'),(9354,2,51,2793,'the peaceful penetration of maple white land was the pressing subject before us'),(9355,2,1649,853,'we had the evidence of our own eyes that the place was inhabited by some unknown creatures and there was that of maple white\'s sketch-book to show that more dreadful and more dangerous monsters might still appear'),(9356,2,163,203,'that there might also prove to be human occupants and that they were of a malevolent character was suggested by the skeleton impaled upon the bamboos which could not have got there had it not been dropped from above'),(9357,2,2043,24662,'our situation stranded without possibility of escape in such a land was clearly full of danger and our reasons endorsed every measure of caution which lord john\'s experience could suggest'),(9358,2,119,67,'yet it was surely impossible that we should halt on the edge of this world of mystery when our very souls were tingling with impatience to push forward and to pluck the heart from it'),(9359,2,1649,53095,'we therefore blocked the entrance to our zareba by filling it up with several thorny bushes and left our camp with the stores entirely surrounded by this protecting hedge'),(9360,2,1649,6135,'we then slowly and cautiously set forth into the unknown following the course of the little stream which flowed from our spring as it should always serve us as a guide on our return'),(9361,2,569,2793,'hardly had we started when we came across signs that there were indeed wonders awaiting us'),(9362,2,171,158862,'after a few hundred yards of thick forest containing many trees which were quite unknown to me but which summerlee who was the botanist of the party recognized as forms of conifera and of cycadaceous plants which have long passed away in the world below we entered a region where the stream widened out and formed a considerable bog'),(9363,2,5476,54262,'high reeds of a peculiar type grew thickly before us which were pronounced to be equisetacea or mare\'s-tails with tree-ferns scattered amongst them all of them swaying in a brisk wind'),(9364,2,5725,410,'suddenly lord john who was walking first halted with uplifted hand'),(9365,2,2046,37,'look at this'),(9366,2,2257,258,'said he'),(9367,2,3,103882,'by george this must be the trail of the father of all birds'),(9368,2,573,2793,'an enormous three-toed track was imprinted in the soft mud before us'),(9369,2,51,145548,'the creature whatever it was had crossed the swamp and had passed on into the forest'),(9370,2,1649,164630,'we all stopped to examine that monstrous spoor'),(9371,2,257,43671,'if it were indeed a bird--and what animal could leave such a mark'),(9372,2,164644,132010,'--its foot was so much larger than an ostrich\'s that its height upon the same scale must be enormous'),(9373,2,22823,164675,'lord john looked eagerly round him and slipped two cartridges into his elephant-gun'),(9374,2,127845,139,'i\'ll stake my good name as a shikarree said he that the track is a fresh one'),(9375,2,51,6645,'the creature has not passed ten minutes'),(9376,2,2046,138939,'look how the water is still oozing into that deeper print'),(9377,2,3,164712,'by jove'),(9378,2,4334,139,'see here is the mark of a little one'),(9379,2,1763,3973,'sure enough smaller tracks of the same general form were running parallel to the large ones'),(9380,2,295,37,'but what do you make of this'),(9381,2,4916,24824,'cried professor summerlee triumphantly pointing to what looked like the huge print of a five-fingered human hand appearing among the three-toed marks'),(9382,2,4916,32261,'cried challenger in an ecstasy'),(9383,2,127793,164778,'i\'ve seen them in the wealden clay'),(9384,2,67,40255,'it is a creature walking erect upon three-toed feet and occasionally putting one of its five-fingered forepaws upon the ground'),(9385,2,55,137407,'not a bird my dear roxton--not a bird'),(9386,2,23,132311,'a beast'),(9387,2,655,136417,'no a reptile--a dinosaur'),(9388,2,1155,145590,'nothing else could have left such a track'),(9389,2,1056,163,'they puzzled a worthy sussex doctor some ninety years ago but who in the world could have hoped--hoped--to have seen a sight like that'),(9390,2,892,32476,'his words died away into a whisper and we all stood in motionless amazement'),(9391,2,5713,53546,'following the tracks we had left the morass and passed through a screen of brushwood and trees'),(9392,2,1653,373,'beyond was an open glade and in this were five of the most extraordinary creatures that i have ever seen'),(9393,2,164896,10708,'crouching down among the bushes we observed them at our leisure'),(9394,2,74,3973,'there were as i say five of them two being adults and three young ones'),(9395,2,50,132010,'in size they were enormous'),(9396,2,1232,373,'even the babies were as big as elephants while the two large ones were far beyond all creatures i have ever seen'),(9397,2,1056,67,'they had slate-colored skin which was scaled like a lizard\'s and shimmered where the sun shone upon it'),(9398,2,172,164998,'all five were sitting up balancing themselves upon their broad powerful tails and their huge three-toed hind-feet while with their small five-fingered front-feet they pulled down the branches upon which they browsed'),(9399,2,279,135622,'i do not know that i can bring their appearance home to you better than by saying that they looked like monstrous kangaroos twenty feet in length and with skins like black crocodiles'),(9400,2,279,165045,'i do not know how long we stayed motionless gazing at this marvelous spectacle'),(9401,2,23,11156,'a strong wind blew towards us and we were well concealed so there was no chance of discovery'),(9402,2,344,116822,'from time to time the little ones played round their parents in unwieldy gambols the great beasts bounding into the air and falling with dull thuds upon the earth'),(9403,2,51,165137,'the strength of the parents seemed to be limitless for one of them having some difficulty in reaching a bunch of foliage which grew upon a considerable-sized tree put his fore-legs round the trunk and tore it down as if it had been a sapling'),(9404,2,51,102726,'the action seemed as i thought to show not only the great development of its muscles but also the small one of its brain for the whole weight came crashing down upon the top of it and it uttered a series of shrill yelps to show that big as it was there was a limit to what it could endure'),(9405,2,51,165225,'the incident made it think apparently that the neighborhood was dangerous for it slowly lurched off through the wood followed by its mate and its three enormous infants'),(9406,2,1649,165245,'we saw the shimmering slaty gleam of their skins between the tree-trunks and their heads undulating high above the brush-wood'),(9407,2,1502,6660,'then they vanished from our sight'),(9408,2,279,147006,'i looked at my comrades'),(9409,2,22823,917,'lord john was standing at gaze with his finger on the trigger of his elephant-gun his eager hunter\'s soul shining from his fierce eyes'),(9410,2,994,143356,'what would he not give for one such head to place between the two crossed oars above the mantelpiece in his snuggery at the albany'),(9411,2,1,7722,'and yet his reason held him in for all our exploration of the wonders of this unknown land depended upon our presence being concealed from its inhabitants'),(9412,2,51,32261,'the two professors were in silent ecstasy'),(9413,2,50,134235,'in their excitement they had unconsciously seized each other by the hand and stood like two little children in the presence of a marvel challenger\'s cheeks bunched up into a seraphic smile and 