bscurity one learned that there were different degrees of darkness among the trees--that some were dimly visible while between and among them there were coal-black shadowed patches like the mouths of caves from which i shrank in horror as i passed'),(9874,2,279,79733,'i thought of the despairing yell of the tortured iguanodon--that dreadful cry which had echoed through the woods'),(9875,2,279,174119,'i thought too of the glimpse i had in the light of lord john\'s torch of that bloated warty blood-slavering muzzle'),(9876,2,1232,174126,'even now i was on its hunting-ground'),(9877,2,308,137770,'at any instant it might spring upon me from the shadows--this nameless and horrible monster'),(9878,2,279,144882,'i stopped and picking a cartridge from my pocket i opened the breech of my gun'),(9879,2,42,424,'as i touched the lever my heart leaped within me'),(9880,2,67,255,'it was the shot-gun not the rifle which i had taken'),(9881,2,3373,424,'again the impulse to return swept over me'),(9882,2,454,424,'here surely was a most excellent reason for my failure--one for which no one would think the less of me'),(9883,2,295,1426,'but again the foolish pride fought against that very word'),(9884,2,279,174220,'i could not--must not--fail'),(9885,2,171,5768,'after all my rifle would probably have been as useless as a shot-gun against such dangers as i might meet'),(9886,2,257,373,'if i were to go back to camp to change my weapon i could hardly expect to enter and to leave again without being seen'),(9887,2,50,764,'in that case there would be explanations and my attempt would no longer be all my own'),(9888,2,171,20290,'after a little hesitation then i screwed up my courage and continued upon my way my useless gun under my arm'),(9889,2,51,165480,'the darkness of the forest had been alarming but even worse was the white still flood of moonlight in the open glade of the iguanodons'),(9890,2,150529,67,'hid among the bushes i looked out at it'),(9891,2,2076,6660,'none of the great brutes were in sight'),(9892,2,290,174360,'perhaps the tragedy which had befallen one of them had driven them from their feeding-ground'),(9893,2,50,109,'in the misty silvery night i could see no sign of any living thing'),(9894,2,3905,34837,'taking courage therefore i slipped rapidly across it and among the jungle on the farther side i picked up once again the brook which was my guide'),(9895,2,67,174430,'it was a cheery companion gurgling and chuckling as it ran like the dear old trout-stream in the west country where i have fished at night in my boyhood'),(9896,2,1051,77102,'so long as i followed it down i must come to the lake and so long as i followed it back i must come to the camp'),(9897,2,4470,152832,'often i had to lose sight of it on account of the tangled brush-wood but i was always within earshot of its tinkle and splash'),(9898,2,42,145548,'as one descended the slope the woods became thinner and bushes with occasional high trees took the place of the forest'),(9899,2,279,373,'i could make good progress therefore and i could see without being seen'),(9900,2,279,6950,'i passed close to the pterodactyl swamp and as i did so with a dry crisp leathery rattle of wings one of these great creatures--it was twenty feet at least from tip to tip--rose up from somewhere near me and soared into the air'),(9901,2,42,143452,'as it passed across the face of the moon the light shone clearly through the membranous wings and it looked like a flying skeleton against the white tropical radiance'),(9902,2,279,132843,'i crouched low among the bushes for i knew from past experience that with a single cry the creature could bring a hundred of its loathsome mates about my ears'),(9903,2,67,1965,'it was not until it had settled again that i dared to steal onwards upon my journey'),(9904,2,51,424,'the night had been exceedingly still but as i advanced i became conscious of a low rumbling sound a continuous murmur somewhere in front of me'),(9905,2,37,424,'this grew louder as i proceeded until at last it was clearly quite close to me'),(9906,2,483,457,'when i stood still the sound was constant so that it seemed to come from some stationary cause'),(9907,2,67,142404,'it was like a boiling kettle or the bubbling of some great pot'),(9908,2,5131,140480,'soon i came upon the source of it for in the center of a small clearing i found a lake--or a pool rather for it was not larger than the basin of the trafalgar square fountain--of some black pitch-like stuff the surface of which rose and fell in great blisters of bursting gas'),(9909,2,51,67,'the air above it was shimmering with heat and the ground round was so hot that i could hardly bear to lay my hand on it'),(9910,2,67,140651,'it was clear that the great volcanic outburst which had raised this strange plateau so many years ago had not yet entirely spent its forces'),(9911,2,133428,159217,'blackened rocks and mounds of lava i had already seen everywhere peeping out from amid the luxuriant vegetation which draped them but this asphalt pool in the jungle was the first sign that we had of actual existing activity on the slopes of the ancient crater'),(9912,2,279,6169,'i had no time to examine it further for i had need to hurry if i were to be back in camp in the morning'),(9913,2,67,174901,'it was a fearsome walk and one which will be with me so long as memory holds'),(9914,2,50,129974,'in the great moonlight clearings i slunk along among the shadows on the margin'),(9915,2,50,63024,'in the jungle i crept forward stopping with a beating heart whenever i heard as i often did the crash of breaking branches as some wild beast went past'),(9916,2,2530,42747,'now and then great shadows loomed up for an instant and were gone--great silent shadows which seemed to prowl upon padded feet'),(9917,2,1338,63835,'how often i stopped with the intention of returning and yet every time my pride conquered my fear and sent me on again until my object should be attained'),(9918,2,308,135185,'at last my watch showed that it was one in the morning i saw the gleam of water amid the openings of the jungle and ten minutes later i was among the reeds upon the borders of the central lake'),(9919,2,279,1905,'i was exceedingly dry so i lay down and took a long draught of its waters which were fresh and cold'),(9920,2,74,130110,'there was a broad pathway with many tracks upon it at the spot which i had found so that it was clearly one of the drinking-places of the animals'),(9921,2,9704,174819,'close to the water\'s edge there was a huge isolated block of lava'),(9922,2,1274,53540,'up this i climbed and lying on the top i had an excellent view in every direction'),(9923,2,51,32476,'the first thing which i saw filled me with amazement'),(9924,2,483,172604,'when i described the view from the summit of the great tree i said that on the farther cliff i could see a number of dark spots which appeared to be the mouths of caves'),(9925,2,2530,145505,'now as i looked up at the same cliffs i saw discs of light in every direction ruddy clearly-defined patches like the port-holes of a liner in the darkness'),(9926,2,284,1051,'for a moment i thought it was the lava-glow from some volcanic action but this could not be so'),(9927,2,345,52871,'any volcanic action would surely be down in the hollow and not high among the rocks'),(9928,2,994,13859,'what then was the alternative'),(9929,2,67,104,'it was wonderful and yet it must surely be'),(9930,2,2457,1278,'these ruddy spots must be the reflection of fires within the caves--fires which could only be lit by the hand of man'),(9931,2,74,138041,'there were human beings then upon the plateau'),(9932,2,1338,26814,'how gloriously my expedition was justified'),(9933,2,454,6833,'here was news indeed for us to bear back with us to london'),(9934,2,284,11189,'for a long time i lay and watched these red quivering blotches of light'),(9935,2,279,112,'i suppose they were ten miles off from me yet even at that distance one could observe how from time to time they twinkled or were obscured as someone passed before them'),(9936,2,994,461,'what would i not have given to be able to crawl up to them to peep in and to take back some word to my comrades as to the appearance and character of the race who lived in so strange a place'),(9937,2,67,543,'it was out of the question for the moment and yet surely we could not leave the plateau until we had some definite knowledge upon the point'),(9938,2,135185,67,'lake gladys--my own lake--lay like a sheet of quicksilver before me with a reflected moon shining brightly in the center of it'),(9939,2,67,82524,'it was shallow for in many places i saw low sandbanks protruding above the water'),(9940,2,8458,137770,'everywhere upon the still surface i could see signs of life sometimes mere rings and ripples in the water sometimes the gleam of a great silver-sided fish in the air sometimes the arched slate-colored back of some passing monster'),(9941,2,855,129974,'once upon a yellow sandbank i saw a creature like a huge swan with a clumsy body and a high flexible neck shuffling about upon the margin'),(9942,2,728,82524,'presently it plunged in and for some time i could see the arched neck and darting head undulating over the water'),(9943,2,1502,77,'then it dived and i saw it no more'),(9944,2,413,42747,'my attention was soon drawn away from these distant sights and brought back to what was going on at my very feet'),(9945,2,1024,175591,'two creatures like large armadillos had come down to the drinking-place and were squatting at the edge of the water their long flexible tongues like red ribbons shooting in and out as they lapped'),(9946,2,23,175562,'a huge deer with branching horns a magnificent creature which carried itself like a king came down with its doe and two fawns and drank beside the armadillos'),(9947,2,655,125325,'no such deer exist anywhere else upon earth for the moose or elks which i have seen would hardly have reached its shoulders'),(9948,2,728,148072,'presently it gave a warning snort and was off with its family among the reeds while the armadillos also scuttled for shelter'),(9949,2,23,20302,'a new-comer a most monstrous animal was coming down the path'),(9950,2,284,40255,'for a moment i wondered where i could have seen that ungainly shape that arched back with triangular fringes along it that strange bird-like head held close to the ground'),(9951,2,1502,424,'then it came back to me'),(9952,2,67,128876,'it was the stegosaurus--the very creature which maple white had preserved in his sketch-book and which had been the first object which arrested the attention of challenger'),(9953,2,74,171793,'there he was--perhaps the very specimen which the american artist had encountered'),(9954,2,51,9700,'the ground shook beneath his tremendous weight and his gulpings of water resounded through the still night'),(9955,2,284,1886,'for five minutes he was so close to my rock that by stretching out my hand i could have touched the hideous waving hackles upon his back'),(9956,2,1502,165999,'then he lumbered away and was lost among the boulders'),(9957,2,4586,1965,'looking at my watch i saw that it was half-past two o\'clock and high time therefore that i started upon my homeward journey'),(9958,2,74,15470,'there was no difficulty about the direction in which i should return for all along i had kept the little brook upon my left and it opened into the central lake within a stone\'s-throw of the boulder upon which i had been lying'),(9959,2,279,47604,'i set off therefore in high spirits for i felt that i had done good work and was bringing back a fine budget of news for my companions'),(9960,2,175899,112,'foremost of all of course were the sight of the fiery caves and the certainty that some troglodytic race inhabited them'),(9961,2,295,135185,'but besides that i could speak from experience of the central lake'),(9962,2,279,171793,'i could testify that it was full of strange creatures and i had seen several land forms of primeval life which we had not before e