ing cubs and the moon shone into the mouth of the cave where they all lived'),(11396,3,2257,202228,'said father wolf'),(11397,3,67,3373,'it is time to hunt again'),(11398,3,258,202324,'he was going to spring down hill when a little shadow with a bushy tail crossed the threshold and whined good luck go with you o chief of the wolves'),(11399,3,1,378,'and good luck and strong white teeth go with noble children that they may never forget the hungry in this world'),(11400,3,67,202377,'it was the jackal--tabaqui the dish-licker--and the wolves of india despise tabaqui because he runs about making mischief and telling tales and eating rags and pieces of leather from the village rubbish-heaps'),(11401,3,295,608,'but they are afraid of him too because tabaqui more than anyone else in the jungle is apt to go mad and then he forgets that he was ever afraid of anyone and runs through the forest biting everything in his way'),(11402,3,1232,7445,'even the tiger runs and hides when little tabaqui goes mad for madness is the most disgraceful thing that can overtake a wild creature'),(11403,3,1649,17034,'we call it hydrophobia but they call it dewanee--the madness--and run'),(11404,3,28576,454,'enter then and look said father wolf stiffly but there is no food here'),(11405,3,284,202489,'for a wolf no said tabaqui but for so mean a person as myself a dry bone is a good feast'),(11406,3,59,4020,'who are we the gidur-log the jackal people to pick and choose'),(11407,3,258,202528,'he scuttled to the back of the cave where he found the bone of a buck with some meat on it and sat cracking the end merrily'),(11408,3,172,4375,'all thanks for this good meal he said licking his lips'),(11409,3,1338,5449,'how beautiful are the noble children'),(11410,3,1338,917,'how large are their eyes'),(11411,3,1,813,'and so young too'),(11412,3,330,25822,'indeed indeed i might have remembered that the children of kings are men from the beginning'),(11413,3,2530,93284,'now tabaqui knew as well as anyone else that there is nothing so unlucky as to compliment children to their faces'),(11414,3,67,16418,'it pleased him to see mother and father wolf look uncomfortable'),(11415,3,202357,3451,'tabaqui sat still rejoicing in the mischief that he had made and then he said spitefully shere khan the big one has shifted his hunting grounds'),(11416,3,258,424,'he will hunt among these hills for the next moon so he has told me'),(11417,3,202619,4025,'shere khan was the tiger who lived near the waingunga river twenty miles away'),(11418,3,258,106,'he has no right'),(11419,3,5882,133466,'father wolf began angrily--by the law of the jungle he has no right to change his quarters without due warning'),(11420,3,258,1390,'he will frighten every head of game within ten miles and i--i have to kill for two these days'),(11421,3,892,37275,'his mother did not call him lungri the lame one for nothing said mother wolf quietly'),(11422,3,258,2550,'he has been lame in one foot from his birth'),(11423,3,163,154091,'that is why he has only killed cattle'),(11424,3,2530,7118,'now the villagers of the waingunga are angry with him and he has come here to make our villagers angry'),(11425,3,1056,202779,'they will scour the jungle for him when he is far away and we and our children must run when the grass is set alight'),(11426,3,330,202620,'indeed we are very grateful to shere khan'),(11427,3,5767,10488,'shall i tell him of your gratitude'),(11428,3,2257,202357,'said tabaqui'),(11429,3,133469,202228,'snapped father wolf'),(11430,3,976,22259,'out and hunt with thy master'),(11431,3,202806,9700,'thou hast done harm enough for one night'),(11432,3,279,37275,'i go said tabaqui quietly'),(11433,3,202819,202827,'ye can hear shere khan below in the thickets'),(11434,3,279,4618,'i might have saved myself the message'),(11435,3,5882,67,'father wolf listened and below in the valley that ran down to a little river he heard the dry angry snarly singsong whine of a tiger who has caught nothing and does not care if all the jungle knows it'),(11436,3,51,1276,'the fool'),(11437,3,2257,202228,'said father wolf'),(11438,3,40,30389,'to begin a nights work with that noise'),(11439,3,297,202899,'does he think that our buck are like his fat waingunga bullocks'),(11440,3,67,202228,'it is neither bullock nor buck he hunts to-night said mother wolf'),(11441,3,67,1278,'it is man'),(11442,3,51,20090,'the whine had changed to a sort of humming purr that seemed to come from every quarter of the compass'),(11443,3,67,169458,'it was the noise that bewilders woodcutters and gypsies sleeping in the open and makes them run sometimes into the very mouth of the tiger'),(11444,3,2257,88510,'said father wolf showing all his white teeth'),(11445,3,60,813,'are there not enough beetles and frogs in the tanks that he must eat man and on our ground too'),(11446,3,51,148398,'the law of the jungle which never orders anything without a reason forbids every beast to eat man except when he is killing to show his children how to kill and then he must hunt outside the hunting grounds of his pack or tribe'),(11447,3,51,194838,'the real reason for this is that man-killing means sooner or later the arrival of white men on elephants with guns and hundreds of brown men with gongs and rockets and torches'),(11448,3,1502,16819,'then everybody in the jungle suffers'),(11449,3,51,247,'the reason the beasts give among themselves is that man is the weakest and most defenseless of all living things and it is unsportsmanlike to touch him'),(11450,3,1056,88510,'they say too--and it is true--that man-eaters become mangy and lose their teeth'),(11451,3,51,203119,'the purr grew louder and ended in the full-throated aaarh'),(11452,3,53,347,'of the tigers charge'),(11453,3,1502,202620,'then there was a howl--an untigerish howl--from shere khan'),(11454,3,258,202228,'he has missed said mother wolf'),(11455,3,994,67,'what is it'),(11456,3,5882,179672,'father wolf ran out a few paces and heard shere khan muttering and mumbling savagely as he tumbled about in the scrub'),(11457,3,51,132287,'the fool has had no more sense than to jump at a woodcutters campfire and has burned his feet said father wolf with a grunt'),(11458,3,202357,247,'tabaqui is with him'),(11459,3,707,18039,'something is coming uphill said mother wolf twitching one ear'),(11460,3,2075,12987,'get ready'),(11461,3,51,203225,'the bushes rustled a little in the thicket and father wolf dropped with his haunches under him ready for his leap'),(11462,3,1502,203246,'then if you had been watching you would have seen the most wonderful thing in the world--the wolf checked in mid-spring'),(11463,3,258,852,'he made his bound before he saw what it was he was jumping at and then he tried to stop himself'),(11464,3,51,40255,'the result was that he shot up straight into the air for four or five feet landing almost where he left ground'),(11465,3,258,133469,'he snapped'),(11466,3,23,203294,'a mans cub'),(11467,3,10168,9700,'directly in front of him holding on by a low branch stood a naked brown baby who could just walk--as soft and as dimpled a little atom as ever came to a wolfs cave at night'),(11468,3,258,15178,'he looked up into father wolfs face and laughed'),(11469,3,75,203294,'is that a mans cub'),(11470,3,2257,202228,'said mother wolf'),(11471,3,279,139,'i have never seen one'),(11472,3,6853,454,'bring it here'),(11473,3,23,202271,'a wolf accustomed to moving his own cubs can if necessary mouth an egg without breaking it and though father wolfs jaws closed right on the childs back not a tooth even scratched the skin as he laid it down among the cubs'),(11474,3,1338,262,'how little'),(11475,3,1338,106446,'how naked and--how bold'),(11476,3,2257,173697,'said mother wolf softly'),(11477,3,51,14917,'the baby was pushing his way between the cubs to get close to the warm hide'),(11478,3,258,90,'he is taking his meal with the others'),(11479,3,1,203294,'and so this is a mans cub'),(11480,3,2530,5449,'now was there ever a wolf that could boast of a mans cub among her children'),(11481,3,279,202228,'i have heard now and again of such a thing but never in our pack or in my time said father wolf'),(11482,3,258,104164,'he is altogether without hair and i could kill him with a touch of my foot'),(11483,3,295,6416,'but see he looks up and is not afraid'),(11484,3,51,6996,'the moonlight was blocked out of the mouth of the cave for shere khans great square head and shoulders were thrust into the entrance'),(11485,3,202357,454,'tabaqui behind him was squeaking my lord my lord it went in here'),(11486,3,202619,7118,'shere khan does us great honor said father wolf but his eyes were very angry'),(11487,3,994,1650,'what does shere khan need'),(11488,3,413,203561,'my quarry'),(11489,3,23,202620,'a mans cub went this way said shere khan'),(11490,3,153,891,'its parents have run off'),(11491,3,2786,424,'give it to me'),(11492,3,202619,42747,'shere khan had jumped at a woodcutters campfire as father wolf had said and was furious from the pain of his burned feet'),(11493,3,295,3,'but father wolf knew that the mouth of the cave was too narrow for a tiger to come in by'),(11494,3,1232,132122,'even where he was shere khans shoulders and forepaws were cramped for want of room as a mans would be if he tried to fight in a barrel'),(11495,3,51,202228,'the wolves are a free people said father wolf'),(11496,3,1056,203674,'they take orders from the head of the pack and not from any striped cattle-killer'),(11497,3,51,4020,'the mans cub is ours--to kill if we choose'),(11498,3,202819,4020,'ye choose and ye do not choose'),(11499,3,994,21254,'what talk is this of choosing'),(11500,3,3,203715,'by the bull that i killed am i to stand nosing into your dogs den for my fair dues'),(11501,3,67,4634,'it is i shere khan who speak'),(11502,3,51,187626,'the tigers roar filled the cave with thunder'),(11503,3,5751,202620,'mother wolf shook herself clear of the cubs and sprang forward her eyes like two green moons in the darkness facing the blazing eyes of shere khan'),(11504,3,1,74110,'and it is i raksha the demon who answers'),(11505,3,51,424,'the mans cub is mine lungri--mine to me'),(11506,3,258,93845,'he shall not be killed'),(11507,3,258,203807,'he shall live to run with the pack and to hunt with the pack and in the end look you hunter of little naked cubs--frog-eater--fish-killer--he shall hunt thee'),(11508,3,2530,378,'now get hence or by the sambhur that i killed i eat no starved cattle back thou goest to thy mother burned beast of the jungle lamer than ever thou camest into the world'),(11509,3,5882,19934,'father wolf looked on amazed'),(11510,3,258,5860,'he had almost forgotten the days when he won mother wolf in fair fight from five other wolves when she ran in the pack and was not called the demon for compliments sake'),(11511,3,202619,511,'shere khan might have faced father wolf but he could not stand up against mother wolf for he knew that where he was she had all the advantage of the ground and would fight to the death'),(11512,3,1051,203939,'so he backed out of the cave mouth growling and when he was clear he shouted each dog barks in his own yard'),(11513,3,1649,203952,'we will see what the pack will say to this fostering of man-cubs'),(11514,3,51,203969,'the cub is mine and to my teeth he will come in the end o bush-tailed thieves'),(11515,3,5751,4788,'mother wolf threw herself down panting among the cubs and father wolf said to her gravely shere khan speaks this much truth'),(11516,3,51,10128,'the cub must be shown to the pack'),(11517,3,204000,5751,'wilt thou still keep him mother'),(11518,3,5854,247,'keep him'),(11519,3,729,159717,'she gasped'),(11520,3,258,6416,'he came naked by night alone and very hungry yet he was not afraid'),(11521,3,2046,2915,'look he has pushed one of my babes to one side already'),(11522,3,1,29120,'and that lame butcher would have killed him and would have run off to the waingunga while the villagers here hunted through all our lairs in revenge'),(11523,3,5854,247,'keep him'),(11524,3,177557,247,'assuredly i will keep him'),(11525,3,3661,204073,'lie still little frog'),(11526,3,7994,203807,'o thou mowgli--for mowgli the frog i will call thee--the time will come when thou wilt hunt shere khan as he has hunted thee'),(11527,3,295,794,'but what will our pack say'),(11528,3,2257,202228,'said father wolf'),(11529,3,51,40,'the law of the jungle lays down very clearly that any wolf may when he marries withdraw from the pack he belongs to'),(11530,3,295,112,'but as soon as his cubs are old enough to stand on their feet he must bring them to the pack council which is generally held once a month at full moon in order that the other wolves may identify them'),(11531,3,171,112,'after that inspection the cubs are free to run where they please and until they have killed their first buck no excuse is accepted if a grown wolf of the pack kills one of them'),(11532,3,51,1051,'the punishment is death where the murderer can be found and if you think for a minute you will see that this must be so'),(11533,3,5882,14917,'father wolf waited till his cubs could run a little and then on the night of the pack meeting took them and mowgli and mother wolf to the council rock--a hilltop covered with stones and boulders where a hundred wolves could hide'),(11534,3,204273,575,'akela the great gray lone wolf who led all the pack by strength and cunning lay out at full length on his rock and below him sat forty or more wolves of every size and color from badger-colored veterans who could handle a buck alone to young black three-year-olds who thought they could'),(11535,3,51,2530,'the lone wolf had led them for a year now'),(11536,3,258,46,'he had fallen twice into a wolf trap in his youth and once he had been beaten and left for dead so he knew the manners and customs of men'),(11537,3,74,135748,'there was very little talking at the rock'),(11538,3,51,42747,'the cubs tumbled over each other in the center of the circle where their mothers and fathers sat and now and again a senior wolf would go quietly up to a cub look at him carefully and return to his place on noiseless feet'),(11539,3,841,23429,'sometimes a mother would push her cub far out into the moonlight to be sure that he had not been overlooked'),(11540,3,204273,28793,'akela from his rock would cry ye know the law--ye know the law'),(11541,3,2046,202324,'look well o wolves'),(11542,3,1,202324,'and the anxious mothers would take up the call look--look well o wolves'),(11543,3,308,174321,'at last--and mother wolfs neck bristles lifted as the time came--father wolf pushed mowgli the frog as they called him into the center where he sat laughing and playing with some pebbles that glistened in the moonlight'),(11544,3,204273,43,'akela never raised his head from his paws but went on with the monotonous cry look well'),(11545,3,23,9989,'a muffled roar came up from behind the rocks--the voice of shere khan crying the cub is mine'),(11546,3,2786,424,'give him to me'),(11547,3,994,203294,'what have the free people to do with a mans cub'),(11548,3,204273,132843,'akela never even twitched his ears'),(11549,3,172,202324,'all he said was look well o wolves'),(11550,3,994,7105,'what have the free people to do with the orders of any save the free people'),(11551,3,2046,43,'look well'),(11552,3,74,203294,'there was a chorus of deep growls and a young wolf in his fourth year flung back shere khans question to akela what have the free people to do with a mans cub'),(11553,3,2530,5751,'now the law of the jungle lays down that if there is any dispute as to the right of a cub to be accepted by the pack he must be spoken for by at least two members of the pack who are not his father and mother'),(11554,3,59,203294,'who speaks for this cub'),(11555,3,2257,204273,'said akela'),(11556,3,5663,9785,'among the free people who speaks'),(11557,3,74,94122,'there was no answer and mother wolf got ready for what she knew would be her last fight if things came to fighting'),(11558,3,1502,204754,'then the only other creature who is allowed at the pack council--baloo the sleepy brown bear who teaches the wolf cubs the law of the jungle old baloo who can come and go where he pleases because he eats only nuts and roots and honey--rose upon his hind quarters and grunted'),(11559,3,51,203294,'the mans cub--the mans cub'),(11560,3,258,2257,'he said'),(11561,3,279,203294,'i speak for the mans cub'),(11562,3,74,203294,'there is no harm in a mans cub'),(11563,3,279,4788,'i have no gift of words but i speak the truth'),(11564,3,2792,90,'let him run with the pack and be entered with the others'),(11565,3,279,247,'i myself will teach him'),(11566,3,1649,204273,'we need yet another said akela'),(11567,3,204731,202271,'baloo has spoken and he is our teacher for the young cubs'),(11568,3,59,204731,'who speaks besides baloo'),(11569,3,23,22572,'a black shadow dropped down into the circle'),(11570,3,67,151229,'it was bagheera the black panther inky black all over but with the panther markings showing up in certain lights like the pattern of watered silk'),(11571,3,406,176368,'everybody knew bagheera and nobody cared to cross his path for he was as cunning as tabaqui as bold as the wild buffalo and as reckless as the wounded elephant'),(11572,3,295,4332,'but he had a voice as soft as wild honey dripping from a tree and a skin softer than down'),(11573,3,7994,61777,'o akela and ye the free people he purred i have no right in your assembly but the law of the jungle says that if there is a doubt which is not a killing matter in regard to a new cub the life of that cub may be bought at a price'),(11574,3,1,61777,'and the law does not say who may or may not pay that price'),(11575,3,1711,106,'am i right'),(11576,3,2257,202342,'said the young wolves who are always hungry'),(11577,3,4723,204836,'listen to bagheera'),(11578,3,51,61777,'the cub can be bought for a price'),(11579,3,67,28793,'it is the law'),(11580,3,15957,4616,'knowing that i have no right to speak here i ask your leave'),(11581,3,4634,141258,'speak then cried twenty voices'),(11582,3,40,18137,'to kill a naked cub is shame'),(11583,3,16049,4039,'besides he may make better sport for you when he is grown'),(11584,3,204731,30733,'baloo has spoken in his behalf'),(11585,3,2530,28793,'now to baloos word i will add one bull and a fat one newly killed not half a mile from here if ye will accept the mans cub according to the law'),(11586,3,75,2733,'is it difficult'),(11587,3,74,6358,'there was a clamor of scores of voices saying what matter'),(11588,3,258,149576,'he will die in the winter rains'),(11589,3,258,143868,'he will scorch in the sun'),(11590,3,994,2793,'what harm can a naked frog do us'),(11591,3,2792,10128,'let him run with the pack'),(11592,3,4187,204836,'where is the bull bagheera'),(11593,3,2792,1478,'let him be accepted'),(11594,3,1,202324,'and then came akelas deep bay crying look well--look well o wolves'),(11595,3,204077,139,'mowgli was still deeply interested in the pebbles and he did not notice when the wolves came and looked at him one by one'),(11596,3,308,1811,'at last they all went down the hill for the dead bull and only akela bagheera baloo and mowglis own wolves were left'),(11597,3,202619,247,'shere khan roared still in the night for he was very angry that mowgli had not been handed over to him'),(11598,3,5908,1278,'ay roar well said bagheera under his whiskers for the time will come when this naked thing will make thee roar to another tune or i know nothing of man'),(11599,3,67,204273,'it was well done said akela'),(11600,3,46,21650,'men and their cubs are very wise'),(11601,3,258,369,'he may be a help in time'),(11602,3,1623,204836,'truly a help in time of need for none can hope to lead the pack forever said bagheera'),(11603,3,204273,1155,'akela said nothing'),(11604,3,258,11455,'he was thinking of the time that comes to every leader of every pack when his strength goes from him and he gets feebler and feebler till at last he is killed by the wolves and a new leader comes up--to be killed in his turn'),(11605,3,1583,7105,'take him away he said to father wolf and train him as befits one of the free people'),(11606,3,1,1426,'and that is how mowgli was entered into the seeonee wolf pack for the price of a bull and on baloos good word'),(11607,3,2530,41,'now you must be content to skip ten or eleven whole years and only guess at all the wonderful life that mowgli led among the wolves because if it were written out it would fill ever so many books'),(11608,3,258,17601,'he grew up with the cubs though they of course were grown wolves almost before he was a child'),(11609,3,1,1278,'and father wolf taught him his business and the meaning of things in the jungle till every rustle in the grass every breath of the warm night air every note of the owls above his head every scratch of a bats claws as it roosted for a while in a tree and every splash of every little fish jumping in a pool meant just as much to him as the work of his office means to a business man'),(11610,3,483,3373,'when he was not learning he sat out in the sun and slept and ate and went to sleep again'),(11611,3,483,513,'when he felt dirty or hot he swam in the forest pools and when he wanted honey baloo told him that honey and nuts were just as pleasant to eat as raw meat he climbed up for it and that bagheera showed him how to do'),(11612,3,204836,172912,'bagheera would lie out on a branch and call come along little brother and at first mowgli would cling like the sloth but afterward he would fling himself through the branches almost as boldly as the gray ape'),(11613,3,258,39838,'he took his place at the council rock too when the pack met and there he discovered that if he stared hard at any wolf the wolf would be forced to drop his eyes and so he used to stare for fun'),(11614,3,308,31846,'at other times he would pick the long thorns out of the pads of his friends for wolves suffer terribly from thorns and burs in their coats'),(11615,3,258,170647,'he would go down the hillside into the cultivated lands by night and look very curiously at the villagers in their huts but he had a mistrust of men because bagheera showed him a square box with a drop gate so cunningly hidden in the jungle that he nearly walked into it and told him that it was a trap'),(11616,3,258,147818,'he loved better than anything else to go with bagheera into the dark warm heart of the forest to sleep all through the drowsy day and at night see how bagheera did his killing'),(11617,3,204836,16842,'bagheera killed right and left as he felt hungry and so did mowgli--with one exception'),(11618,3,42,534,'as soon as he was old enough to understand things bagheera told him that he must never touch cattle because he had been bought into the pack at the price of a bulls life'),(11619,3,172,2018,'all the jungle is thine said bagheera and thou canst kill everything that thou art strong enough to kill but for the sake of the bull that bought thee thou must never kill or eat any cattle young or old'),(11620,3,163,148066,'that is the law of the jungle'),(11621,3,204077,104345,'mowgli obeyed faithfully'),(11622,3,1,9833,'and he grew and grew strong as a boy must grow who does not know that he is learning any lessons and who has nothing in the world to think of except things to eat'),(11623,3,5751,202620,'mother wolf told him once or twice that shere khan was not a creature to be trusted and that some day he must kill shere khan'),(11624,3,295,3696,'but though a young wolf would have remembered that advice every hour mowgli forgot it because he was only a boy--though he would have called himself a wolf if he had been able to speak in any human tongue'),(11625,3,202619,43905,'shere khan was always crossing his path in the jungle for as akela grew older and feebler the lame tiger had come to be great friends with the younger wolves of the pack who followed him for scraps a thing akela would never have allowed if he had dared to push his authority to the proper bounds'),(11626,3,1502,203294,'then shere khan would flatter them and wonder that such fine young hunters were content to be led by a dying wolf and a mans cub'),(11627,3,1056,917,'they tell me shere khan would say that at council ye dare not look him between the eyes'),(11628,3,1,159850,'and the young wolves would growl and bristle'),(11629,3,204836,2045,'bagheera who had eyes and ears everywhere knew something of this and once or twice he told mowgli in so many words that shere khan would kill him some day'),(11630,3,204077,5860,'mowgli would laugh and answer i have the pack and i have thee and baloo though he is so lazy might strike a blow or two for my sake'),(11631,3,4949,6416,'why should i be afraid'),(11632,3,67,3846,'it was one very warm day that a new notion came to bagheera--born of something that he had heard'),(11633,3,290,86811,'perhaps ikki the porcupine had told him but he said to mowgli when they were deep in the jungle as the boy lay with his head on bagheeras beautiful black skin little brother how often have i told thee that shere khan is thy enemy'),(11634,3,42,1134,'as many times as there are nuts on that palm said mowgli who naturally could not count'),(11635,3,994,67,'what of it'),(11636,3,279,154633,'i am sleepy bagheera and shere khan is all long tail and loud talk--like mao the peacock'),(11637,3,295,93214,'but this is no time for sleeping'),(11638,3,204731,515,'baloo knows it i know it the pack know it and even the foolish foolish deer know'),(11639,3,202357,813,'tabaqui has told thee too'),(11640,3,2257,204077,'said mowgli'),(11641,3,202357,206296,'tabaqui came to me not long ago with some rude talk that i was a naked mans cub and not fit to dig pig-nuts'),(11642,3,295,2559,'but i caught tabaqui by the tail and swung him twice against a palm-tree to teach him better manners'),(11643,3,163,87414,'that was foolishness for though tabaqui is a mischief-maker he would have told thee of something that concerned thee closely'),(11644,3,2679,8526,'open those eyes little brother'),(11645,3,202619,148066,'shere khan dare not kill thee in the jungle'),(11646,3,295,77,'but remember akela is very old and soon the day comes when he cannot kill his buck and then he will be leader no more'),(11647,3,2455,10128,'many of the wolves that looked thee over when thou wast brought to the council first are old too and the young wolves believe as shere khan has taught them that a man-cub has no place with the pack'),(11648,3,50,1278,'in a little time thou wilt be a man'),(11649,3,1,8709,'and what is a man that he should not run with his brothers'),(11650,3,2257,204077,'said mowgli'),(11651,3,279,148066,'i was born in the jungle'),(11652,3,279,173836,'i have obeyed the law of the jungle and there is no wolf of ours from whose paws i have not pulled a thorn'),(11653,3,5320,8709,'surely they are my brothers'),(11654,3,204836,917,'bagheera stretched himself at full length and half shut his eyes'),(11655,3,262,171969,'little brother said he feel under my jaw'),(11656,3,204077,26336,'mowgli put up his strong brown hand and just under bagheeras silky chin where the giant rolling muscles were all hid by the glossy hair he came upon a little bald spot'),(11657,3,74,206568,'there is no one in the jungle that knows that i bagheera carry that mark--the mark of the collar and yet little brother i was born among men and it was among men that my mother died--in the cages of the kings palace at oodeypore'),(11658,3,67,203294,'it was because of this that i paid the price for thee at the council when thou wast a little naked cub'),(11659,3,9487,46,'yes i too was born among men'),(11660,3,279,148066,'i had never seen the jungle'),(11661,3,1056,4025,'they fed me behind bars from an iron pan till one night i felt that i was bagheera--the panther--and no mans plaything and i broke the silly lock with one blow of my paw and came away'),(11662,3,1,202620,'and because i had learned the ways of men i became more terrible in the jungle than shere khan'),(11663,3,75,1051,'is it not so'),(11664,3,9487,204077,'yes said mowgli all the jungle fear bagheera--all except mowgli'),(11665,3,5037,4644,'oh thou art a mans cub said the black panther very tenderly'),(11666,3,1,155390,'and even as i returned to my jungle so thou must go back to men at last--to the men who are thy brothers--if thou art not killed in the council'),(11667,3,295,424,'but why--but why should any wish to kill me'),(11668,3,2257,204077,'said mowgli'),(11669,3,2046,204836,'look at me said bagheera'),(11670,3,1,917,'and mowgli looked at him steadily between the eyes'),(11671,3,51,26616,'the big panther turned his head away in half a minute'),(11672,3,163,18660,'that is why he said shifting his paw on the leaves'),(11673,3,55,8526,'not even i can look thee between the eyes and i was born among men and i love thee little brother'),(11674,3,51,1278,'the others they hate thee because their eyes cannot meet thine because thou art wise because thou hast pulled out thorns from their feet--because thou art a man'),(11675,3,279,143872,'i did not know these things said mowgli sullenly and he frowned under his heavy black eyebrows'),(11676,3,994,148066,'what is the law of the jungle'),(11677,3,33162,3696,'strike first and then give tongue'),(11678,3,3,1278,'by thy very carelessness they know that thou art a man'),(11679,3,295,21650,'but be wise'),(11680,3,67,203807,'it is in my heart that when akela misses his next kill--and at each hunt it costs him more to pin the buck--the pack will turn against him and against thee'),(11681,3,1056,67,'they will hold a jungle council at the rock and then--and then--i have it'),(11682,3,2257,1274,'said bagheera leaping up'),(11683,3,351,203807,'go thou down quickly to the mens huts in the valley and take some of the red flower which they grow there so that when the time comes thou mayest have even a stronger friend than i or baloo or those of the pack that love thee'),(11684,3,2075,165943,'get the red flower'),(11685,3,3,5031,