e nuts they pick and throw them down'),(12156,3,1056,1024,'they carry a branch half a day meaning to do great things with it and then they snap it in two'),(12157,3,163,17588,'that man-thing is not to be envied'),(12158,3,1056,55,'they called me also--yellow fish was it not'),(12159,3,214034,18137,'worm--worm--earth-worm said bagheera as well as other things which i cannot now say for shame'),(12160,3,1649,22259,'we must remind them to speak well of their master'),(12161,3,1649,8705,'we must help their wandering memories'),(12162,3,2530,203294,'now whither went they with the cub'),(12163,3,51,1986,'the jungle alone knows'),(12164,3,214076,204731,'toward the sunset i believe said baloo'),(12165,3,1649,212986,'we had thought that thou wouldst know kaa'),(12166,3,279,6358,'i take them when they come in my way but i do not hunt the bandar-log or frogs--or green scum on a water-hole for that matter'),(12167,3,1274,1274,'up up'),(12168,3,1274,1274,'up up'),(12169,3,214121,10128,'illo look up baloo of the seeonee wolf pack'),(12170,3,204731,155351,'baloo looked up to see where the voice came from and there was rann the kite sweeping down with the sun shining on the upturned flanges of his wings'),(12171,3,67,163970,'it was near ranns bedtime but he had ranged all over the jungle looking for the bear and had missed him in the thick foliage'),(12172,3,994,67,'what is it'),(12173,3,2257,204731,'said baloo'),(12174,3,279,211307,'i have seen mowgli among the bandar-log'),(12175,3,258,2074,'he bade me tell you'),(12176,3,279,34885,'i watched'),(12177,3,51,190906,'the bandar-log have taken him beyond the river to the monkey city--to the cold lairs'),(12178,3,1056,8903,'they may stay there for a night or ten nights or an hour'),(12179,3,279,369,'i have told the bats to watch through the dark time'),(12180,3,163,4618,'that is my message'),(12181,3,676,15363,'good hunting all you below'),(12182,3,1844,204836,'full gorge and a deep sleep to you rann cried bagheera'),(12183,3,279,210608,'i will remember thee in my next kill and put aside the head for thee alone o best of kites'),(12184,3,67,1155,'it is nothing'),(12185,3,67,1155,'it is nothing'),(12186,3,51,1426,'the boy held the master word'),(12187,3,279,214307,'i could have done no less and rann circled up again to his roost'),(12188,3,258,417,'he has not forgotten to use his tongue said baloo with a chuckle of pride'),(12189,3,40,53546,'to think of one so young remembering the master word for the birds too while he was being pulled across trees'),(12190,3,67,204836,'it was most firmly driven into him said bagheera'),(12191,3,295,190906,'but i am proud of him and now we must go to the cold lairs'),(12192,3,1056,10071,'they all knew where that place was but few of the jungle people ever went there because what they called the cold lairs was an old deserted city lost and buried in the jungle and beasts seldom use a place that men have once used'),(12193,3,51,55,'the wild boar will but the hunting tribes do not'),(12194,3,16049,82524,'besides the monkeys lived there as much as they could be said to live anywhere and no self-respecting animal would come within eyeshot of it except in times of drought when the half-ruined tanks and reservoirs held a little water'),(12195,3,4305,3749,'illustration the cold lairs it is half a nights journey--at full speed said bagheera and baloo looked very serious'),(12196,3,279,89575,'i will go as fast as i can he said anxiously'),(12197,3,1649,203807,'we dare not wait for thee'),(12198,3,17006,204731,'follow baloo'),(12199,3,1649,279,'we must go on the quick-foot--kaa and i'),(12200,3,42747,33186,'feet or no feet i can keep abreast of all thy four said kaa shortly'),(12201,3,204731,214552,'baloo made one effort to hurry but had to sit down panting and so they left him to come on later while bagheera hurried forward at the quick panther-canter'),(12202,3,212986,247,'kaa said nothing but strive as bagheera might the huge rock-python held level with him'),(12203,3,483,12661,'when they came to a hill stream bagheera gained because he bounded across while kaa swam his head and two feet of his neck clearing the water but on level ground kaa made up the distance'),(12204,3,3,214621,'by the broken lock that freed me said bagheera when twilight had fallen thou art no slow goer'),(12205,3,279,212986,'i am hungry said kaa'),(12206,3,16049,204073,'besides they called me speckled frog'),(12207,3,214633,165513,'worm--earth-worm and yellow to boot'),(12208,3,172,139,'all one'),(12209,3,2792,67,'let us go on and kaa seemed to pour himself along the ground finding the shortest road with his steady eyes and keeping to it'),(12210,3,50,172,'in the cold lairs the monkey-people were not thinking of mowglis friends at all'),(12211,3,1056,369,'they had brought the boy to the lost city and were very much pleased with themselves for the time'),(12212,3,204077,7760,'mowgli had never seen an indian city before and though this was almost a heap of ruins it seemed very wonderful and splendid'),(12213,3,144,1850,'some king had built it long ago on a little hill'),(12214,3,2074,214757,'you could still trace the stone causeways that led up to the ruined gates where the last splinters of wood hung to the worn rusted hinges'),(12215,3,53546,148546,'trees had grown into and out of the walls the battlements were tumbled down and decayed and wild creepers hung out of the windows of the towers on the walls in bushy hanging clumps'),(12216,3,23,53546,'a great roofless palace crowned the hill and the marble of the courtyards and the fountains was split and stained with red and green and the very cobblestones in the courtyard where the kings elephants used to live had been thrust up and apart by grasses and young trees'),(12217,3,344,68646,'from the palace you could see the rows and rows of roofless houses that made up the city looking like empty honeycombs filled with blackness the shapeless block of stone that had been an idol in the square where four roads met the pits and dimples at street corners where the public wells once stood and the shattered domes of temples with wild figs sprouting on their sides'),(12218,3,51,145548,'the monkeys called the place their city and pretended to despise the jungle-people because they lived in the forest'),(12219,3,1,112,'and yet they never knew what the buildings were made for nor how to use them'),(12220,3,1056,3136,'they would sit in circles on the hall of the kings council chamber and scratch for fleas and pretend to be men or they would run in and out of the roofless houses and collect pieces of plaster and old bricks in a corner and forget where they had hidden them and fight and cry in scuffling crowds and then break off to play up and down the terraces of the kings garden where they would shake the rose trees and the oranges in sport to see the fruit and flowers fall'),(12221,3,1056,991,'they explored all the passages and dark tunnels in the palace and the hundreds of little dark rooms but they never remembered what they had seen and what they had not and so drifted about in ones and twos or crowds telling each other that they were doing as men did'),(12222,3,1056,211307,'they drank at the tanks and made the water all muddy and then they fought over it and then they would all rush together in mobs and shout there is no one in the jungle so wise and good and clever and strong and gentle as the bandar-log'),(12223,3,1502,112,'then all would begin again till they grew tired of the city and went back to the tree-tops hoping the jungle-people would notice them'),(12224,3,204077,534,'mowgli who had been trained under the law of the jungle did not like or understand this kind of life'),(12225,3,51,19783,'the monkeys dragged him into the cold lairs late in the afternoon and instead of going to sleep as mowgli would have done after a long journey they joined hands and danced about and sang their foolish songs'),(12226,3,139,1905,'one of the monkeys made a speech and told his companions that mowglis capture marked a new thing in the history of the bandar-log for mowgli was going to show them how to weave sticks and canes together as a protection against rain and cold'),(12227,3,204077,5855,'mowgli picked up some creepers and began to work them in and out and the monkeys tried to imitate but in a very few minutes they lost interest and began to pull their friends tails or jump up and down on all fours coughing'),(12228,3,279,204077,'i wish to eat said mowgli'),(12229,3,279,148066,'i am a stranger in this part of the jungle'),(12230,3,6853,454,'bring me food or give me leave to hunt here'),(12231,3,5495,215344,'twenty or thirty monkeys bounded away to bring him nuts and wild pawpaws'),(12232,3,295,88808,'but they fell to fighting on the road and it was too much trouble to go back with what was left of the fruit'),(12233,3,204077,330,'mowgli was sore and angry as well as hungry and he roamed through the empty city giving the strangers hunting call from time to time but no one answered him and mowgli felt that he had reached a very bad place indeed'),(12234,3,172,852,'all that baloo has said about the bandar-log is true he thought to himself'),(12235,3,1056,22498,'they have no law no hunting call and no leaders--nothing but foolish words and little picking thievish hands'),(12236,3,1051,8377,'so if i am starved or killed here it will be all my own fault'),(12237,3,295,148066,'but i must try to return to my own jungle'),(12238,3,204731,211307,'baloo will surely beat me but that is better than chasing silly rose leaves with the bandar-log'),(12239,3,655,23347,'no sooner had he walked to the city wall than the monkeys pulled him back telling him that he did not know how happy he was and pinching him to make him grateful'),(12240,3,258,82524,'he set his teeth and said nothing but went with the shouting monkeys to a terrace above the red sandstone reservoirs that were half-full of rain water'),(12241,3,74,2566,'there was a ruined summer-house of white marble in the center of the terrace built for queens dead a hundred years ago'),(12242,3,51,28576,'the domed roof had half fallen in and blocked up the underground passage from the palace by which the queens used to enter'),(12243,3,295,215635,'but the walls were made of screens of marble tracery--beautiful milk-white fretwork set with agates and cornelians and jasper and lapis lazuli and as the moon came up behind the hill it shone through the open work casting shadows on the ground like black velvet embroidery'),(12244,3,13724,112,'sore sleepy and hungry as he was mowgli could not help laughing when the bandar-log began twenty at a time to tell him how great and wise and strong and gentle they were and how foolish he was to wish to leave them'),(12245,3,1649,1108,'we are great'),(12246,3,1649,26108,'we are free'),(12247,3,1649,29444,'we are wonderful'),(12248,3,1649,148066,'we are the most wonderful people in all the jungle'),(12249,3,1649,142149,'we all say so and so it must be true they shouted'),(12250,3,2530,86838,'now as you are a new listener and can carry our words back to the jungle-people so that they may notice us in future we will tell you all about our most excellent selves'),(12251,3,204077,1051,'mowgli made no objection and the monkeys gathered by hundreds and hundreds on the terrace to listen to their own speakers singing the praises of the bandar-log and whenever a speaker stopped for want of breath they would all shout together this is true we all say so'),(12252,3,204077,30389,'mowgli nodded and blinked and said yes when they asked him a question and his head spun with the noise'),(12253,3,202357,181431,'tabaqui the jackal must have bitten all these people he said to himself and now they have madness'),(12254,3,829,181431,'certainly this is dewanee the madness'),(12255,3,513,20721,'do they never go to sleep'),(12256,3,2530,154870,'now there is a cloud coming to cover that moon'),(12257,3,257,145505,'if it were only a big enough cloud i might try to run away in the darkness'),(12258,3,295,21958,'but i am tired'),(12259,3,163,215911,'that same cloud was being watched by two good friends in the ruined ditch below the city wall for bagheera and kaa knowing well how dangerous the monkey-people were in large numbers did not wish to run any risks'),(12260,3,51,155747,'the monkeys never fight unless they are a hundred to one and few in the jungle care for those odds'),(12261,3,279,129049,'i will go to the west wall kaa whispered and come down swiftly with the slope of the ground in my favor'),(12262,3,1056,204836,'they will not throw themselves upon my back in their hundreds but-- i know it said bagheera'),(12263,3,126,850,'would that baloo were here but we must do what we can'),(12264,3,483,129871,'when that cloud covers the moon i shall go to the terrace'),(12265,3,1056,10170,'they hold some sort of council there over the boy'),(12266,3,676,132350,'good hunting said kaa grimly and glided away to the west wall'),(12267,3,163,152254,'that happened to be the least ruined of any and the big snake was delayed awhile before he could find a way up the stones'),(12268,3,51,129871,'the cloud hid the moon and as mowgli wondered what would come next he heard bagheeras light feet on the terrace'),(12269,3,51,6103,'the black panther had raced up the slope almost without a sound and was striking--he knew better than to waste time in biting--right and left among the monkeys who were seated round mowgli in circles fifty and sixty deep'),(12270,3,74,454,'there was a howl of fright and rage and then as bagheera tripped on the rolling kicking bodies beneath him a monkey shouted there is only one here'),(12271,3,103880,247,'kill him'),(12272,3,23,193815,'a scuffling mass of monkeys biting scratching tearing and pulling closed over bagheera while five or six laid hold of mowgli dragged him up the wall of the summerhouse and pushed him through the hole of the broken dome'),(12273,3,23,42747,'a man-trained boy would have been badly bruised for the fall was a good fifteen feet but mowgli fell as baloo had taught him to fall and landed on his feet'),(12274,3,4414,2362,'stay there shouted the monkeys till we have killed thy friends and later we will play with thee--if the poison-people leave thee alive'),(12275,3,1649,18735,'we be of one blood ye and i said mowgli quickly giving the snakes call'),(12276,3,258,1763,'he could hear rustling and hissing in the rubbish all round him and gave the call a second time to make sure'),(12277,3,1232,216263,'even ssso'),(12278,3,4332,172,'down hoods all'),(12279,3,2257,216293,'said half a dozen low voices every ruin in india becomes sooner or later a dwelling place of snakes and the old summerhouse was alive with cobras'),(12280,3,6021,46509,'stand still little brother for thy feet may do us harm'),(12281,3,204077,34021,'mowgli stood as quietly as he could peering through the open work and listening to the furious din of the fight round the black panther--the yells and chatterings and scufflings and bagheeras deep hoarse cough as he backed and bucked and twisted and plunged under the heaps of his enemies'),(12282,3,284,534,'for the first time since he was born bagheera was fighting for his life'),(12283,3,204731,864,'baloo must be at hand bagheera would not have come alone mowgli thought'),(12284,3,1,204836,'and then he called aloud to the tank bagheera'),(12285,3,151802,202977,'roll to the water tanks'),(12286,3,151802,188660,'roll and plunge'),(12287,3,2075,82524,'get to the water'),(12288,3,204836,46322,'bagheera heard and the cry that told him mowgli was safe gave him new courage'),(12289,3,258,8550,'he worked his way desperately inch by inch straight for the reservoirs halting in silence'),(12290,3,1502,204731,'then from the ruined wall nearest the jungle rose up the rumbling war-shout of baloo'),(12291,3,51,509,'the old bear had done his best but he could not come before'),(12292,3,204836,454,'bagheera he shouted i am here'),(12293,3,279,151379,'i climb'),(12294,3,279,13277,'i haste'),(12295,3,51,42747,'the stones slip under my feet'),(12296,3,18092,211307,'wait my coming o most infamous bandar-log'),(12297,3,258,216536,'he panted up the terrace only to disappear to the head in a wave of monkeys but he threw himself squarely on his haunches and spreading out his forepaws hugged as many as he could hold and then began to hit with a regular bat-bat-bat like the flipping strokes of a paddle wheel'),(12298,3,23,17006,'a crash and a splash told mowgli that bagheera had fought his way to the tank where the monkeys could not follow'),(12299,3,51,204731,'the panther lay gasping for breath his head just out of the water while the monkeys stood three deep on the red steps dancing up and down with rage ready to spring upon him from all sides if he came out to help baloo'),(12300,3,67,26616,'it was then that bagheera lifted up his dripping chin and in despair gave the snakes call for protection--we be of one blood ye and i--for he believed that kaa had turned tail at the last minute'),(12301,3,1232,4236,'even baloo half smothered under the monkeys on the edge of the terrace could not help chuckling as he heard the black panther asking for help'),(12302,3,212986,215886,'kaa had only just worked his way over the west wall landing with a wrench that dislodged a coping stone into the ditch'),(12303,3,258,4244,'he had no intention of losing any advantage of the ground and coiled and uncoiled himself once or twice to be sure that every foot of his long body was in working order'),(12304,3,172,4732,'all that while the fight with baloo went on and the monkeys yelled in the tank round bagheera and mang the bat flying to and fro carried the news of the great battle over the jungle till even hathi the wild elephant trumpeted and far away scattered bands of the monkey-folk woke and came leaping along the tree-roads to help their comrades in the cold lairs and the noise of the fight roused all the day birds for miles round'),(12305,3,1502,103880,'then kaa came straight quickly and anxious to kill'),(12306,3,51,133885,'the fighting strength of a python is in the driving blow of his head backed by all the strength and weight of his body'),(12307,3,257,174212,'if you can imagine a lance or a battering ram or a hammer weighing nearly half a ton driven by a cool quiet mind living in the handle of it you can roughly imagine what kaa was like when he fought'),(12308,3,23,515,'a python four or five feet long can knock a man down if he hits him fairly in the chest and kaa was thirty feet long as you know'),(12309,3,892,204731,'his first stroke was delivered into the heart of the crowd round baloo'),(12310,3,67,2295,'it was sent home with shut mouth in silence and there was no need of a second'),(12311,3,51,216940,'the monkeys scattered with cries of--kaa'),(12312,3,67,212986,'it is kaa'),(12313,3,15572,112,'generations of monkeys had been scared into good behavior by the stories their elders told them of kaa the night thief who could slip along the branches as quietly as moss grows and steal away the strongest monkey that ever lived of old kaa who could make himself look so like a dead branch or a rotten stump that the wisest were deceived till the branch caught them'),(12314,3,212986,21545,'kaa was everything that the monkeys feared in the jungle for none of them knew the limits of his power none of them could look him in the face and none had ever come alive out of his hug'),(12315,3,1,16441,'and so they ran stammering with terror to the walls and the roofs of the houses and baloo drew a deep breath of relief'),(12316,3,892,93834,'his fur was much thicker than bagheeras but he had suffered sorely in the fight'),(12317,3,1502,112,'then kaa opened his mouth for the first time and spoke one long hissing word and the far-away monkeys hurrying to the defense of the cold lairs stayed where they were cowering till the loaded branches bent and crackled under them'),(12318,3,51,216389,'the monkeys on the walls and the empty houses stopped their cries and in the stillness that fell upon the city mowgli heard bagheera shaking his wet sides as he came up from the tank'),(12319,3,1502,3373,'then the clamor broke out again'),(12320,3,51,143533,'the monkeys leaped higher up the walls'),(12321,3,1056,3878,'they clung around the necks of the big stone idols and shrieked as they skipped along the battlements while mowgli dancing in the summerhouse put his eye to the screenwork and hooted owl-fashion between his front teeth to show his derision and contempt'),(12322,3,2075,159717,'get the man-cub out of that trap i can do no more bagheera gasped'),(12323,3,2792,351,'let us take the man-cub and go'),(12324,3,1056,3373,'they may attack again'),(12325,3,1056,112,'they will not move till i order them'),(12326,3,4414,217257,'stay you sssso'),(12327,3,212986,77,'kaa hissed and the city was silent once more'),(12328,3,279,204836,'i could not come before brother but i think i heard thee call--this was to bagheera'),(12329,3,202693,13526,'i--i may have cried out in the battle bagheera answered'),(12330,3,204731,39767,'baloo art thou hurt'),(12331,3,279,409,'i am not sure that they did not pull me into a hundred little bearlings said baloo gravely shaking one leg after the other'),(12332,3,279,13724,'i am sore'),(12333,3,212986,279,'kaa we owe thee i think our lives--bagheera and i'),(12334,3,655,6358,'no matter'),(12335,3,4187,217339,'where is the manling'),(12336,3,454,170647,'here in a trap'),(12337,3,279,204077,'i cannot climb out cried mowgli'),(12338,3,51,1999,'the curve of the broken dome was above his head'),(12339,3,1583,4025,'take him away'),(12340,3,258,154633,'he dances like mao the peacock'),(12341,3,258,148771,'he will crush our young said the cobras inside'),(12342,3,2257,217339,'said kaa with a chuckle he has friends everywhere this manling'),(12343,3,6021,217339,'stand back manling'),(12344,3,1,7105,'and hide you o poison people'),(12345,3,279,132350,'i break down the wall'),(12346,3,21