k and side'),(12455,3,4187,3,'where is the haste that ye hurry by'),(12456,3,8526,13797,'brother i go to my lair--to die'),(12457,3,2530,180552,'now we must go back to the first tale'),(12458,3,483,155390,'when mowgli left the wolfs cave after the fight with the pack at the council rock he went down to the plowed lands where the villagers lived but he would not stop there because it was too near to the jungle and he knew that he had made at least one bad enemy at the council'),(12459,3,1051,515,'so he hurried on keeping to the rough road that ran down the valley and followed it at a steady jog-trot for nearly twenty miles till he came to a country that he did not know'),(12460,3,51,219008,'the valley opened out into a great plain dotted over with rocks and cut up by ravines'),(12461,3,308,219043,'at one end stood a little village and at the other the thick jungle came down in a sweep to the grazing-grounds and stopped there as though it had been cut off with a hoe'),(12462,3,172,219081,'all over the plain cattle and buffaloes were grazing and when the little boys in charge of the herds saw mowgli they shouted and ran away and the yellow pariah dogs that hang about every indian village barked'),(12463,3,204077,1473,'mowgli walked on for he was feeling hungry and when he came to the village gate he saw the big thorn-bush that was drawn up before the gate at twilight pushed to one side'),(12464,3,258,9833,'he said for he had come across more than one such barricade in his night rambles after things to eat'),(12465,3,1051,1187,'so men are afraid of the people of the jungle here also'),(12466,3,258,17728,'he sat down by the gate and when a man came out he stood up opened his mouth and pointed down it to show that he wanted food'),(12467,3,51,128997,'the man stared and ran back up the one street of the village shouting for the priest who was a big fat man dressed in white with a red and yellow mark on his forehead'),(12468,3,51,204077,'the priest came to the gate and with him at least a hundred people who stared and talked and shouted and pointed at mowgli'),(12469,3,1056,852,'they have no manners these men folk said mowgli to himself'),(12470,3,241,513,'only the gray ape would behave as they do'),(12471,3,1051,41478,'so he threw back his long hair and frowned at the crowd'),(12472,3,994,53,'what is there to be afraid of'),(12473,3,2257,141845,'said the priest'),(12474,3,2046,133223,'look at the marks on his arms and legs'),(12475,3,1056,202324,'they are the bites of wolves'),(12476,3,258,148066,'he is but a wolf-child run away from the jungle'),(12477,3,53,133223,'of course in playing together the cubs had often nipped mowgli harder than they intended and there were white scars all over his arms and legs'),(12478,3,295,6559,'but he would have been the last person in the world to call these bites for he knew what real biting meant'),(12479,3,2257,10658,'said two or three women together'),(12480,3,40,17601,'to be bitten by wolves poor child'),(12481,3,258,10170,'he is a handsome boy'),(12482,3,258,2140,'he has eyes like red fire'),(12483,3,3,169458,'by my honor messua he is not unlike thy boy that was taken by the tiger'),(12484,3,2792,410,'let me look said a woman with heavy copper rings on her wrists and ankles and she peered at mowgli under the palm of her hand'),(12485,3,330,55,'indeed he is not'),(12486,3,258,10170,'he is thinner but he has the very look of my boy'),(12487,3,51,461,'the priest was a clever man and he knew that messua was wife to the richest villager in the place'),(12488,3,1051,14651,'so he looked up at the sky for a minute and said solemnly what the jungle has taken the jungle has restored'),(12489,3,1583,46,'take the boy into thy house my sister and forget not to honor the priest who sees so far into the lives of men'),(12490,3,3,10128,'by the bull that bought me said mowgli to himself but all this talking is like another looking-over by the pack'),(12491,3,43,16809,'well if i am a man a man i must become'),(12492,3,51,219590,'the crowd parted as the woman beckoned mowgli to her hut where there was a red lacquered bedstead a great earthen grain chest with funny raised patterns on it half a dozen copper cooking pots an image of a hindu god in a little alcove and on the wall a real looking glass such as they sell at the country fairs'),(12493,3,729,247,'she gave him a long drink of milk and some bread and then she laid her hand on his head and looked into his eyes for she thought perhaps that he might be her real son come back from the jungle where the tiger had taken him'),(12494,3,1051,219641,'so she said nathoo o nathoo'),(12495,3,204077,5031,'mowgli did not show that he knew the name'),(12496,3,210271,1901,'dost thou not remember the day when i gave thee thy new shoes'),(12497,3,729,219677,'she touched his foot and it was almost as hard as horn'),(12498,3,655,1423,'no she said sorrowfully those feet have never worn shoes but thou art very like my nathoo and thou shalt be my son'),(12499,3,204077,509,'mowgli was uneasy because he had never been under a roof before'),(12500,3,295,219742,'but as he looked at the thatch he saw that he could tear it out any time if he wanted to get away and that the window had no fastenings'),(12501,3,994,5107,'what is the good of a man he said to himself at last if he does not understand mans talk'),(12502,3,2530,148066,'now i am as silly and dumb as a man would be with us in the jungle'),(12503,3,279,5107,'i must speak their talk'),(12504,3,67,170415,'it was not for fun that he had learned while he was with the wolves to imitate the challenge of bucks in the jungle and the grunt of the little wild pig'),(12505,3,1051,150533,'so as soon as messua pronounced a word mowgli would imitate it almost perfectly and before dark he had learned the names of many things in the hut'),(12506,3,74,6694,'there was a difficulty at bedtime because mowgli would not sleep under anything that looked so like a panther trap as that hut and when they shut the door he went through the window'),(12507,3,2786,1321,'give him his will said messuas husband'),(12508,3,1830,14397,'remember he can never till now have slept on a bed'),(12509,3,257,4025,'if he is indeed sent in the place of our son he will not run away'),(12510,3,1051,73001,'so mowgli stretched himself in some long clean grass at the edge of the field but before he had closed his eyes a soft gray nose poked him under the chin'),(12511,3,2257,202271,'said gray brother he was the eldest of mother wolfs cubs'),(12512,3,37,13944,'this is a poor reward for following thee twenty miles'),(12513,3,202806,2915,'thou smellest of wood smoke and cattle--altogether like a man already'),(12514,3,188514,5637,'wake little brother i bring news'),(12515,3,60,148066,'are all well in the jungle'),(12516,3,2257,247,'said mowgli hugging him'),(12517,3,172,165943,'all except the wolves that were burned with the red flower'),(12518,3,2530,4723,'now listen'),(12519,3,202619,209097,'shere khan has gone away to hunt far off till his coat grows again for he is badly singed'),(12520,3,483,202653,'when he returns he swears that he will lay thy bones in the waingunga'),(12521,3,74,163,'there are two words to that'),(12522,3,279,9907,'i also have made a little promise'),(12523,3,295,676,'but news is always good'),(12524,3,279,705,'i am tired to-night--very tired with new things gray brother--but bring me the news always'),(12525,3,202806,202228,'thou wilt not forget that thou art a wolf'),(12526,3,46,5761,'men will not make thee forget'),(12527,3,2257,89575,'said gray brother anxiously'),(12528,3,279,157427,'i will always remember that i love thee and all in our cave'),(12529,3,295,10128,'but also i will always remember that i have been cast out of the pack'),(12530,3,1,10128,'and that thou mayest be cast out of another pack'),(12531,3,46,209566,'men are only men little brother and their talk is like the talk of frogs in a pond'),(12532,3,483,220165,'when i come down here again i will wait for thee in the bamboos at the edge of the grazing-ground'),(12533,3,284,46,'for three months after that night mowgli hardly ever left the village gate he was so busy learning the ways and customs of men'),(12534,3,183,1674,'first he had to wear a cloth round him which annoyed him horribly and then he had to learn about money which he did not in the least understand and about plowing of which he did not see the use'),(12535,3,1502,7118,'then the little children in the village made him very angry'),(12536,3,45546,1024,'luckily the law of the jungle had taught him to keep his temper for in the jungle life and food depend on keeping your temper but when they made fun of him because he would not play games or fly kites or because he mispronounced some word only the knowledge that it was unsportsmanlike to kill little naked cubs kept him from picking them up and breaking them in two'),(12537,3,258,309,'he did not know his own strength in the least'),(12538,3,50,132057,'in the jungle he knew he was weak compared with the beasts but in the village people said that he was as strong as a bull'),(12539,3,1,1278,'and mowgli had not the faintest idea of the difference that caste makes between man and man'),(12540,3,483,220393,'when the potters donkey slipped in the clay pit mowgli hauled it out by the tail and helped to stack the pots for their journey to the market at khanhiwara'),(12541,3,163,1594,'that was very shocking too for the potter is a low-caste man and his donkey is worse'),(12542,3,483,167069,'when the priest scolded him mowgli threatened to put him on the donkey too and the priest told messuas husband that mowgli had better be set to work as soon as possible and the village head-man told mowgli that he would have to go out with the buffaloes next day and herd them while they grazed'),(12543,3,655,220507,'no one was more pleased than mowgli and that night because he had been appointed a servant of the village as it were he went off to a circle that met every evening on a masonry platform under a great fig-tree'),(12544,3,67,201588,'it was the village club and the head-man and the watchman and the barber who knew all the gossip of the village and old buldeo the village hunter who had a tower musket met and smoked'),(12545,3,51,9700,'the monkeys sat and talked in the upper branches and there was a hole under the platform where a cobra lived and he had his little platter of milk every night because he was sacred and the old men sat around the tree and talked and pulled at the big huqas the water-pipes till far into the night'),(12546,3,1056,84985,'they told wonderful tales of gods and men and ghosts and buldeo told even more wonderful ones of the ways of beasts in the jungle till the eyes of the children sitting outside the circle bulged out of their heads'),(12547,3,387,4543,'most of the tales were about animals for the jungle was always at their door'),(12548,3,51,65185,'the deer and the wild pig grubbed up their crops and now and again the tiger carried off a man at twilight within sight of the village gates'),(12549,3,204077,22497,'mowgli who naturally knew something about what they were talking of had to cover his face not to show that he was laughing while buldeo the tower musket across his knees climbed on from one wonderful story to another and mowglis shoulders shook'),(12550,3,220532,2566,'buldeo was explaining how the tiger that had carried away messuas son was a ghost-tiger and his body was inhabited by the ghost of a wicked old money-lender who had died some years ago'),(12551,3,1,65054,'and i know that this is true he said because purun dass always limped from the blow that he got in a riot when his account books were burned and the tiger that i speak of he limps too for the tracks of his pads are unequal'),(12552,3,2011,10658,'true true that must be the truth said the gray-beards nodding together'),(12553,3,60,220828,'are all these tales such cobwebs and moontalk'),(12554,3,2257,204077,'said mowgli'),(12555,3,163,1986,'that tiger limps because he was born lame as everyone knows'),(12556,3,40,5107,'to talk of the soul of a money-lender in a beast that never had the courage of a jackal is childs talk'),(12557,3,220532,6048,'buldeo was speechless with surprise for a moment and the head-man stared'),(12558,3,67,67,'it is the jungle brat is it'),(12559,3,2257,220532,'said buldeo'),(12560,3,257,534,'if thou art so wise better bring his hide to khanhiwara for the government has set a hundred rupees on his life'),(12561,3,949,4634,'better still talk not when thy elders speak'),(12562,3,204077,351,'mowgli rose to go'),(12563,3,172,54258,'all the evening i have lain here listening he called back over his shoulder and except once or twice buldeo has not said one word of truth concerning the jungle which is at his very doors'),(12564,3,1338,373,'how then shall i believe the tales of ghosts and gods and goblins which he says he has seen'),(12565,3,67,14810,'it is full time that boy went to herding said the head-man while buldeo puffed and snorted at mowglis impertinence'),(12566,3,51,9700,'the custom of most indian villages is for a few boys to take the cattle and buffaloes out to graze in the early morning and bring them back at night'),(12567,3,51,221053,'the very cattle that would trample a white man to death allow themselves to be banged and bullied and shouted at by children that hardly come up to their noses'),(12568,3,1051,154091,'so long as the boys keep with the herds they are safe for not even the tiger will charge a mob of cattle'),(12569,3,295,891,'but if they straggle to pick flowers or hunt lizards they are sometimes carried off'),(12570,3,204077,132057,'mowgli went through the village street in the dawn sitting on the back of rama the great herd bull'),(12571,3,51,22259,'the slaty-blue buffaloes with their long backward-sweeping horns and savage eyes rose out their byres one by one and followed him and mowgli made it very clear to the children with him that he was the master'),(12572,3,258,132280,'he beat the buffaloes with a long polished bamboo and told kamya one of the boys to graze the cattle by themselves while he went on with the buffaloes and to be very careful not to stray away from the herd'),(12573,3,573,138338,'an indian grazing ground is all rocks and scrub and tussocks and little ravines among which the herds scatter and disappear'),(12574,3,51,10657,'the buffaloes generally keep to the pools and muddy places where they lie wallowing or basking in the warm mud for hours'),(12575,3,204077,8526,'mowgli drove them on to the edge of the plain where the waingunga came out of the jungle then he dropped from ramas neck trotted off to a bamboo clump and found gray brother'),(12576,3,5499,1390,'ah said gray brother i have waited here very many days'),(12577,3,994,83,'what is the meaning of this cattle-herding work'),(12578,3,67,204077,'it is an order said mowgli'),(12579,3,279,1394,'i am a village herd for a while'),(12580,3,994,202620,'what news of shere khan'),(12581,3,258,203807,'he has come back to this country and has waited here a long time for thee'),(12582,3,2530,59115,'now he has gone off again for the game is scarce'),(12583,3,295,203807,'but he means to kill thee'),(12584,3,56,204077,'very good said mowgli'),(12585,3,1051,7711,'so long as he is away do thou or one of the four brothers sit on that rock so that i can see thee as i come out of the village'),(12586,3,483,11635,'when he comes back wait for me in the ravine by the dhk tree in the center of the plain'),(12587,3,1649,2725,'we need not walk into shere khans mouth'),(12588,3,1502,247,'then mowgli picked out a shady place and lay down and slept while the buffaloes grazed round him'),(12589,3,220982,378,'herding in india is one of the laziest things in the world'),(12590,3,51,4516,'the cattle move and crunch and lie down and move on again and they do not even low'),(12591,3,1056,189163,'they only grunt and the buffaloes very seldom say anything but get down into the muddy pools one after another and work their way into the mud till only their noses and staring china-blue eyes show above the surface and then they lie like logs'),(12592,3,51,2223,'the sun makes the rocks dance in the heat and the herd children hear one kite never any more whistling almost out of sight overhead and they know that if they died or a cow died that kite would sweep down and the next kite miles away would see him drop and follow and the next and the next and almost before they were dead there would be a score of hungry kites come out of nowhere'),(12593,3,1502,221625,'then they sleep and wake and sleep again and weave little baskets of dried grass and put grasshoppers in them or catch two praying mantises and make them fight or string a necklace of red and black jungle nuts or watch a lizard basking on a rock or a snake hunting a frog near the wallows'),(12594,3,1502,221693,'then they sing long long songs with odd native quavers at the end of them and the day seems longer than most peoples whole lives and perhaps they make a mud castle with mud figures of men and horses and buffaloes and put reeds into the mens hands and pretend that they are kings and the figures are their armies or that they are gods to be worshiped'),(12595,3,1502,143286,'then evening comes and the children call and the buffaloes lumber up out of the sticky mud with noises like gunshots going off one after the other and they all string across the gray plain back to the twinkling village lights'),(12596,3,2045,148066,'day after day mowgli would lead the buffaloes out to their wallows and day after day he would see gray brothers back a mile and a half away across the plain so he knew that shere khan had not come back and day after day he would lie on the grass listening to the noises round him and dreaming of old days in the jungle'),(12597,3,257,31252,'if shere khan had made a false step with his lame paw up in the jungles by the waingunga mowgli would have heard him in those long still mornings'),(12598,3,308,192033,'at last a day came when he did not see gray brother at the signal place and he laughed and headed the buffaloes for the ravine by the dhk tree which was all covered with golden-red flowers'),(12599,3,74,94572,'there sat gray brother every bristle on his back lifted'),(12600,3,258,10398,'he has hidden for a month to throw thee off thy guard'),(12601,3,258,98456,'he crossed the ranges last night with tabaqui hot-foot on thy trail said the wolf panting'),(12602,3,204077,206823,'mowgli frowned'),(12603,3,279,16305,'i am not afraid of shere khan but tabaqui is very cunning'),(12604,3,254,262,'have no fear said gray brother licking his lips a little'),(12605,3,279,151482,'i met tabaqui in the dawn'),(12606,3,2530,1886,'now he is telling all his wisdom to the kites but he told me everything before i broke his back'),(12607,3,202619,2224,'shere khans plan is to wait for thee at the village gate this evening--for thee and for no one else'),(12608,3,258,202653,'he is lying up now in the big dry ravine of the waingunga'),(12609,3,21,156302,'has he eaten today or does he hunt empty'),(12610,3,2257,247,'said mowgli for the answer meant life and death to him'),(12611,3,258,813,'he killed at dawn--a pig--and he has drunk too'),(12612,3,1830,29120,'remember shere khan could never fast even for the sake of revenge'),(12613,3,1276,1276,'fool fool'),(12614,3,994,75,'what a cubs cub it is'),(12615,3,179415,14240,'eaten and drunk too and he thinks that i shall wait till he has slept'),(12616,3,2530,1274,'now where does he lie up'),(12617,3,257,92780,'if there were but ten of us we might pull him down as he lies'),(12618,3,2457,1437,'these buffaloes will not charge unless they wind him and i cannot speak their language'),(12619,3,850,67,'can we get behind his track so that they may smell it'),(12620,3,258,8526,'he swam far down the waingunga to cut that off said gray brother'),(12621,3,202357,515,'tabaqui told him that i know'),(12622,3,258,8177,'he would never have thought of it alone'),(12623,3,204077,5089,'mowgli stood with his finger in his mouth thinking'),(12624,3,51,202653,'the big ravine of the waingunga'),(12625,3,163,454,'that opens out on the plain not half a mile from here'),(12626,3,279,104164,'i can take the herd round through the jungle to the head of the ravine and then sweep down--but he would slink out at the foot'),(12627,3,1649,1690,'we must block that end'),(12628,3,137255,424,'gray brother canst thou cut the herd in two for me'),(12629,3,55,222204,'not i perhaps--but i have brought a wise helper'),(12630,3,137255,144577,'gray brother trotted off and dropped into a hole'),(12631,3,1502,154182,'then there lifted up a huge gray head that mowgli knew well and the hot air was filled with the most desolate cry of all the jungle--the hunting howl of a wolf at midday'),(12632,3,2257,22498,'said mowgli clapping his hands'),(12633,3,279,424,'i might have known that thou wouldst not forget me'),(12634,3,1649,410,'we have a big work in hand'),(12635,3,134233,204273,'cut the herd in two akela'),(12636,3,5854,2601,'keep the cows and calves together and the bulls and the plow buffaloes by themselves'),(12637,3,51,148546,'the two wolves ran ladies-chain fashion in and out of the herd which snorted and threw up its head and separated into two clumps'),(12638,3,50,247,'in one the cow-buffaloes stood with their calves in the center and glared and pawed ready if a wolf would only stay still to charge down and trample the life out of him'),(12639,3,50,186064,'in the other the bulls and the young bulls snorted and stamped but though they looked more imposing they were much less dangerous for they had no calves to protect'),(12640,3,655,194449,'no six men could have divided the herd so neatly'),(12641,3,994,13783,'what orders'),(12642,3,133304,204273,'panted akela'),(12643,3,1056,3373,'they are trying to join again'),(12644,3,204077,1886,'mowgli slipped on to ramas back'),(12645,3,24882,204273,'drive the bulls away to the left akela'),(12646,3,137255,221381,'gray brother when we are gone hold the cows together and drive them into the foot of the ravine'),(12647,3,1338,987,'how far'),(12648,3,2257,158557,'said gray brother panting and snapping'),(12649,3,505,204077,'till the sides are higher than shere khan can jump shouted mowgli'),(12650,3,5854,4332,'keep them there till we come down'),(12651,3,51,55699,'the bulls swept off as akela bayed and gray brother stopped in front of the cows'),(12652,3,1056,1811,'they charged down on him and he ran just before them to the foot of the ravine as akela drove the bulls far to the left'),(12653,3,43,3873,'well done'),(12654,3,1351,24407,'another charge and they are fairly started'),(12655,3,22346,204273,'careful now--careful akela'),(12656,3,23,347,'a snap too much and the bulls will charge'),(12657,3,37,222527,'this is wilder work than driving black-buck'),(12658,3,212605,166062,'didst thou think these creatures could move so swiftly'),(12659,3,204077,1756,'mowgli called'),(12660,3,279,212480,'i have--have hunted these too in my time gasped akela in the dust'),(12661,3,5767,148066,'shall i turn them into the jungle'),(12662,3,166062,112,'swiftly turn them'),(12663,3,221106,76737,'rama is mad with rage'),(12664,3,5037,13310,'oh if i could only tell him what i need of him to-day'),(12665,3,51,154062,'the bulls were turned to the right this time and crashed into the standing thicket'),(12666,3,51,4025,'the other herd children watching with the cattle half a mile away hurried to the village as fast as their legs could carry them crying that the buffaloes had gone mad and run away'),(12667,3,295,566,'but mowglis plan was simple enough'),(12668,3,172,221381,'all he wanted to do was to make a big circle uphill and get at the head of the ravine and then take the bulls down it and catch shere khan between the bulls and the cows for he knew that after a meal and a full drink shere khan would not be in any condition to fight or to clamber up the sides of the ravine'),(12669,3,258,222726,'he was soothing the buffaloes now by voice and akela had dropped far to the rear only whimpering once or twice to hurry the rear-guard'),(12670,3,67,133466,'it was a long long circle for they did not wish to get too near the ravine and give shere khan warning'),(12671,3,308,339,'at last mowgli rounded up the bewildered herd at the head of the ravine on a grassy patch that sloped steeply down to the ravine itself'),(12672,3,344,976,'from that height you could see across the tops of the trees down to the plain below but what mowgli looked at was the sides of the ravine and he saw with a great deal of satisfaction that they ran nearly straight up and down while the vines and creepers that hung over them would give no foothold to a tiger who wanted to get out'),(12673,3,2792,410,'let them breathe akela he said holding up his hand'),(12674,3,1056,119,'they have not winded him yet'),(12675,3,2792,222843,'let them breathe'),(12676,3,279,3747,'i must tell shere khan who comes'),(12677,3,1649,170647,'we have him in the trap'),(12678,3,258,135748,'he put his hands to his mouth and shouted down the ravine--it was almost like shouting down a tunnel--and the echoes jumped from rock to rock'),(12679,3,171,222915,'after a long time there came back the drawling sleepy snarl of a full-fed tiger just wakened'),(12680,3,59,90935,'who calls'),(12681,3,2257,222931,'said shere khan and a splendid peacock fluttered up out of the ravine screeching'),(12682,3,279,204077,'i mowgli'),(12683,3,154091,135748,'cattle thief it is time to come to the council rock'),(12684,3,222945,204273,'down--hurry them down akela'),(12685,3,4332,4332,'down rama down'),(12686,3,51,112,'the herd paused for an instant at the edge of the slope but akela gave tongue in the full hunting-yell and they pitched over one after the other just as steamers shoot rapids the sand and stones spurting up round them'),(12687,3,855,134033,'once started there was no chance of stopping and before they were fairly in the bed of the ravine rama winded shere khan and bellowed'),(12688,3,2257,1886,'said mowgli on his back'),(12689,3,2530,210543,'now thou knowest'),(12690,3,1,178775,'and the torrent of black horns foaming muzzles and staring eyes whirled down the ravine just as boulders go down in floodtime the weaker buffaloes being shouldered out to the sides of the ravine where they tore through the creepers'),(12691,3,1056,6021,'they knew what the business was before them--the terrible charge of the buffalo herd against which no tiger can hope to stand'),(12692,3,202619,93834,'shere khan heard the thunder of their hoofs picked himself up and lumbered down the ravine looking from side to side for some way of escape but the walls of the ravine were straight and he had to hold on heavy with his dinner and his drink willing to do anything rather than fight'),(12693,3,51,39431,'the herd splashed through the pool he had just left bellowing till the narrow cut rang'),(12694,3,204077,3448,'mowgli heard an answering bellow from the foot of the ravine saw shere khan turn the tiger knew if the worst came to the worst it was better to meet the bulls than the cows with their calves and then rama tripped stumbled and went on again over something soft and with the bulls at his heels crashed full into the other herd while the weaker buffaloes were lifted clean off their feet by the shock of the meeting'),(12695,3,163,223250,'that charge carried both herds out into the plain goring and stamping and snorting'),(12696,3,204077,182208,'mowgli watched his time and slipped off ramas neck laying about him right and left with his stick'),(12697,3,5571,204273,'quick akela'),(12698,3,32511,1274,'break them up'),(12699,3,221207,1351,'scatter them or they will be fighting one another'),(12700,3,24882,204273,'drive them away akela'),(12701,3,223287,221106,'hai rama'),(12702,3,223287,223287,'hai hai hai'),(12703,3,413,5449,'my children'),(12704,3,173697,173697,'softly now softly'),(127