toomai held high in the air above his head and shouted listen my brothers'),(13919,3,4723,3403,'listen too you my lords in the lines there for i machua appa am speaking'),(13920,3,37,247,'this little one shall no more be called little toomai but toomai of the elephants as his great-grandfather was called before him'),(13921,3,994,247,'what never man has seen he has seen through the long night and the favor of the elephant-folk and of the gods of the jungles is with him'),(13922,3,258,241400,'he shall become a great tracker'),(13923,3,258,241397,'he shall become greater than i even i machua appa'),(13924,3,258,902,'he shall follow the new trail and the stale trail and the mixed trail with a clear eye'),(13925,3,258,247,'he shall take no harm in the keddah when he runs under their bellies to rope the wild tuskers and if he slips before the feet of the charging bull elephant the bull elephant shall know who he is and shall not crush him'),(13926,3,413,6674,'my lords in the chains--he whirled up the line of pickets--here is the little one that has seen your dances in your hidden places--the sight that never man saw'),(13927,3,2786,245976,'give him honor my lords'),(13928,3,246149,5449,'salaam karo my children'),(13929,3,1665,136772,'make your salute to toomai of the elephants'),(13930,3,246161,246163,'gunga pershad ahaa'),(13931,3,246164,246163,'hira guj birchi guj kuttar guj ahaa'),(13932,3,246171,136772,'pudmini--thou hast seen him at the dance and thou too kala nag my pearl among elephants'),(13933,3,40,136772,'to toomai of the elephants'),(13934,3,1,240661,'and at that last wild yell the whole line flung up their trunks till the tips touched their foreheads and broke out into the full salute--the crashing trumpet-peal that only the viceroy of india hears the salaamut of the keddah'),(13935,3,295,80090,'but it was all for the sake of little toomai who had seen what never man had seen before--the dance of the elephants at night and alone in the heart of the garo hills'),(13936,3,242766,53115,'shiv and the grasshopper the song that toomais mother sang to the baby shiv who poured the harvest and made the winds to blow sitting at the doorways of a day of long ago gave to each his portion food and toil and fate from the king upon the guddee to the beggar at the gate'),(13937,3,172,242836,'all things made he--shiva the preserver'),(13938,3,258,9989,'he made all-- thorn for the camel fodder for the kine and mothers heart for sleepy head o little son of mine'),(13939,3,228225,9700,'wheat he gave to rich folk millet to the poor broken scraps for holy men that beg from door to door battle to the tiger carrion to the kite and rags and bones to wicked wolves without the wall at night'),(13940,3,246391,33595,'naught he found too lofty none he saw too low-- parbati beside him watched them come and go thought to cheat her husband turning shiv to jest-- stole the little grasshopper and hid it in her breast'),(13941,3,1051,242836,'so she tricked him shiva the preserver'),(13942,3,11455,4334,'turn and see'),(13943,3,3641,9989,'tall are the camels heavy are the kine but this was least of little things o little son of mine'),(13944,3,483,246474,'when the dole was ended laughingly she said master of a million mouths is not one unfed'),(13945,3,37476,1308,'laughing shiv made answer all have had their part even he the little one hidden neath thy heart'),(13946,3,344,184589,'from her breast she plucked it parbati the thief saw the least of little things gnawed a new-grown leaf'),(13947,3,6674,1517,'saw and feared and wondered making prayer to shiv who hath surely given meat to all that live'),(13948,3,172,242836,'all things made he--shiva the preserver'),(13949,3,258,9989,'he made all-- thorn for the camel fodder for the kine and mothers heart for sleepy head o little son of mine'),(13950,3,382,246584,'her majestys servants you can work it out by fractions or by simple rule of three but the way of tweedle-dum is not the way of tweedle-dee'),(13951,3,2074,246611,'you can twist it you can turn it you can plait it till you drop but the way of pilly winkys not the way of winkie pop'),(13952,3,67,128504,'it had been raining heavily for one whole month--raining on a camp of thirty thousand men and thousands of camels elephants horses bullocks and mules all gathered together at a place called rawal pindi to be reviewed by the viceroy of india'),(13953,3,258,1878,'he was receiving a visit from the amir of afghanistan--a wild king of a very wild country'),(13954,3,51,246709,'the amir had brought with him for a bodyguard eight hundred men and horses who had never seen a camp or a locomotive before in their lives--savage men and savage horses from somewhere at the back of central asia'),(13955,3,138,20721,'every night a mob of these horses would be sure to break their heel ropes and stampede up and down the camp through the mud in the dark or the camels would break loose and run about and fall over the ropes of the tents and you can imagine how pleasant that was for men trying to go to sleep'),(13956,3,413,10499,'my tent lay far away from the camel lines and i thought it was safe'),(13957,3,295,5571,'but one night a man popped his head in and shouted get out quick'),(13958,3,229084,14215,'theyre coming'),(13959,3,413,6285,'my tents gone'),(13960,3,279,246822,'i knew who they were so i put on my boots and waterproof and scuttled out into the slush'),(13961,3,262,220750,'little vixen my fox terrier went out through the other side and then there was a roaring and a grunting and bubbling and i saw the tent cave in as the pole snapped and begin to dance about like a mad ghost'),(13962,3,23,37476,'a camel had blundered into it and wet and angry as i was i could not help laughing'),(13963,3,1502,14903,'then i ran on because i did not know how many camels might have got loose and before long i was out of sight of the camp plowing my way through the mud'),(13964,3,308,9700,'at last i fell over the tail-end of a gun and by that knew i was somewhere near the artillery lines where the cannon were stacked at night'),(13965,3,42,104,'as i did not want to plowter about any more in the drizzle and the dark i put my waterproof over the muzzle of one gun and made a sort of wigwam with two or three rammers that i found and lay along the tail of another gun wondering where vixen had got to and where i might be'),(13966,3,26,132843,'just as i was getting ready to go to sleep i heard a jingle of harness and a grunt and a mule passed me shaking his wet ears'),(13967,3,258,239181,'he belonged to a screw-gun battery for i could hear the rattle of the straps and rings and chains and things on his saddle pad'),(13968,3,51,112,'the screw-guns are tiny little cannon made in two pieces that are screwed together when the time comes to use them'),(13969,3,1056,1878,'they are taken up mountains anywhere that a mule can find a road and they are very useful for fighting in rocky country'),(13970,3,7459,247128,'behind the mule there was a camel with his big soft feet squelching and slipping in the mud and his neck bobbing to and fro like a strayed hens'),(13971,3,45546,11564,'luckily i knew enough of beast language--not wild-beast language but camp-beast language of course--from the natives to know what he was saying'),(13972,3,258,513,'he must have been the one that flopped into my tent for he called to the mule what shall i do'),(13973,3,4187,351,'where shall i go'),(13974,3,279,156026,'i have fought with a white thing that waved and it took a stick and hit me on the neck'),(13975,3,163,67,'that was my broken tent pole and i was very glad to know it'),(13976,3,5767,407,'shall we run on'),(13977,3,5037,77102,'oh it was you said the mule you and your friends that have been disturbing the camp'),(13978,3,172,106,'all right'),(13979,3,104155,6169,'youll be beaten for this in the morning'),(13980,3,295,2530,'but i may as well give you something on account now'),(13981,3,279,151733,'i heard the harness jingle as the mule backed and caught the camel two kicks in the ribs that rang like a drum'),(13982,3,1351,2140,'another time he said youll know better than to run through a mule battery at night shouting thieves and fire'),(13983,3,7250,53992,'sit down and keep your silly neck quiet'),(13984,3,51,222719,'the camel doubled up camel-fashion like a two-foot rule and sat down whimpering'),(13985,3,74,247024,'there was a regular beat of hoofs in the darkness and a big troop-horse cantered up as steadily as though he were on parade jumped a gun tail and landed close to the mule'),(13986,3,153,142239,'its disgraceful he said blowing out his nostrils'),(13987,3,89,5027,'those camels have racketed through our lines again--the third time this week'),(13988,3,247370,20721,'hows a horse to keep his condition if he isnt allowed to sleep'),(13989,3,247383,454,'whos here'),(13990,3,4348,5485,'im the breech-piece mule of number two gun of the first screw battery said the mule and the others one of your friends'),(13991,3,228518,813,'hes waked me up too'),(13992,3,59,2074,'who are you'),(13993,3,1170,4355,'number fifteen e troop ninth lancers--dick cunliffes horse'),(13994,3,6021,74,'stand over a little there'),(13995,3,5037,247024,'oh beg your pardon said the mule'),(13996,3,153,76,'its too dark to see much'),(13997,3,247442,2241,'arent these camels too sickening for anything'),(13998,3,279,454,'i walked out of my lines to get a little peace and quiet here'),(13999,3,413,6416,'my lords said the camel humbly we dreamed bad dreams in the night and we were very much afraid'),(14000,3,279,245976,'i am only a baggage camel of the th native infantry and i am not as brave as you are my lords'),(14001,3,1502,77102,'then why didnt you stay and carry baggage for the th native infantry instead of running all round the camp'),(14002,3,2257,247024,'said the mule'),(14003,3,1056,242843,'they were such very bad dreams said the camel'),(14004,3,279,6149,'i am sorry'),(14005,3,994,163,'what is that'),(14006,3,5767,3373,'shall we run on again'),(14007,3,7250,159396,'sit down said the mule or youll snap your long stick-legs between the guns'),(14008,3,258,8548,'he cocked one ear and listened'),(14009,3,258,2257,'he said'),(14010,3,144882,202899,'gun bullocks'),(14011,3,407,3506,'on my word you and your friends have waked the camp very thoroughly'),(14012,3,67,247595,'it takes a good deal of prodding to put up a gun-bullock'),(14013,3,279,10658,'i heard a chain dragging along the ground and a yoke of the great sulky white bullocks that drag the heavy siege guns when the elephants wont go any nearer to the firing came shouldering along together'),(14014,3,1,247646,'and almost stepping on the chain was another battery mule calling wildly for billy'),(14015,3,228305,4355,'thats one of our recruits said the old mule to the troop horse'),(14016,3,228518,424,'hes calling for me'),(14017,3,454,202270,'here youngster stop squealing'),(14018,3,51,119,'the dark never hurt anybody yet'),(14019,3,51,247646,'the gun-bullocks lay down together and began chewing the cud but the young mule huddled close to billy'),(14020,3,258,2257,'he said'),(14021,3,22155,247646,'fearful and horrible billy'),(14022,3,1056,15299,'they came into our lines while we were asleep'),(14023,3,247707,2793,'dyou think theyll kill us'),(14024,3,226572,247646,'ive a very great mind to give you a number-one kicking said billy'),(14025,3,51,6961,'the idea of a fourteen-hand mule with your training disgracing the battery before this gentleman'),(14026,3,171695,171695,'gently gently'),(14027,3,2257,247329,'said the troop-horse'),(14028,3,1830,34,'remember they are always like this to begin with'),(14029,3,51,1040,'the first time i ever saw a man it was in australia when i was a three-year-old i ran for half a day and if id seen a camel i should have been running still'),(14030,3,1486,2601,'nearly all our horses for the english cavalry are brought to india from australia and are broken in by the troopers themselves'),(14031,3,2011,247646,'true enough said billy'),(14032,3,37491,173498,'stop shaking youngster'),(14033,3,51,891,'the first time they put the full harness with all its chains on my back i stood on my forelegs and kicked every bit of it off'),(14034,3,279,67,'i hadnt learned the real science of kicking then but the battery said they had never seen anything like it'),(14035,3,295,247024,'but this wasnt harness or anything that jingled said the young mule'),(14036,3,2074,247646,'you know i dont mind that now billy'),(14037,3,67,4570,'it was things like trees and they fell up and down the lines and bubbled and my head-rope broke and i couldnt find my driver and i couldnt find you billy so i ran off with--with these gentlemen'),(14038,3,2257,247646,'said billy'),(14039,3,42,743,'as soon as i heard the camels were loose i came away on my own account'),(14040,3,483,1274,'when a battery--a screw-gun mule calls gun-bullocks gentlemen he must be very badly shaken up'),(14041,3,59,74,'who are you fellows on the ground there'),(14042,3,51,130400,'the gun bullocks rolled their cuds and answered both together the seventh yoke of the first gun of the big gun battery'),(14043,3,1649,4025,'we were asleep when the camels came but when we were trampled on we got up and walked away'),(14044,3,67,248020,'it is better to lie quiet in the mud than to be disturbed on good bedding'),(14045,3,1649,699,'we told your friend here that there was nothing to be afraid of but he knew so much that he thought otherwise'),(14046,3,1056,247681,'they went on chewing'),(14047,3,163,247646,'that comes of being afraid said billy'),(14048,3,2074,247675,'you get laughed at by gun-bullocks'),(14049,3,279,248066,'i hope you like it young un'),(14050,3,51,378,'the young mules teeth snapped and i heard him say something about not being afraid of any beefy old bullock in the world'),(14051,3,295,247681,'but the bullocks only clicked their horns together and went on chewing'),(14052,3,2530,6416,'now dont be angry after youve been afraid'),(14053,3,228305,247329,'thats the worst kind of cowardice said the troop-horse'),(14054,3,1833,5591,'anybody can be forgiven for being scared in the night i think if they see things they dont understand'),(14055,3,218723,248181,'weve broken out of our pickets again and again four hundred and fifty of us just because a new recruit got to telling tales of whip snakes at home in australia till we were scared to death of the loose ends of our head-ropes'),(14056,3,228305,247646,'thats all very well in camp said billy'),(14057,3,4348,1024,'im not above stampeding myself for the fun of the thing when i havent been out for a day or two'),(14058,3,295,16362,'but what do you do on active service'),(14059,3,5037,4355,'oh thats quite another set of new shoes said the troop horse'),(14060,3,248231,248269,'dick cunliffes on my back then and drives his knees into me and all i have to do is to watch where i am putting my feet and to keep my hind legs well under me and be bridle-wise'),(14061,3,109115,248269,'whats bridle-wise'),(14062,3,2257,247024,'said the young mule'),(14063,3,3,661,'by the blue gums of the back blocks snorted the troop-horse do you mean to say that you arent taught to be bridle-wise in your business'),(14064,3,1338,156026,'how can you do anything unless you can spin round at once when the rein is pressed on your neck'),(14065,3,67,2074,'it means life or death to your man and of course thats life and death to you'),(14066,3,2075,156026,'get round with your hind legs under you the instant you feel the rein on your neck'),(14067,3,257,133223,'if you havent room to swing round rear up a little and come round on your hind legs'),(14068,3,228305,248269,'thats being bridle-wise'),(14069,3,1649,114887,'we arent taught that way said billy the mule stiffly'),(14070,3,798,1051,'were taught to obey the man at our head step off when he says so and step in when he says so'),(14071,3,279,109,'i suppose it comes to the same thing'),(14072,3,2530,513,'now with all this fine fancy business and rearing which must be very bad for your hocks what do you do'),(14073,3,163,247329,'that depends said the troop-horse'),(14074,3,1618,67,'generally i have to go in among a lot of yelling hairy men with knives--long shiny knives worse than the farriers knives--and i have to take care that dicks boot is just touching the next mans boot without crushing it'),(14075,3,279,10499,'i can see dicks lance to the right of my right eye and i know im safe'),(14076,3,279,16900,'i shouldnt care to be the man or horse that stood up to dick and me when were in a hurry'),(14077,3,5853,39767,'dont the knives hurt'),(14078,3,2257,247024,'said the young mule'),(14079,3,43,39767,'well i got one cut across the chest once but that wasnt dicks fault-- a lot i should have cared whose fault it was if it hurt'),(14080,3,2257,247024,'said the young mule'),(14081,3,2074,4355,'you must said the troop horse'),(14082,3,257,855,'if you dont trust your man you may as well run away at once'),(14083,3,228305,112,'thats what some of our horses do and i dont blame them'),(14084,3,42,8377,'as i was saying it wasnt dicks fault'),(14085,3,51,424,'the man was lying on the ground and i stretched myself not to tread on him and he slashed up at me'),(14086,3,5026,248637,'next time i have to go over a man lying down i shall step on him--hard'),(14087,3,2257,247646,'said billy'),(14088,3,67,13077,'it sounds very foolish'),(14089,3,163858,369,'knives are dirty things at any time'),(14090,3,51,223095,'the proper thing to do is to climb up a mountain with a well-balanced saddle hang on by all four feet and your ears too and creep and crawl and wriggle along till you come out hundreds of feet above anyone else on a ledge where theres just room enough for your hoofs'),(14091,3,1502,15363,'then you stand still and keep quiet--never ask a man to hold your head young un--keep quiet while the guns are being put together and then you watch the little poppy shells drop down into the tree-tops