698,7312,'you\'ll have to play about and look after yourself'),(14731,4,127698,53,'you\'ll be told what rooms you can go into and what rooms you\'re to keep out of'),(14732,4,129216,566,'there\'s gardens enough'),(14733,4,295,482,'but when you\'re in the house don\'t go wandering and poking about'),(14734,4,255382,67,'craven won\'t have it'),(14735,4,279,636,'i shall not want to go poking about said sour little mary and just as suddenly as she had begun to be rather sorry for mr'),(14736,4,255381,247,'archibald craven she began to cease to be sorry and to think he was unpleasant enough to deserve all that had happened to him'),(14737,4,1,784,'and she turned her face toward the streaming panes of the window of the railway carriage and gazed out at the gray rain-storm which looked as if it would go on forever and ever'),(14738,4,729,15299,'she watched it so long and steadily that the grayness grew heavier and heavier before her eyes and she fell asleep'),(14739,4,4328,273,'chapter iii across the moor she slept a long time and when she awakened mrs'),(14740,4,255890,20644,'medlock had bought a lunchbasket at one of the stations and they had some chicken and cold beef and bread and butter and some hot tea'),(14741,4,51,257578,'the rain seemed to be streaming down more heavily than ever and everybody in the station wore wet and glistening waterproofs'),(14742,4,51,273,'the guard lighted the lamps in the carriage and mrs'),(14743,4,255890,185686,'medlock cheered up very much over her tea and chicken and beef'),(14744,4,729,26928,'she ate a great deal and afterward fell asleep herself and mary sat and stared at her and watched her fine bonnet slip on one side until she herself fell asleep once more in the corner of the carriage lulled by the splashing of the rain against the windows'),(14745,4,67,3373,'it was quite dark when she awakened again'),(14746,4,51,273,'the train had stopped at a station and mrs'),(14747,4,255890,382,'medlock was shaking her'),(14748,4,2074,20721,'you have had a sleep'),(14749,4,729,2257,'she said'),(14750,4,127528,917,'it\'s time to open your eyes'),(14751,4,145707,2793,'we\'re at thwaite station and we\'ve got a long drive before us'),(14752,4,245,273,'mary stood up and tried to keep her eyes open while mrs'),(14753,4,255890,72080,'medlock collected her parcels'),(14754,4,51,139,'the little girl did not offer to help her because in india native servants always picked up or carried things and it seemed quite proper that other people should wait on one'),(14755,4,51,35120,'the station was a small one and nobody but themselves seemed to be getting out of the train'),(14756,4,51,273,'the station-master spoke to mrs'),(14757,4,255890,256232,'medlock in a rough good-natured way pronouncing his words in a queer broad fashion which mary found out afterward was yorkshire'),(14758,4,279,2257,'i see tha\'s got back he said'),(14759,4,144004,203807,'an\' tha\'s browt th\' young \'un with thee'),(14760,4,257803,273,'aye that\'s her answered mrs'),(14761,4,255890,245,'medlock speaking with a yorkshire accent herself and jerking her head over her shoulder toward mary'),(14762,4,131032,257826,'how\'s thy missus'),(14763,4,43,257828,'well enow'),(14764,4,257798,203807,'th\' carriage is waitin\' outside for thee'),(14765,4,23,138631,'a brougham stood on the road before the little outside platform'),(14766,4,245,50,'mary saw that it was a smart carriage and that it was a smart footman who helped her in'),(14767,4,892,27138,'his long waterproof coat and the waterproof covering of his hat were shining and dripping with rain as everything was the burly station-master included'),(14768,4,483,3373,'when he shut the door mounted the box with the coachman and they drove off the little girl found herself seated in a comfortably cushioned corner but she was not inclined to go to sleep again'),(14769,4,729,273,'she sat and looked out of the window curious to see something of the road over which she was being driven to the queer place mrs'),(14770,4,255890,53,'medlock had spoken of'),(14771,4,729,252796,'she was not at all a timid child and she was not exactly frightened but she felt that there was no knowing what might happen in a house with a hundred rooms nearly all shut up--a house standing on the edge of a moor'),(14772,4,994,252796,'what is a moor'),(14773,4,729,273,'she said suddenly to mrs'),(14774,4,2046,13526,'look out of the window in about ten minutes and you\'ll see the woman answered'),(14775,4,143977,8774,'we\'ve got to drive five miles across missel moor before we get to the manor'),(14776,4,2074,707,'you won\'t see much because it\'s a dark night but you can see something'),(14777,4,245,6694,'mary asked no more questions but waited in the darkness of her corner keeping her eyes on the window'),(14778,4,51,7605,'the carriage lamps cast rays of light a little distance ahead of them and she caught glimpses of the things they passed'),(14779,4,171,1889,'after they had left the station they had driven through a tiny village and she had seen whitewashed cottages and the lights of a public house'),(14780,4,1502,258148,'then they had passed a church and a vicarage and a little shop-window or so in a cottage with toys and sweets and odd things set out for sale'),(14781,4,1502,53546,'then they were on the highroad and she saw hedges and trees'),(14782,4,171,382,'after that there seemed nothing different for a long time--or at least it seemed a long time to her'),(14783,4,308,53546,'at last the horses began to go more slowly as if they were climbing up-hill and presently there seemed to be no more hedges and no more trees'),(14784,4,729,1473,'she could see nothing in fact but a dense darkness on either side'),(14785,4,729,258238,'she leaned forward and pressed her face against the window just as the carriage gave a big jolt'),(14786,4,145707,273,'we\'re on the moor now sure enough said mrs'),(14787,4,51,112,'the carriage lamps shed a yellow light on a rough-looking road which seemed to be cut through bushes and low growing things which ended in the great expanse of dark apparently spread out before and around them'),(14788,4,23,11034,'a wind was rising and making a singular wild low rushing sound'),(14789,4,258297,67,'it\'s--it\'s not the sea is it'),(14790,4,2257,11994,'said mary looking round at her companion'),(14791,4,655,273,'no not it answered mrs'),(14792,4,5398,227106,'nor it isn\'t fields nor mountains it\'s just miles and miles and miles of wild land that nothing grows on but heather and gorse and broom and nothing lives on but wild ponies and sheep'),(14793,4,279,245,'i feel as if it might be the sea if there were water on it said mary'),(14794,4,67,2530,'it sounds like the sea just now'),(14795,4,128237,273,'that\'s the wind blowing through the bushes mrs'),(14796,4,255890,2257,'medlock said'),(14797,4,127528,190939,'it\'s a wild dreary enough place to my mind though there\'s plenty that likes it--particularly when the heather\'s in bloom'),(14798,4,407,100118,'on and on they drove through the darkness and though the rain stopped the wind rushed by and whistled and made strange sounds'),(14799,4,51,30389,'the road went up and down and several times the carriage passed over a little bridge beneath which water rushed very fast with a great deal of noise'),(14800,4,245,118264,'mary felt as if the drive would never come to an end and that the wide bleak moor was a wide expanse of black ocean through which she was passing on a strip of dry land'),(14801,4,279,1755,'i don\'t like it she said to herself'),(14802,4,279,10658,'i don\'t like it and she pinched her thin lips more tightly together'),(14803,4,51,11189,'the horses were climbing up a hilly piece of road when she first caught sight of a light'),(14804,4,255890,16441,'medlock saw it as soon as she did and drew a long sigh of relief'),(14805,4,129268,25251,'eh i am glad to see that bit o\' light twinkling she exclaimed'),(14806,4,127528,6694,'it\'s the light in the lodge window'),(14807,4,1649,51712,'we shall get a good cup of tea after a bit at all events'),(14808,4,67,258625,'it was after a bit as she said for when the carriage passed through the park gates there was still two miles of avenue to drive through and the trees which nearly met overhead made it seem as if they were driving through a long dark vault'),(14809,4,1056,24907,'they drove out of the vault into a clear space and stopped before an immensely long but low-built house which seemed to ramble round a stone court'),(14810,4,308,33987,'at first mary thought that there were no lights at all in the windows but as she got out of the carriage she saw that one room in a corner up-stairs showed a dull glow'),(14811,4,51,206608,'the entrance door was a huge one made of massive curiously shaped panels of oak studded with big iron nails and bound with great iron bars'),(14812,4,67,112,'it opened into an enormous hall which was so dimly lighted that the faces in the portraits on the walls and the figures in the suits of armor made mary feel that she did not want to look at them'),(14813,4,42,4135,'as she stood on the stone floor she looked a very small odd little black figure and she felt as small and lost and odd as she looked'),(14814,4,23,112,'a neat thin old man stood near the manservant who opened the door for them'),(14815,4,2074,4517,'you are to take her to her room he said in a husky voice'),(14816,4,258,382,'he doesn\'t want to see her'),(14817,4,129787,6169,'he\'s going to london in the morning'),(14818,4,56,636,'very well mr'),(14819,4,257183,273,'pitcher mrs'),(14820,4,255890,13526,'medlock answered'),(14821,4,1051,30532,'so long as i know what\'s expected of me i can manage'),(14822,4,127755,273,'what\'s expected of you mrs'),(14823,4,255890,636,'medlock mr'),(14824,4,257183,4334,'pitcher said is that you make sure that he\'s not disturbed and that he doesn\'t see what he doesn\'t want to see'),(14825,4,1,8843,'and then mary lennox was led up a broad staircase and down a long corridor and up a short flight of steps and through another corridor and another until a door opened in a wall and she found herself in a room with a fire in it and a supper on a table'),(14826,4,255890,60,'medlock said unceremoniously well here you are'),(14827,4,37,112,'this room and the next are where you\'ll live--and you must keep to them'),(14828,4,127385,163,'don\'t you forget that'),(14829,4,67,534,'it was in this way mistress mary arrived at misselthwaite manor and she had perhaps never felt quite so contrary in all her life'),(14830,4,4328,259010,'chapter iv martha when she opened her eyes in the morning it was because a young housemaid had come into her room to light the fire and was kneeling on the hearth-rug raking out the cinders noisily'),(14831,4,245,6317,'mary lay and watched her for a few moments and then began to look about the room'),(14832,4,729,2790,'she had never seen a room at all like it and thought it curious and gloomy'),(14833,4,51,67,'the walls were covered with tapestry with a forest scene embroidered on it'),(14834,4,74,221657,'there were fantastically dressed people under the trees and in the distance there was a glimpse of the turrets of a castle'),(14835,4,74,1884,'there were hunters and horses and dogs and ladies'),(14836,4,245,112,'mary felt as if she were in the forest with them'),(14837,4,976,139268,'out of a deep window she could see a great climbing stretch of land which seemed to have no trees on it and to look rather like an endless dull purplish sea'),(14838,4,994,163,'what is that'),(14839,4,729,6694,'she said pointing out of the window'),(14840,4,252798,1187,'martha the young housemaid who had just risen to her feet looked and pointed also'),(14841,4,163,74,'that there'),(14842,4,729,2257,'she said'),(14843,4,128237,259166,'that\'s th\' moor with a good-natured grin'),(14844,4,297,67,'does tha\' like it'),(14845,4,655,245,'no answered mary'),(14846,4,279,67,'i hate it'),(14847,4,128237,113139,'that\'s because tha\'rt not used to it martha said going back to her hearth'),(14848,4,252871,2530,'tha\' thinks it\'s too big an\' bare now'),(14849,4,295,67,'but tha\' will like it'),(14850,4,513,2074,'do you'),(14851,4,7919,245,'inquired mary'),(14852,4,257803,259219,'aye that i do answered martha cheerfully polishing away at the grate'),(14853,4,279,67,'i just love it'),(14854,4,127528,12740,'it\'s none bare'),(14855,4,127528,9190,'it\'s covered wi\' growin\' things as smells sweet'),(14856,4,127528,165943,'it\'s fair lovely in spring an\' summer when th\' gorse an\' broom an\' heather\'s in flower'),(14857,4,67,180705,'it smells o\' honey an\' there\'s such a lot o\' fresh air--an\' th\' sky looks so high an\' th\' bees an\' skylarks makes such a nice noise hummin\' an\' singin\''),(14858,4,279,145486,'i wouldn\'t live away from th\' moor for anythin\''),(14859,4,245,3355,'mary listened to her with a grave puzzled expression'),(14860,4,51,37,'the native servants she had been used to in india were not in the least like this'),(14861,4,1056,29829,'they were obsequious and servile and did not presume to talk to their masters as if they were their equals'),(14862,4,1056,15031,'they made salaams and called them protector of the poor and names of that sort'),(14863,4,134966,7867,'indian servants were commanded to do things not asked'),(14864,4,67,7118,'it was not the custom to say please and thank you and mary had always slapped her ayah in the face when she was angry'),(14865,4,729,11135,'she wondered a little what this girl would do if one slapped her in the face'),(14866,4,729,1873,'she was a round rosy good-natured looking creature but she had a sturdy way which made mistress mary wonder if she might not even slap back--if the person who slapped her was only a little girl'),(14867,4,2074,259448,'you are a strange servant she said from her pillows rather haughtily'),(14868,4,252798,5612,'martha sat up on her heels with her blacking-brush in her hand and laughed without seeming the least out of temper'),(14869,4,279,2257,'i know that she said'),(14870,4,257,103428,'if there was a grand missus at misselthwaite i should never have been even one of th\' under housemaids'),(14871,4,279,258683,'i might have been let to be scullery-maid but i\'d never have been let up-stairs'),(14872,4,127759,256232,'i\'m too common an\' i talk too much yorkshire'),(14873,4,295,41982,'but this is a funny house for all it\'s so grand'),(14874,4,422,636,'seems like there\'s neither master nor mistress except mr'),(14875,4,257183,273,'pitcher an\' mrs'),(14876,4,255382,4025,'craven he won\'t be troubled about anythin\' when he\'s here an\' he\'s nearly always away'),(14877,4,255890,6034,'medlock gave me th\' place out o\' kindness'),(14878,4,729,44366,'she told me she could never have done it if misselthwaite had been like other big houses'),(14879,4,60,13240,'are you going to be my servant'),(14880,4,245,608,'mary asked still in her imperious little indian way'),(14881,4,252798,3373,'martha began to rub her grate again'),(14882,4,127759,273,'i\'m mrs'),(14883,4,259606,6412,'medlock\'s servant she said stoutly'),(14884,4,144004,636,'an\' she\'s mr'),(14885,4,259614,5381,'craven\'s--but i\'m to do the housemaid\'s work up here an\' wait on you a bit'),(14886,4,295,407,'but you won\'t need much waitin\' on'),(14887,4,59,424,'who is going to dress me'),(14888,4,259642,245,'demanded mary'),(14889,4,252798,6048,'martha sat up on her heels again and stared'),(14890,4,729,32476,'she spoke in broad yorkshire in her amazement'),(14891,4,259661,259664,'canna\' tha\' dress thysen'),(14892,4,729,2257,'she said'),(14893,4,994,1577,'what do you mean'),(14894,4,279,245,'i don\'t understand your language said mary'),(14895,4,279,2257,'i forgot martha said'),(14896,4,255890,181535,'medlock told me i\'d have to be careful or you wouldn\'t know what i was sayin\''),(14897,4,279,14533,'i mean can\'t you put on your own clothes'),(14898,4,655,186694,'no answered mary quite indignantly'),(14899,4,279,534,'i never did in my life'),(14900,4,413,611,'my ayah dressed me of course'),(14901,4,43,3031,'well said martha evidently not in the least aware that she was impudent it\'s time tha\' should learn'),(14902,4,252871,10238,'tha\' cannot begin younger'),(14903,4,259746,5381,'it\'ll do thee good to wait on thysen a bit'),(14904,4,413,140168,'my mother always said she couldn\'t see why grand people\'s children didn\'t turn out fair fools--what with nurses an\' bein\' washed an\' dressed an\' took out to walk as if they was puppies'),(14905,4,67,259797,'it is different in india said mistress mary disdainfully'),(14906,4,729,37,'she could scarcely stand this'),(14907,4,295,163284,'but martha was not at all crushed'),(14908,4,279,141606,'i can see it\'s different she answered almost sympathetically'),(14909,4,279,7105,'i dare say it\'s because there\'s such a lot o\' blacks there instead o\' respectable white people'),(14910,4,483,813,'when i heard you was comin\' from india i thought you was a black too'),(14911,4,245,133600,'mary sat up in bed furious'),(14912,4,729,2257,'she said'),(14913,4,2074,149071,'you thought i was a native'),(14914,4,259865,170415,'you--you daughter of a pig'),(14915,4,252798,27012,'martha stared and looked hot'),(14916,4,59,4240,'who are you callin\' names'),(14917,4,729,2257,'she said'),(14918,4,2074,7881,'you needn\'t be so vexed'),(14919,4,128237,5107,'that\'s not th\' way for a young lady to talk'),(14920,4,127793,259829,'i\'ve nothin\' against th\' blacks'),(14921,4,483,259912,'when you read about \'em in tracts they\'re always very religious'),(14922,4,2074,8526,'you always read as a black\'s a man an\' a brother'),(14923,4,127793,9704,'i\'ve never seen a black an\' i was fair pleased to think i was goin\' to see one close'),(14924,4,483,2074,'when i come in to light your fire this mornin\' i crep\' up to your bed an\' pulled th\' cover back careful to look at you'),(14925,4,144004,259982,'an\' there you was disappointedly no more black than me