 mary at last decided to get up the clothes martha took from the wardrobe were not the ones she had worn when she arrived the night before with mrs'),(14952,4,89,2257,'those are not mine she said'),(14953,4,9989,1969,'mine are black'),(14954,4,729,9989,'she looked the thick white wool coat and dress over and added with cool approval those are nicer than mine'),(14955,4,2457,13526,'these are th\' ones tha\' must put on martha answered'),(14956,4,255382,273,'craven ordered mrs'),(14957,4,255890,6833,'medlock to get \'em in london'),(14958,4,258,2257,'he said \'i won\'t have a child dressed in black wanderin\' about like a lost soul\' he said'),(14959,4,260327,75,'\'it\'d make the place sadder than it is'),(14960,4,983,382,'put color on her'),(14961,4,5751,6559,'mother she said she knew what he meant'),(14962,4,5751,920,'mother always knows what a body means'),(14963,4,729,260359,'she doesn\'t hold with black hersel\''),(14964,4,279,245,'i hate black things said mary'),(14965,4,51,707,'the dressing process was one which taught them both something'),(14966,4,252798,764,'martha had buttoned up her little sisters and brothers but she had never seen a child who stood still and waited for another person to do things for her as if she had neither hands nor feet of her own'),(14967,4,4949,1901,'why doesn\'t tha\' put on tha\' own shoes'),(14968,4,729,104164,'she said when mary quietly held out her foot'),(14969,4,413,128946,'my ayah did it answered mary staring'),(14970,4,67,1202,'it was the custom'),(14971,4,729,1202,'she said that very often--it was the custom'),(14972,4,51,67,'the native servants were always saying it'),(14973,4,257,6358,'if one told them to do a thing their ancestors had not done for a thousand years they gazed at one mildly and said it is not the custom and one knew that was the end of the matter'),(14974,4,67,3136,'it had not been the custom that mistress mary should do anything but stand and allow herself to be dressed like a doll but before she was ready for breakfast she began to suspect that her life at misselthwaite manor would end by teaching her a number of things quite new to her--things such as putting on her own shoes and stockings and picking up things she let fall'),(14975,4,257,4025,'if martha had been a well-trained fine young lady\'s maid she would have been more subservient and respectful and would have known that it was her business to brush hair and button boots and pick things up and lay them away'),(14976,4,729,314,'she was however only an untrained yorkshire rustic who had been brought up in a moorland cottage with a swarm of little brothers and sisters who had never dreamed of doing anything but waiting on themselves and on the younger ones who were either babies in arms or just learning to totter about and tumble over things'),(14977,4,257,3728,'if mary lennox had been a child who was ready to be amused she would perhaps have laughed at martha\'s readiness to talk but mary only listened to her coldly and wondered at her freedom of manner'),(14978,4,308,11564,'at first she was not at all interested but gradually as the girl rattled on in her good-tempered homely way mary began to notice what she was saying'),(14979,4,2074,2257,'you should see \'em all she said'),(14980,4,129216,5027,'there\'s twelve of us an\' my father only gets sixteen shilling a week'),(14981,4,279,172,'i can tell you my mother\'s put to it to get porridge for \'em all'),(14982,4,1056,179730,'they tumble about on th\' moor an\' play there all day an\' mother says th\' air of th\' moor fattens \'em'),(14983,4,729,513,'she says she believes they eat th\' grass same as th\' wild ponies do'),(14984,4,2043,764,'our dickon he\'s twelve years old and he\'s got a young pony he calls his own'),(14985,4,4187,67,'where did he get it'),(14986,4,7867,245,'asked mary'),(14987,4,258,67,'he found it on th\' moor with its mother when it was a little one an\' he began to make friends with it an\' give it bits o\' bread an\' pluck young grass for it'),(14988,4,1,1886,'and it got to like him so it follows him about an\' it lets him get on its back'),(14989,4,260877,247,'dickon\'s a kind lad an\' animals likes him'),(14990,4,245,139,'mary had never possessed an animal pet of her own and had always thought she should like one'),(14991,4,1051,34355,'so she began to feel a slight interest in dickon and as she had never before been interested in any one but herself it was the dawning of a healthy sentiment'),(14992,4,483,50,'when she went into the room which had been made into a nursery for her she found that it was rather like the one she had slept in'),(14993,4,67,199788,'it was not a child\'s room but a grown-up person\'s room with gloomy old pictures on the walls and heavy old oak chairs'),(14994,4,23,13647,'a table in the center was set with a good substantial breakfast'),(14995,4,295,382,'but she had always had a very small appetite and she looked with something more than indifference at the first plate martha set before her'),(14996,4,279,2257,'i don\'t want it she said'),(14997,4,252871,260761,'tha\' doesn\'t want thy porridge'),(14998,4,252798,261035,'martha exclaimed incredulously'),(14999,4,252871,75,'tha\' doesn\'t know how good it is'),(15000,4,983,261054,'put a bit o\' treacle on it or a bit o\' sugar'),(15001,4,279,245,'i don\'t want it repeated mary'),(15002,4,2257,252798,'said martha'),(15003,4,279,112665,'i can\'t abide to see good victuals go to waste'),(15004,4,257,6645,'if our children was at this table they\'d clean it bare in five minutes'),(15005,4,2257,7511,'said mary coldly'),(15006,4,174095,252798,'echoed martha'),(15007,4,99,13329,'because they scarce ever had their stomachs full in their lives'),(15008,4,259909,261110,'they\'re as hungry as young hawks an\' foxes'),(15009,4,279,33952,'i don\'t know what it is to be hungry said mary with the indifference of ignorance'),(15010,4,252798,35663,'martha looked indignant'),(15011,4,43,67,'well it would do thee good to try it'),(15012,4,279,261147,'i can see that plain enough she said outspokenly'),(15013,4,127793,57237,'i\'ve no patience with folk as sits an\' just stares at good bread an\' meat'),(15014,4,413,1426,'my word'),(15015,4,127385,261183,'don\'t i wish dickon and phil an\' jane an\' th\' rest of \'em had what\'s here under their pinafores'),(15016,4,4949,112,'why don\'t you take it to them'),(15017,4,22375,245,'suggested mary'),(15018,4,127528,6412,'it\'s not mine answered martha stoutly'),(15019,4,144004,976,'an\' this isn\'t my day out'),(15020,4,279,2096,'i get my day out once a month same as th\' rest'),(15021,4,1502,2096,'then i go home an\' clean up for mother an\' give her a day\'s rest'),(15022,4,245,261243,'mary drank some tea and ate a little toast and some marmalade'),(15023,4,2074,252798,'you wrap up warm an\' run out an\' play you said martha'),(15024,4,259746,57237,'it\'ll do you good and give you some stomach for your meat'),(15025,4,245,6694,'mary went to the window'),(15026,4,74,261286,'there were gardens and paths and big trees but everything looked dull and wintry'),(15027,4,4949,37,'why should i go out on a day like this'),(15028,4,43,513,'well if tha\' doesn\'t go out tha\'lt have to stay in an\' what has tha\' got to do'),(15029,4,245,382,'mary glanced about her'),(15030,4,74,513,'there was nothing to do'),(15031,4,483,273,'when mrs'),(15032,4,255890,5894,'medlock had prepared the nursery she had not thought of amusement'),(15033,4,290,591,'perhaps it would be better to go and see what the gardens were like'),(15034,4,59,424,'who will go with me'),(15035,4,729,7919,'she inquired'),(15036,4,252798,6048,'martha stared'),(15037,4,127698,13526,'you\'ll go by yourself she answered'),(15038,4,127698,8709,'you\'ll have to learn to play like other children does when they haven\'t got sisters and brothers'),(15039,4,2043,10657,'our dickon goes off on th\' moor by himself an\' plays for hours'),(15040,4,128237,260811,'that\'s how he made friends with th\' pony'),(15041,4,129