257,'that\'s what mother said'),(15797,4,729,159776,'she says \'nothin\' will do her more good than skippin\' rope'),(15798,4,127528,254,'it\'s th\' sensiblest toy a child can have'),(15799,4,2792,179730,'let her play out in th\' fresh air skippin\' an\' it\'ll stretch her legs an\' arms an\' give her some strength in \'em'),(15800,4,67,205371,'it was plain that there was not a great deal of strength in mistress mary\'s arms and legs when she first began to skip'),(15801,4,729,37491,'she was not very clever at it but she liked it so much that she did not want to stop'),(15802,4,983,252798,'put on tha\' things and run an\' skip out o\' doors said martha'),(15803,4,5751,28891,'mother said i must tell you to keep out o\' doors as much as you could even when it rains a bit so as tha\' wrap up warm'),(15804,4,245,20290,'mary put on her coat and hat and took her skipping-rope over her arm'),(15805,4,729,18229,'she opened the door to go out and then suddenly thought of something and turned back rather slowly'),(15806,4,252798,241292,'martha she said they were your wages'),(15807,4,67,2236,'it was your twopence really'),(15808,4,18463,2074,'thank you'),(15809,4,729,382,'she said it stiffly because she was not used to thanking people or noticing that they did things for her'),(15810,4,18463,513,'thank you she said and held out her hand because she did not know what else to do'),(15811,4,252798,3279,'martha gave her hand a clumsy little shake as if she was not accustomed to this sort of thing either'),(15812,4,1502,15178,'then she laughed'),(15813,4,252871,2257,'tha\' art a queer old-womanish thing she said'),(15814,4,257,93318,'if tha\'d been our \'lizabeth ellen tha\'d have give me a kiss'),(15815,4,245,784,'mary looked stiffer than ever'),(15816,4,513,2074,'do you want me to kiss you'),(15817,4,252798,3373,'martha laughed again'),(15818,4,12472,13526,'nay not me she answered'),(15819,4,257,269474,'if tha\' was different p\'raps tha\'d want to thysel\''),(15820,4,295,127648,'but tha\' isn\'t'),(15821,4,17034,159776,'run off outside an\' play with thy rope'),(15822,4,25837,6317,'mistress mary felt a little awkward as she went out of the room'),(15823,4,256232,382,'yorkshire people seemed strange and martha was always rather a puzzle to her'),(15824,4,308,55,'at first she had disliked her very much but now she did not'),(15825,4,51,109,'the skipping-rope was a wonderful thing'),(15826,4,729,1834,'she counted and skipped and skipped and counted until her cheeks were quite red and she was more interested than she had ever been since she was born'),(15827,4,51,67,'the sun was shining and a little wind was blowing--not a rough wind but one which came in delightful little gusts and brought a fresh scent of newly turned earth with it'),(15828,4,729,1351,'she skipped round the fountain garden and up one walk and down another'),(15829,4,729,247,'she skipped at last into the kitchen-garden and saw ben weatherstaff digging and talking to his robin which was hopping about him'),(15830,4,729,3355,'she skipped down the walk toward him and he lifted his head and looked at her with a curious expression'),(15831,4,729,382,'she had wondered if he would notice her'),(15832,4,729,205371,'she really wanted him to see her skip'),(15833,4,258,25251,'he exclaimed'),(15834,4,787,1426,'upon my word'),(15835,4,272522,272739,'p\'raps tha\' art a young \'un after all an\' p\'raps tha\'s got child\'s blood in thy veins instead of sour buttermilk'),(15836,4,257790,252889,'tha\'s skipped red into thy cheeks as sure as my name\'s ben weatherstaff'),(15837,4,279,67,'i wouldn\'t have believed tha\' could do it'),(15838,4,279,2257,'i never skipped before mary said'),(15839,4,127759,25822,'i\'m just beginning'),(15840,4,279,5495,'i can only go up to twenty'),(15841,4,252871,252888,'tha\' keep on said ben'),(15842,4,252871,272795,'tha\' shapes well enough at it for a young \'un that\'s lived with heathen'),(15843,4,26,252820,'just see how he\'s watchin\' thee jerking his head toward the robin'),(15844,4,258,13685,'he followed after thee yesterday'),(15845,4,272813,13310,'he\'ll be at it again to-day'),(15846,4,272813,75,'he\'ll be bound to find out what th\' skippin\'-rope is'),(15847,4,129787,139,'he\'s never seen one'),(15848,4,24764,75672,'shaking his head at the bird tha\' curosity will be th\' death of thee sometime if tha\' doesn\'t look sharp'),(15849,4,245,6645,'mary skipped round all the gardens and round the orchard resting every few minutes'),(15850,4,308,67,'at length she went to her own special walk and made up her mind to try if she could skip the whole length of it'),(15851,4,67,37491,'it was a good long skip and she began slowly but before she had gone half-way down the path she was so hot and breathless that she was obliged to stop'),(15852,4,729,57841,'she did not mind much because she had already counted up to thirty'),(15853,4,729,261791,'she stopped with a little laugh of pleasure and there lo and behold was the robin swaying on a long branch of ivy'),(15854,4,258,264904,'he had followed her and he greeted her with a chirp'),(15855,4,42,3373,'as mary had skipped toward him she felt something heavy in her pocket strike against her at each jump and when she saw the robin she laughed again'),(15856,4,2074,2257,'you showed me where the key was yesterday she said'),(15857,4,2074,515,'you ought to show me the door to-day but i don\'t believe you know'),(15858,4,51,891,'the robin flew from his swinging spray of ivy on to the top of the wall and he opened his beak and sang a loud lovely trill merely to show off'),(15859,4,1155,67,'nothing in the world is quite as adorably lovely as a robin when he shows off--and they are nearly always doing it'),(15860,4,245,2111,'mary lennox had heard a great deal about magic in her ayah\'s stories and she always said that what happened almost at that moment was magic'),(15861,4,139,2096,'one of the nice little gusts of wind rushed down the walk and it was a stronger one than the rest')