nd see how thick and warm it is she said'),(16956,4,279,1711,'i will pinch you a little if you like to show you how real i am'),(16957,4,284,813,'for a minute i thought you might be a dream too'),(16958,4,4187,344,'where did you come from'),(16959,4,258,7867,'he asked'),(16960,4,344,6317,'from my own room'),(16961,4,51,473,'the wind wuthered so i couldn\'t go to sleep and i heard some one crying and wanted to find out who it was'),(16962,4,994,284,'what were you crying for'),(16963,4,99,14416,'because i couldn\'t go to sleep either and my head ached'),(16964,4,4923,3373,'tell me your name again'),(16965,4,245,252924,'mary lennox'),(16966,4,991,454,'did no one ever tell you i had come to live here'),(16967,4,258,2448,'he was still fingering the fold of her wrapper but he began to look a little more as if he believed in her reality'),(16968,4,655,13526,'no he answered'),(16969,4,1056,286564,'they daren\'t'),(16970,4,7867,245,'asked mary'),(16971,4,99,424,'because i should have been afraid you would see me'),(16972,4,279,554,'i won\'t let people see me and talk me over'),(16973,4,245,2845,'mary asked again feeling more mystified every moment'),(16974,4,99,4332,'because i am like this always ill and having to lie down'),(16975,4,413,3279,'my father won\'t let people talk me over either'),(16976,4,51,424,'the servants are not allowed to speak about me'),(16977,4,257,1517,'if i live i may be a hunchback but i shan\'t live'),(16978,4,413,247,'my father hates to think i may be like him'),(16979,4,5037,75,'oh what a queer house this is'),(16980,4,245,2257,'mary said'),(16981,4,994,1889,'what a queer house'),(16982,4,9949,3555,'everything is a kind of secret'),(16983,4,8912,2074,'rooms are locked up and gardens are locked up--and you'),(16984,4,254,1274,'have you been locked up'),(16985,4,279,67,'i stay in this room because i don\'t want to be moved out of it'),(16986,4,67,76,'it tires me too much'),(16987,4,297,2074,'does your father come and see you'),(16988,4,245,46033,'mary ventured'),(16989,4,1618,15299,'generally when i am asleep'),(16990,4,258,424,'he doesn\'t want to see me'),(16991,4,245,3373,'mary could not help asking again'),(16992,4,23,11135,'a sort of angry shadow passed over the boy\'s face'),(16993,4,413,424,'my mother died when i was born and it makes him wretched to look at me'),(16994,4,258,2742,'he thinks i don\'t know but i\'ve heard people talking'),(16995,4,258,424,'he almost hates me'),(16996,4,258,1755,'he hates the garden because she died said mary half speaking to herself'),(16997,4,994,24714,'what garden'),(16998,4,51,7867,'the boy asked'),(16999,4,286785,141285,'just--just a garden she used to like mary stammered'),(17000,4,254,705,'have you been here always'),(17001,4,1486,705,'nearly always'),(17002,4,841,424,'sometimes i have been taken to places at the seaside but i won\'t stay because people stare at me'),(17003,4,279,7315,'i used to wear an iron thing to keep my back straight but a grand doctor came from london to see me and said it was stupid'),(17004,4,258,6950,'he told them to take it off and keep me out in the fresh air'),(17005,4,279,976,'i hate fresh air and i don\'t want to go out'),(17006,4,279,245,'i didn\'t when first i came here said mary'),(17007,4,4949,163,'why do you keep looking at me like that'),(17008,4,99,5897,'because of the dreams that are so real he answered rather fretfully'),(17009,4,841,21213,'sometimes when i open my eyes i don\'t believe i\'m awake'),(17010,4,145707,245,'we\'re both awake said mary'),(17011,4,729,194419,'she glanced round the room with its high ceiling and shadowy corners and dim firelight'),(17012,4,67,2793,'it looks quite like a dream and it\'s the middle of the night and everybody in the house is asleep--everybody but us'),(17013,4,1649,21213,'we are wide awake'),(17014,4,279,276471,'i don\'t want it to be a dream the boy said restlessly'),(17015,4,245,855,'mary thought of something all at once'),(17016,4,257,4025,'if you don\'t like people to see you she began do you want me to go away'),(17017,4,258,72691,'he still held the fold of her wrapper and he gave it a little pull'),(17018,4,655,2257,'no he said'),(17019,4,279,1885,'i should be sure you were a dream if you went'),(17020,4,257,5107,'if you are real sit down on that big footstool and talk'),(17021,4,279,2074,'i want to hear about you'),(17022,4,245,133817,'mary put down her candle on the table near the bed and sat down on the cushioned stool'),(17023,4,729,172,'she did not want to go away at all'),(17024,4,729,10170,'she wanted to stay in the mysterious hidden-away room and talk to the mysterious boy'),(17025,4,994,2074,'what do you want me to tell you'),(17026,4,729,2257,'she said'),(17027,4,258,256232,'he wanted to know how long she had been at misselthwaite he wanted to know which corridor her room was on he wanted to know what she had been doing if she disliked the moor as he disliked it where she had lived before she came to yorkshire'),(17028,4,729,8548,'she answered all these questions and many more and he lay back on his pillow and listened'),(17029,4,258,228276,'he made her tell him a great deal about india and about her voyage across the ocean'),(17030,4,729,259,'she found out that because he had been an invalid he had not learned things as other children had'),(17031,4,139,41,'one of his nurses had taught him to read when he was quite little and he was always reading and looking at pictures in splendid books'),(17032,4,384,34,'though his father rarely saw him when he was awake he was given all sorts of wonderful things to amuse himself with'),(17033,4,258,303,'he never seemed to have been amused however'),(17034,4,258,513,'he could have anything he asked for and was never made to do anything he did not like to do'),(17035,4,138,287283,'every one is obliged to do what pleases me he said indifferently'),(17036,4,67,7118,'it makes me ill to be angry'),(17037,4,655,1274,'no one believes i shall live to grow up'),(17038,4,258,172,'he said it as if he was