ng to pounce as cats do'),(19797,4,483,18229,'when cats are preparing to pounce they creep over the ground very slowly'),(19798,4,51,135578,'the robin talked this over with his mate a great deal for a few days but after that he decided not to speak of the subject because her terror was so great that he was afraid it might be injurious to the eggs'),(19799,4,483,16441,'when the boy began to walk by himself and even to move more quickly it was an immense relief'),(19800,4,295,14679,'but for a long time--or it seemed a long time to the robin--he was a source of some anxiety'),(19801,4,258,991,'he did not act as the other humans did'),(19802,4,258,3373,'he seemed very fond of walking but he had a way of sitting or lying down for a while and then getting up in a disconcerting manner to begin again'),(19803,4,139,109,'one day the robin remembered that when he himself had been made to learn to fly by his parents he had done much the same sort of thing'),(19804,4,258,2096,'he had taken short flights of a few yards and then had been obliged to rest'),(19805,4,1051,9238,'so it occurred to him that this boy was learning to fly--or rather to walk'),(19806,4,258,16572,'he mentioned this to his mate and when he told her that the eggs would probably conduct themselves in the same way after they were fledged she was quite comforted and even became eagerly interested and derived great pleasure from watching the boy over the edge of her nest--though she always thought that the eggs would be much cleverer and learn more quickly'),(19807,4,295,172,'but then she said indulgently that humans were always more clumsy and slow than eggs and most of them never seemed really to learn to fly at all'),(19808,4,2074,211482,'you never met them in the air or on tree-tops'),(19809,4,171,314,'after a while the boy began to move about as the others did but all three of the children at times did unusual things'),(19810,4,1056,4332,'they would stand under the trees and move their arms and legs and heads about in a way which was neither walking nor running nor sitting down'),(19811,4,1056,513,'they went through these movements at intervals every day and the robin was never able to explain to his mate what they were doing or trying to do'),(19812,4,258,1212,'he could only say that he was sure that the eggs would never flap about in such a manner but as the boy who could speak robin so fluently was doing the thing with them birds could be quite sure that the actions were not of a dangerous nature'),(19813,4,53,169343,'of course neither the robin nor his mate had ever heard of the champion wrestler bob haworth and his exercises for making the muscles stand out like lumps'),(19814,4,277891,3728,'robins are not like human beings their muscles are always exercised from the first and so they develop themselves in a natural manner'),(19815,4,257,1674,'if you have to fly about to find every meal you eat your muscles do not become atrophied atrophied means wasted away through want of use'),(19816,4,483,76968,'when the boy was walking and running about and digging and weeding like the others the nest in the corner was brooded over by a great peace and content'),(19817,4,1912,63024,'fears for the eggs became things of the past'),(19818,4,15957,26921,'knowing that your eggs were as safe as if they were locked in a bank vault and the fact that you could watch so many curious things going on made setting a most entertaining occupation'),(19819,4,407,24714,'on wet days the eggs\' mother sometimes felt even a little dull because the children did not come into the garden'),(19820,4,295,70171,'but even on wet days it could not be said that mary and colin were dull'),(19821,4,139,129924,'one morning when the rain streamed down unceasingly and colin was beginning to feel a little restive as he was obliged to remain on his sofa because it was not safe to get up and walk about mary had an inspiration'),(19822,4,2530,1040,'now that i am a real boy colin had said my legs and arms and all 