me a startled one'),(20022,4,59,454,'who is coming in here'),(20023,4,258,16572,'he said quickly'),(20024,4,59,67,'who is it'),(20025,4,51,4359,'the door in the ivied wall had been pushed gently open and a woman had entered'),(20026,4,729,112,'she had come in with the last line of their song and she had stood still listening and looking at them'),(20027,4,34,41,'with the ivy behind her the sunlight drifting through the trees and dappling her long blue cloak and her nice fresh face smiling across the greenery she was rather like a softly colored illustration in one of colin\'s books'),(20028,4,729,190939,'she had wonderful affectionate eyes which seemed to take everything in--all of them even ben weatherstaff and the creatures and every flower that was in bloom'),(20029,4,27921,172,'unexpectedly as she had appeared not one of them felt that she was an intruder at all'),(20030,4,260877,143437,'dickon\'s eyes lighted like lamps'),(20031,4,127528,75,'it\'s mother--that\'s who it is'),(20032,4,258,17034,'he cried and he went across the grass at a run'),(20033,4,252854,247,'colin began to move toward her too and mary went with him'),(20034,4,1056,182202,'they both felt their pulses beat faster'),(20035,4,127528,5751,'it\'s mother'),(20036,4,252835,155210,'dickon said again when they met half-way'),(20037,4,279,150529,'i knowed tha\' wanted to see her an\' i told her where th\' door was hid'),(20038,4,252854,11135,'colin held out his hand with a sort of flushed royal shyness but his eyes quite devoured her face'),(20039,4,1232,24714,'even when i was ill i wanted to see you he said you and dickon and the secret garden'),(20040,4,128247,509,'i\'d never wanted to see any one or anything before'),(20041,4,51,764,'the sight of his uplifted face brought about a sudden change in her own'),(20042,4,729,917,'she flushed and the corners of her mouth shook and a mist seemed to sweep over her eyes'),(20043,4,2783,49341,'dear lad'),(20044,4,729,284265,'she broke out tremulously'),(20045,4,2783,49341,'dear lad'),(20046,4,42,67,'as if she had not known she were going to say it'),(20047,4,729,5725,'she did not say mester colin but just dear lad quite suddenly'),(20048,4,729,382,'she might have said it to dickon in the same way if she had seen something in his face which touched her'),(20049,4,252854,67,'colin liked it'),(20050,4,60,43,'are you surprised because i am so well'),(20051,4,258,7867,'he asked'),(20052,4,729,917,'she put her hand on his shoulder and smiled the mist out of her eyes'),(20053,4,257803,1711,'aye that i am'),(20054,4,729,127772,'she said but tha\'rt so like thy mother tha\' made my heart jump'),(20055,4,513,424,'do you think said colin a little awkwardly that will make my father like me'),(20056,4,257803,181242,'aye for sure dear lad she answered and she gave his shoulder a soft quick pat'),(20057,4,258,8866,'he mun come home--he mun come home'),(20058,4,267176,382,'susan sowerby said ben weatherstaff getting close to her'),(20059,4,2046,252871,'look at th\' lad\'s legs wilt tha\''),(20060,4,1056,369,'they was like drumsticks i\' stockin\' two month\' ago--an\' i heard folk tell as they was bandy an\' knock-kneed both at th\' same time'),(20061,4,2046,2530,'look at \'em now'),(20062,4,267176,2714,'susan sowerby laughed a comfortable laugh'),(20063,4,259909,2257,'they\'re goin\' to be fine strong lad\'s legs in a bit she said'),(20064,4,2792,67,'let him go on playin\' an\' workin\' in th\' garden an\' eatin\' hearty an\' drinkin\' plenty o\' good sweet milk an\' there\'ll not be a finer pair i\' yorkshire thank god for it'),(20065,4,729,652,'she put both hands on mistress mary\'s shoulders and looked her little face over in a motherly fashion'),(20066,4,144004,813,'an\' thee too'),(20067,4,729,2257,'she said'),(20068,4,259179,271874,'tha\'rt grown near as hearty as our \'lizabeth ellen'),(20069,4,127845,813,'i\'ll warrant tha\'rt like thy mother too'),(20070,4,2043,273,'our martha told me as mrs'),(20071,4,255890,1506,'medlock heard she was a pretty woman'),(20072,4,261303,203807,'tha\'lt be like a blush rose when tha\' grows up my little lass bless thee'),(20073,4,729,273,'she did not mention that when martha came home on her day out and described the plain sallow child she had said that she had no confidence whatever in what mrs'),(20074,4,255890,3846,'medlock had heard'),(20075,4,67,282954,'it doesn\'t stand to reason that a pretty woman could be th\' mother o\' such a fou\' little lass she had added obstinately'),(20076,4,245,11135,'mary had not had time to pay much attention to her changing face'),(20077,4,729,18376,'she had only known that she looked different and seemed to have a great deal more hair and that it was growing very fast'),(20078,4,295,382,'but remembering her pleasure in looking at the mem sahib in the past she was glad to hear that she might some day look like her'),(20079,4,267176,2362,'susan sowerby went round their garden with them and was told the whole story of it and shown every bush and tree which had come alive'),(20080,4,252854,409,'colin walked on one side of her and mary on the other'),(20081,4,2163,3812,'each of them kept looking up at her comfortable rosy face secretly curious about the delightful feeling she gave them--a sort of warm supported feeling'),(20082,4,67,62031,'it seemed as if she understood them as dickon understood his creatures'),(20083,4,729,5449,'she stooped over the flowers and talked about them as if they were children'),(20084,4,266682,260877,'soot followed her and once or twice cawed at her and flew upon her shoulder as if it were dickon\'s'),(20085,4,483,13725,'when they told her about the robin and the first flight of the young ones she laughed a motherly little mellow laugh in her throat'),(20086,4,279,2257,'i suppose learnin\' \'em to fly is like learnin\' children to walk but i\'m feared i should be all in a worrit if mine had wings instead o\' legs she said'),(20087,4,67,2111,'it was because she seemed such a wonderful woman in 