0107,4,729,314,'she was full of fun and made them laugh at all sorts of odd things'),(20108,4,729,2458,'she told them stories in broad yorkshire and taught them new words'),(20109,4,729,18225,'she laughed as if she could not help it when they told her of the increasing difficulty there was in pretending that colin was still a fretful invalid'),(20110,4,2074,252854,'you see we can\'t help laughing nearly all the time when we are together explained colin'),(20111,4,1,172,'and it doesn\'t sound ill at all'),(20112,4,1649,784,'we try to choke it back but it will burst out and that sounds worse than ever'),(20113,4,129216,5725,'there\'s one thing that comes into my mind so often said mary and i can scarcely ever hold in when i think of it suddenly'),(20114,4,279,154870,'i keep thinking suppose colin\'s face should get to look like a full moon'),(20115,4,67,513,'it isn\'t like one yet but he gets a tiny bit fatter every day--and suppose some morning it should look like one--what should we do'),(20116,4,68061,276051,'bless us all i can see tha\' has a good bit o\' play actin\' to do said susan sowerby'),(20117,4,295,16111,'but tha\' won\'t have to keep it up much longer'),(20118,4,263287,8866,'mester craven\'ll come home'),(20119,4,513,865,'do you think he will'),(20120,4,7867,252854,'asked colin'),(20121,4,267176,173697,'susan sowerby chuckled softly'),(20122,4,279,2257,'i suppose it \'ud nigh break thy heart if he found out before tha\' told him in tha\' own way she said'),(20123,4,257790,67,'tha\'s laid awake nights plannin\' it'),(20124,4,279,252854,'i couldn\'t bear any one else to tell him said colin'),(20125,4,279,2045,'i think about different ways every day'),(20126,4,279,6317,'i think now i just want to run into his room'),(20127,4,292742,276051,'that\'d be a fine start for him said susan sowerby'),(20128,4,128247,49341,'i\'d like to see his face lad'),(20129,4,279,163,'i would that'),(20130,4,258,302356,'he mun come back--that he mun'),(20131,4,139,258137,'one of the things they talked of was the visit they were to make to her cottage'),(20132,4,1056,172,'they planned it all'),(20133,4,1056,258336,'they were to drive over the moor and lunch out of doors among the heather'),(20134,4,1056,21958,'they would see all the twelve children and dickon\'s garden and would not come back until they were tired'),(20135,4,267176,273,'susan sowerby got up at last to return to the house and mrs'),(20136,4,67,1187,'it was time for colin to be wheeled back also'),(20137,4,295,18376,'but before he got into his chair he stood quite close to susan and fixed his eyes on her with a kind of bewildered adoration and he suddenly caught hold of the fold of her blue cloak and held it fast'),(20138,4,2074,2257,'you are just what i--what i wanted he said'),(20139,4,279,260877,'i wish you were my mother--as well as dickon\'s'),(20140,4,172,8526,'all at once susan sowerby bent down and drew him with her warm arms close against the bosom under the blue cloak--as if he had been dickon\'s brother'),(20141,4,51,917,'the quick mist swept over her eyes'),(20142,4,2783,49341,'dear lad'),(20143,4,729,2257,'she said'),(20144,4,202804,280,'thy own mother\'s in this \'ere very garden i do believe'),(20145,4,729,67,'she couldna\' keep out of it'),(20146,4,202804,302356,'thy father mun come back to thee--he mun'),(20147,4,4328,7055,'chapter xxvii in the garden in each century since the beginning of the world wonderful things have been discovered'),(20148,4,50,509,'in the last century more amazing things were found out than in any century before'),(20149,4,50,11189,'in this new century hundreds of things still more astounding will be brought to light'),(20150,4,308,2566,'at first people refuse to believe that a strange new thing can be done then they begin to hope it can be done then they see it can be done--then it is done and all the world wonders why it was not done centuries ago'),(20151,4,139,170306,'one of the new things people began to find out in the last century was that thoughts--just mere thoughts--are as powerful as electric batteries--as good for one as sunlight is or as bad for one as poison'),(20152,4,40,133885,'to let a sad thought or a bad one get into your mind is as dangerous as letting a scarlet fever germ get into your body'),(20153,4,257,1517,'if you let it stay there after it has got in you may never get over it as long as you live'),(20154,4,1051,17601,'so long as mistress mary\'s mind was full of disagreeable thoughts about her dislikes and sour opinions of people and her determination not to be pleased by or interested in anything she was a yellow-faced sickly bored and wretched child'),(20155,4,28652,67,'circumstances however were very kind to her though she was not at all aware of it'),(20156,4,1056,676,'they began to push her about for her own good'),(20157,4,483,21958,'when her mind gradually filled itself with robins and moorland cottages crowded with children with queer crabbed old gardeners and common little yorkshire housemaids with springtime and with secret gardens coming alive day by day and also with a moor boy and his creatures there was no room left for the disagreeable thoughts which affected her liver and her digestion and made her yellow and tired'),(20158,4,1051,67,'so long as colin shut himself up in his room and thought only of his fears and weakness and his detestation of people who looked at him and reflected hourly on humps and early death he was a hysterical half-crazy little hypochondriac who knew nothing of the sunshine and the spring and also did not know that he could get well and could stand upon his feet if he tried to do it'),(20159,4,483,174319,'when new beautiful thoughts began to push out the old hideous ones life began to come back to him his blood ran healthily through his veins and strength poured into him like a flood'),(20160,4,892,172,'his scientific experiment was quite practical and simple and there was nothing weird about it at all'),(20161,4,76,139,'much more surprising things can happen to any one who when a disagreeable or discouraged thought comes into his mind just has the sense to remember in time and push it out by putting in an agreeable determinedly courageous one'),(20162,4,1024,461,'two things cannot be in one place'),(20163,4,4187,10991,'where you tend a rose my lad a thistle cannot grow'),(20164,4,1394,5089,'while the secret garden was coming alive and two children were coming alive with it there was a man wandering about certain far-away beautiful places in the norwegian fiords and the valleys and mountains of switzerland and he was a man who for ten years had kept his mind filled with dark and heart-broken thinking'),(20165,4,258,3973,'he had not been courageous he had never tried to put any other thoughts in the place of the dark ones'),(20166,4,258,112,'he had wandered by blue lakes and thought them he had lain on mountain-sides with sheets of deep blue gentians blooming all about him and flower breaths filling all the air and he had thought them'),(20167,4,23,2751,'a terrible sorrow had fallen upon him when he had been happy and he had let his soul fill itself with blackness and had refused obstinately to allow any rift of light to pierce through'),(20168,4,258,36134,'he had forgotten and deserted his home and his duties'),(20169,4,483,47715,'when he traveled about darkness so brooded over him that the sight of him was a wrong done to other people because it was as if he poisoned the air about him with gloom'),(20170,4,387,28464,'most strangers thought he must be either half mad or a man with some hidden crime on his soul'),(20171,4,258,4973,'he was a tall man with a drawn face and crooked shoulders and the name he always entered on hotel registers was archibald craven misselthwaite manor yorkshire england'),(20172,4,258,116822,'he had traveled far and wide since the day he saw mistress mary in his study and told her she might have her bit of earth'),(20173,4,258,1390,'he had been in the most beautiful places in europe though he had remained nowhere more than a few days'),(20174,4,258,82745,'he had chosen the quietest and remotest spots'),(20175,4,258,1834,'he had been on the tops of mountains whose heads were in the clouds and had looked down on other mountains when the sun rose and touched them with such light as made it seem as if the world were just being born'),(20176,4,295,4729,'but the light had never seemed to touch himself until one day when he realized that for the first time in ten years a strange thing had happened'),(20177,4,258,148987,'he was in a wonderful valley in the austrian tyrol and he had been walking alone through such beauty as might have lifted any man\'s soul out of shadow'),(20178,4,258,892,'he had walked a long way and it had not lifted his'),(20179,4,295,80095,'but at last he had felt tired and had thrown himself down to rest on a carpet of moss by a stream'),(20180,4,67,310979,'it was a clear little stream which ran quite merrily along on its narrow way through the luscious damp greenness'),(20181,4,841,152254,'sometimes it made a sound rather like very low laughter as it bubbled over and round stones'),(20182,4,258,4025,'he saw birds come and dip their heads to drink in it and then flick their wings and fly away'),(20183,4,67,32872,'it seemed like a thing alive and yet its tiny voice made the stillness seem deeper'),(20184,4,51,1040,'the valley was very very still'),(20185,4,42,339,'as he sat gazing into the clear running of the water archibald craven gradually felt his mind and body both grow quiet as quiet as the valley itself'),(20186,4,258,55,'he wondered if he were going to sleep but he was not'),(20187,4,258,55120,'he sat and gazed at the sunlit water and his eyes began to see things growing at its edge'),(20188,4,74,2566,'there was one lovely mass of blue forget-me-nots growing so close to the stream that its leaves were wet and at these he found himself looking as he remembered he had looked at such things years ago'),(20189,4,258,798,'he was actually thinking tenderly how lovely it was and what wonders of blue its hundreds of little blossoms were'),(20190,4,258,15729,'he did not know that just that simple thought was slowly filling his mind--filling and filling it until other things were softly pushed aside'),(20191,4,67,4025,'it was as if a sweet clear spring had begun to rise in a stagnant pool and had risen and risen until at last it swept the dark water away'),(20192,4,295,852,'but of course he did not think of this himself'),(20193,4,258,268866,'he only knew that the valley seemed to grow quieter and quieter as he sat and stared at the bright delicate blueness'),(20194,4,258,852,'he did not know how long he sat there or what was happening to him but at last he moved as if he were awakening and he got up slowly and stood on the moss carpet drawing a long deep soft breath and wondering at himself'),(20195,4,707,37275,'something seemed to have been unbound and released in him very quietly'),(20196,4,994,67,'what is it'),(20197,4,258,128997,'he said almost in a whisper and he passed his hand over his forehead'),(20198,4,279,2362,'i almost feel as if--i were alive'),(20199,4,279,247,'i do not know enough about the wonderfulness of undiscovered things to be able to explain how this had happened to him'),(20200,4,3483,119,'neither does any one else yet'),(20201,4,258,784,'he did not understand at all himself--but he remembered this strange hour months afterward when he was at misselthwaite again and he found out quite by accident that on this very day colin had cried out as he went into the secret garden i am going to live forever and ever and ever'),(20202,4,51,2583,'the singular calmness remained with him the rest of the evening and he slept a new reposeful sleep but it was not with him very long'),(20203,4,258,23243,'he did not know that it could be kept'),(20204,4,3,1886,'by the next night he had opened the doors wide to his dark thoughts and they had come trooping and rushing back'),(