s to be given to the performers'),(26684,5,513,82,'do you really think the organdy will be best'),(26685,5,198366,89575,'queried anne anxiously'),(26686,5,279,11206,'i dont think its as pretty as my blue-flowered muslin--and it certainly isnt so fashionable'),(26687,5,295,4247,'but it suits you ever so much better said diana'),(26688,5,153,159877,'its so soft and frilly and clinging'),(26689,5,51,1274,'the muslin is stiff and makes you look too dressed up'),(26690,5,295,2074,'but the organdy seems as if it grew on you'),(26691,5,30104,97436,'anne sighed and yielded'),(26692,5,4247,171,'diana was beginning to have a reputation for notable taste in dressing and her advice on such subjects was much sought after'),(26693,5,729,9343,'she was looking very pretty herself on this particular night in a dress of the lovely wild-rose pink from which anne was forever debarred but she was not to take any part in the concert so her appearance was of minor importance'),(26694,5,172,1289,'all her pains were bestowed upon anne who she vowed must for the credit of avonlea be dressed and combed and adorned to the queens taste'),(26695,5,72691,396734,'pull out that frill a little more--so here let me tie your sash now for your slippers'),(26696,5,4348,414,'im going to braid your hair in two thick braids and tie them halfway up with big white bows--no dont pull out a single curl over your forehead--just have the soft part'),(26697,5,74,273,'there is no way you do your hair suits you so well anne and mrs'),(26698,5,389358,1051,'allan says you look like a madonna when you part it so'),(26699,5,279,18039,'i shall fasten this little white house rose just behind your ear'),(26700,5,74,2074,'there was just one on my bush and i saved it for you'),(26701,5,5767,407,'shall i put my pearl beads on'),(26702,5,7867,30104,'asked anne'),(26703,5,333259,424,'matthew brought me a string from town last week and i know hed like to see them on me'),(26704,5,4247,13725,'diana pursed up her lips put her black head on one side critically and finally pronounced in favor of the beads which were thereupon tied around annes slim milk-white throat'),(26705,5,226864,2184,'theres something so stylish about you anne said diana with unenvious admiration'),(26706,5,2074,6950,'you hold your head with such an air'),(26707,5,279,7012,'i suppose its your figure'),(26708,5,279,421879,'i am just a dumpling'),(26709,5,226572,1051,'ive always been afraid of it and now i know it is so'),(26710,5,43,67,'well i suppose i shall just have to resign myself to it'),(26711,5,295,764,'but you have such dimples said anne smiling affectionately into the pretty vivacious face so near her own'),(26712,5,25773,320908,'lovely dimples like little dents in cream'),(26713,5,279,214886,'i have given up all hope of dimples'),(26714,5,413,36656,'my dimple-dream will never come true but so many of my dreams have that i mustnt complain'),(26715,5,1711,2530,'am i all ready now'),(26716,5,172,11135,'all ready assured diana as marilla appeared in the doorway a gaunt figure with grayer hair than of yore and no fewer angles but with a much softer face'),(26717,5,3479,333265,'come right in and look at our elocutionist marilla'),(26718,5,233650,25773,'doesnt she look lovely'),(26719,5,333265,132287,'marilla emitted a sound between a sniff and a grunt'),(26720,5,729,108,'she looks neat and proper'),(26721,5,279,14884,'i like that way of fixing her hair'),(26722,5,295,192045,'but i expect shell ruin that dress driving over there in the dust and dew with it and it looks most too thin for these damp nights'),(26723,5,422052,67,'organdys the most unserviceable stuff in the world anyhow and i told matthew so when he got it'),(26724,5,295,390514,'but there is no use in saying anything to matthew nowadays'),(26725,5,369,247,'time was when he would take my advice but now he just buys things for anne regardless and the clerks at carmody know they can palm anything off on him'),(26726,5,26,67,'just let them tell him a thing is pretty and fashionable and matthew plunks his money down for it'),(26727,5,2129,407,'mind you keep your skirt clear of the wheel anne and put your warm jacket on'),(26728,5,1502,360048,'then marilla stalked downstairs thinking proudly how sweet anne looked with that one moonbeam from the forehead to the crown and regretting that she could not go to the concert herself to hear her girl recite'),(26729,5,279,89575,'i wonder if it is too damp for my dress said anne anxiously'),(26730,5,55,8440,'not a bit of it said diana pulling up the window blind'),(26731,5,153,207556,'its a perfect night and there wont be any dew'),(26732,5,2046,174321,'look at the moonlight'),(26733,5,4348,4247,'im so glad my window looks east into the sun rising said anne going over to diana'),(26734,5,153,154041,'its so splendid to see the morning coming up over those long hills and glowing through those sharp fir tops'),(26735,5,153,137884,'its new every morning and i feel as if i washed my very soul in that bath of earliest sunshine'),(26736,5,5037,21583,'oh diana i love this little room so dearly'),(26737,5,279,19004,'i dont know how ill get along without it when i go to town next month'),(26738,5,5853,4247,'dont speak of your going away tonight begged diana'),(26739,5,279,5695,'i dont want to think of it it makes me so miserable and i do want to have a good time this evening'),(26740,5,994,30104,'what are you going to recite anne'),(26741,5,1,5620,'and are you nervous'),(26742,5,55,5381,'not a bit'),(26743,5,226572,2530,'ive recited so often in public i dont mind at all now'),(26744,5,226572,109128,'ive decided to give the maidens vow'),(26745,5,153,930,'its so pathetic'),(26746,5,421385,2714,'laura spencer is going to give a comic recitation but id rather make people cry than laugh'),(26747,5,994,2074,'what will you recite if they encore you'),(26748,5,1056,8843,'they wont dream of encoring me scoffed anne who was not without her own secret hopes that they would and already visioned herself