tself away in ready tears'),(26903,5,295,7445,'but that night when marilla went to bed acutely and miserably conscious that the little gable room at the end of the hall was untenanted by any vivid young life and unstirred by any soft breathing she buried her face in her pillow and wept for her girl in a passion of sobs that appalled her when she grew calm enough to reflect how very wicked it must be to take on so about a sinful fellow creature'),(26904,5,30104,129536,'anne and the rest of the avonlea scholars reached town just in time to hurry off to the academy'),(26905,5,163,425224,'that first day passed pleasantly enough in a whirl of excitement meeting all the new students learning to know the professors by sight and being assorted and organized into classes'),(26906,5,30104,648,'anne intended taking up the second year work being advised to do so by miss stacy gilbert blythe elected to do the same'),(26907,5,37,83,'this meant getting a first class teachers license in one year instead of two if they were successful but it also meant much more and harder work'),(26908,5,4,83,'jane ruby josie charlie and moody spurgeon not being troubled with the stirrings of ambition were content to take up the second class work'),(26909,5,30104,377968,'anne was conscious of a pang of loneliness when she found herself in a room with fifty other students not one of whom she knew except the tall brown-haired boy across the room and knowing him in the fashion she did did not help her much as she reflected pessimistically'),(26910,5,119,225492,'yet she was undeniably glad that they were in the same class the old rivalry could still be carried on and anne would hardly have known what to do if it had been lacking'),(26911,5,279,864,'i wouldnt feel comfortable without it she thought'),(26912,5,368685,4631,'gilbert looks awfully determined'),(26913,5,279,129556,'i suppose hes making up his mind here and now to win the medal'),(26914,5,994,21,'what a splendid chin he has'),(26915,5,279,509,'i never noticed it before'),(26916,5,279,813,'i do wish jane and ruby had gone in for first class too'),(26917,5,279,384,'i suppose i wont feel so much like a cat in a strange garret when i get acquainted though'),(26918,5,279,5485,'i wonder which of the girls here are going to be my friends'),(26919,5,153,108550,'its really an interesting speculation'),(26920,5,53,12374,'of course i promised diana that no queens girl no matter how much i liked her should ever be as dear to me as she is but ive lots of second-best affections to bestow'),(26921,5,279,193950,'i like the look of that girl with the brown eyes and the crimson waist'),(26922,5,729,6694,'she looks vivid and red-rosy theres that pale fair one gazing out of the window'),(26923,5,729,142935,'she has lovely hair and looks as if she knew a thing or two about dreams'),(26924,5,130931,425570,'id like to know them both--know them well--well enough to walk with my arm about their waists and call them nicknames'),(26925,5,295,7335,'but just now i dont know them and they dont know me and probably dont want to know me particularly'),(26926,5,5037,229079,'oh its lonesome'),(26927,5,67,128779,'it was lonesomer still when anne found herself alone in her hall bedroom that night at twilight'),(26928,5,729,112,'she was not to board with the other girls who all had relatives in town to take pity on them'),(26929,5,334,30104,'miss josephine barry would have liked to board her but beechwood was so far from the academy that it was out of the question so miss barry hunted up a boarding-house assuring matthew and marilla that it was the very place for anne'),(26930,5,51,340351,'the lady who keeps it is a reduced gentlewoman explained miss barry'),(26931,5,382,20076,'her husband was a british officer and she is very careful what sort of boarders she takes'),(26932,5,30104,151141,'anne will not meet with any objectionable persons under her roof'),(26933,5,51,165206,'the table is good and the house is near the academy in a quiet neighborhood'),(26934,5,172,382,'all this might be quite true and indeed proved to be so but it did not materially help anne in the first agony of homesickness that seized upon her'),(26935,5,729,53546,'she looked dismally about her narrow little room with its dull-papered pictureless walls its small iron bedstead and empty bookcase and a horrible choke came into her throat as she thought of her own white room at green gables where she would have the pleasant consciousness of a great green still outdoors of sweet peas growing in the garden and moonlight falling on the orchard of the brook below the slope and the spruce boughs tossing in the night wind beyond it of a vast starry sky and the light from dianas window shining out through the gap in the trees'),(26936,5,454,93284,'here there was nothing of this anne knew that outside of her window was a hard street with a network of telephone wires shutting out the sky the tramp of alien feet and a thousand lights gleaming on stranger faces'),(26937,5,729,67,'she knew that she was going to cry and fought against it'),(26938,5,279,98162,'i wont cry'),(26939,5,153,88499,'its silly--and weak--theres the third tear splashing down by my nose'),(26940,5,74,14215,'there are more coming'),(26941,5,279,112,'i must think of something funny to stop them'),(26942,5,295,4025,'but theres nothing funny except what is connected with avonlea and that only makes things worse--four--five--im going home next friday but that seems a hundred years away'),(26943,5,5037,112,'oh matthew is nearly home by now--and marilla is at the gate looking down the lane for him--six--seven--eight--oh theres no use in counting them'),(26944,5,229084,728,'theyre coming in a flood presently'),(26945,5,279,1274,'i cant cheer up--i dont want to cheer up'),(26946,5,153,16425,'its nicer to be miserable'),(26947,5,51,2845,'the flood of tears would have come no doubt had not josie pye appeared at that moment'),(26948,5,50,369126,'in the joy of seeing a familiar face anne forgot that there had never been much love lost between her and josie'),(26949,5,42,4687,'as a part of avonlea life even a pye was welcome'),(26950,5,4348,41485,'im so glad you came up anne said sincerely'),(26951,5,226481,17540,'youve been crying remarked josie with aggravating pity'),(26952,5,279,1614,'i suppose youre homesick--some people have so little self-control in that respect'),(26953,5,226572,2074,'ive no intention of being homesick i can tell you'),(26954,5,426098,333528,'towns too jolly after that poky old avonlea'),(26955,5,279,2583,'i wonder how i ever existed there so long'),(26956,5,2074,13125,'you shouldnt cry anne it isnt becoming for your nose and eyes get red and then you seem all red'),(26957,5,130931,210285,'id a perfectly scrumptious time in the academy today'),(26958,5,2043,406030,'our french professor is simply a duck'),(26959,5,892,1308,'his moustache would give you kerwollowps of the heart'),(26960,5,254,30104,'have you anything eatable around anne'),(26961,5,4348,182079,'im literally starving'),(26962,5,5499,87577,'ah i guessed likely marilla d load you up with cake'),(26963,5,228305,4732,'thats why i called round'),(26964,5,699,426199,'otherwise id have gone to the park to hear the band play with frank stockley'),(26965,5,258,46210,'he boards same place as i do and hes a sport'),(26966,5,258,473,'he noticed you in class today and asked me who the red-headed girl was'),(26967,5,279,163,'i told him you were an orphan that the cuthberts had adopted and nobody knew very much about what youd been before that'),(26968,5,30104,6700,'anne was wondering if after all solitude and tears were not more satisfactory than josie pyes companionship when jane and ruby appeared each with an inch of queens color ribbon--purple and scarlet--pinned proudly to her coat'),(26969,5,42,426299,'as josie was not speaking to jane just then she had to subside into comparative harmlessness'),(26970,5,43,6169,'well said jane with a sigh i feel as if id lived many moons since the morning'),(26971,5,279,235994,'i ought to be home studying my virgil--that horrid old professor gave us twenty lines to start in on tomorrow'),(26972,5,295,207221,'but i simply couldnt settle down to study tonight'),(26973,5,30104,62883,'anne methinks i see the traces of tears'),(26974,5,257,1274,'if youve been crying do own up'),(26975,5,67,12535,'it will restore my self-respect for i was shedding tears freely before ruby came along'),(26976,5,279,813,'i dont mind being a goose so much if somebody else is goosey too'),(26977,5,104155,2074,'youll give me a teeny piece wont you'),(26978,5,18463,2074,'thank you'),(26979,5,67,318965,'it has the real avonlea flavor'),(26980,5,332585,129556,'ruby perceiving the queens calendar lying on the table wanted to know if anne meant to try for the gold medal'),(26981,5,30104,67,'anne blushed and admitted she was thinking of it'),(26982,5,5037,172,'oh that reminds me said josie queens is to get one of the avery scholarships after all'),(26983,5,51,210285,'the word came today'),(26984,5,667,515,'frank stockley told me--his uncle is one of the board of governors you know'),(26985,5,67,235994,'it will be announced in the academy tomorrow'),(26986,5,573,426481,'an avery scholarship'),(26987,5,30104,2111,'anne felt her heart beat more quickly and the horizons of her ambition shifted and broadened as if by magic'),(26988,5,509,129556,'before josie had told the news annes highest pinnacle of aspiration had been a teachers provincial license first class at the end of the year and perhaps the medal'),(26989,5,295,4025,'but now in one moment anne saw herself winning the avery scholarship taking an arts course at redmond college and graduating in a gown and mortar board before the echo of josies words had died away'),(26990,5,284,426584,'for the avery scholarship was in english and anne felt that here her foot was on native heath'),(26991,5,23,426624,'a wealthy manufacturer of new brunswick had died and left part of his fortune to endow a large number of scholarships to be distributed among the various high schools and academies of the maritime provinces according to their respective standings'),(26992,5,74,414164,'there had been much doubt whether one would be allotted to queens but the matter was settled at last and at the end of the year the graduate who made the highest mark in english and english literature would win the scholarship--two hundred and fifty dollars a year for four years at redmond college'),(26993,5,655,63657,'no wonder that anne went to bed that night with tingling cheeks'),(26994,5,5886,18816,'ill win that scholarship if hard work can do it she resolved'),(26995,5,218657,89502,'wouldnt matthew be proud if i got to be a b'),(26996,5,5037,426719,'oh its delightful to have ambitions'),(26997,5,4348,18418,'im so glad i have such a lot'),(26998,5,1,67,'and there never seems to be any end to them--thats the best of it'),(26999,5,26,1040,'just as soon as you attain to one ambition you see another one glittering higher up still'),(27000,5,67,2323,'it does make life so interesting'),(27001,5,4328,65073,'chapter xxxv'),(27002,5,51,8866,'the winter at queens annes homesickness wore off greatly helped in the wearing by her weekend visits home'),(27003,5,42,9700,'as long as the open weather lasted the avonlea students went out to carmody on the new branch railway every friday night'),(27004,5,4247,283,'diana and several other avonlea young folks were generally on hand to meet them and they all walked over to avonlea in a merry party'),(27005,5,30104,5027,'anne thought those friday evening gypsyings over the autumnal hills in the crisp golden air with the homelights of avonlea twinkling beyond were the best and dearest hours in the whole week'),(27006,5,368685,382,'gilbert blythe nearly always walked with ruby gillis and carried her satchel for her'),(27007,5,332585,8866,'ruby was a very handsome young lady now thinking herself quite as grown up as she really was she wore her skirts as long as her mother would let her and did her hair up in town though she had to take it down when she went home'),(27008,5,729,7012,'she had large bright-blue eyes a brilliant complexion and a plump showy figure'),(27009,5,729,119836,'she laughed a great deal was cheerful and good-tempered and enjoyed the pleasant things of life frankly'),(27010,5,295,30104,'but i shouldnt think she was the sort of girl gilbert would like whispered jane to anne'),(27011,5,30104,426481,'anne did not think so either but she would not have said so for the avery scholarship'),(27012,5,729,426719,'she could not help thinking too that it would be very pleasant to have such a friend as gilbert to jest and chatter with and exchange ideas about books and studies and ambitions'),(27013,5,368685,69209,'gilbert had ambitions she knew and ruby gillis did not seem the sort of person with whom such could be profitably discussed'),(27014,5,74,368685,'there was no silly sentiment in annes ideas concerning gilbert'),(27015,5,43135,147006,'boys were to her when she thought about them at all merely possible good comrades'),(27016,5,257,4492,'if she and gilbert had been friends she would not have cared how many other friends he had nor with whom he walked'),(27017,5,729,1656,'she had a genius for friendship girl friends she had in plenty but she had a vague consciousness that masculine friendship might also be a good thing to round out ones conceptions of companionship and furnish broader standpoints of judgment and comparison'),(27018,5,55,68342,'not that anne could have put her feelings on the matter into just such clear definition'),(27019,5,295,67211,'but she thought that if gilbert had ever walked home with her from the train over the crisp fields and along the ferny byways they might have had many and merry and interesting conversations about the new world that was opening around them and their hopes and ambitions therein'),(27020,5,368685,67,'gilbert was a clever young fellow with his own thoughts about things and a determination to get the best out of life and put the best into it'),(27021,5,332585,40,'ruby gillis told jane andrews that she didnt understand half the things gilbert blythe said he talked just like anne shirley did when she had a thoughtful fit on and for her part she didnt think it any fun to be bothering about books and that sort of thing when you didnt have to'),(27022,5,667,82,'frank stockley had lots more dash and go but then he wasnt half as good-looking as gilbert and she really couldnt decide which she liked best'),(27023,5,50,1755,'in the academy anne gradually drew a little circle of friends about her thoughtful imaginative ambitious students like herself'),(27024,5,34,764,'with the rose-red girl stella maynard and the dream girl priscilla grant she soon became intimate finding the latter pale spiritual-looking maiden to be full to the brim of mischief and pranks and fun while the vivid black-eyed stella had a heartful of wistful dreams and fancies as aerial and rainbow-like as annes own'),(27025,5,171,83,'after the christmas holidays the avonlea students gave up going home on fridays and settled down to hard work'),(27026,5,3,427427,'by this time all the queens scholars had gravitated into their own places in the ranks and the various classes had assumed distinct and settled shadings of individuality'),(27027,5,1193,1478,'certain facts had become generally accepted'),(27028,5,67,129553,'it was admitted that the medal contestants had practically narrowed down to three--gilbert blythe anne shirley and lewis wilson the avery scholarship was more doubtful any one of a certain six being a possible winner'),(27029,5,51,6700,'the bronze medal for mathematics was considered as good as won by a fat funny little up-country boy with a bumpy forehead and a patched coat'),(27030,5,332585,341716,'ruby gillis was the handsomest girl of the year at the academy in the second year classes stella maynard carried off the palm for beauty with small but critical minority in favor of anne shirley'),(27031,5,427530,611,'ethel marr was admitted by all competent judges to have the most stylish modes of hair-dressing and jane andrews--plain plodding conscientious jane--carried off the honors in the domestic science course'),(27032,5,1232,215561,'even josie pye attained a certain preeminence as the sharpest-tongued young lady in attendance at queens'),(27033,5,1051,611,'so it may be fairly stated that miss stacys old pupils held their own in the wider arena of the academical course'),(27034,5,30104,27218,'anne worked hard and steadily'),(27035,5,382,67,'her rivalry with gilbert was as intense as it had ever been in avonlea school although it was not known in the class at large but somehow the bitterness had gone out of it'),(27036,5,30104,414386,'anne no longer wished to win for the sake of defeating gilbert rather for the proud consciousness of a well-won victory over a worthy foeman'),(27037,5,67,55,'it would be worth while to win but she no longer thought life would be insupportable if she did not'),(27038,5,50,1061,'in spite of lessons the students found opportunities for pleasant times'),(27039,5,30104,340351,'anne spent many of her spare hours at beechwood and generally ate her sunday dinners there and went to church with miss barry'),(27040,5,51,427740,'the latter was as she admitted growing old but her black eyes were not dim nor the vigor of her tongue in the least abated'),(27041,5,295,1293,'but she never sharpened the latter on anne who continued to be a prime favorite with the critical old lady'),(27042,5,163,2257,'that anne-girl improves all the time she said'),(27043,5,279,112,'i get tired of other girls--there is such a provoking and eternal sameness about them'),(27044,5,30104,427800,'anne has as many shades as a rainbow and every shade is the prettiest while it lasts'),(27045,5,279,112,'i dont know that she is as amusing as she was when she was a child but she makes me love her and i like people who make me love them'),(27046,5,67,112,'it saves me so much trouble in making myself love them'),(27047,5,1502,240397,'then almost before anybody realized it spring had come out in avonlea the mayflowers were peeping pinkly out on the sere barrens where snow-wreaths lingered and the mist of green was on the woods and in the valleys'),(27048,5,295,378206,'but in charlottetown harassed queens students thought and talked only of examinations'),(27049,5,67,30104,'it doesnt seem possible that the term is nearly over said anne'),(27050,5,4949,425224,'why last fall it seemed so long to look forward to--a whole winter of studies and classes'),(27051,5,1,5027,'and here we are with the exams looming up next week'),(27052,5,4407,17468,'girls sometimes i feel as if those exams meant everything but when i look at the big buds swelling on those chestnut trees and the misty blue air at the end of the streets they dont seem half so important'),(27053,5,4,67,'jane and ruby and josie who had dropped in did not take this view of it'),(27054,5,40,428006,'to them the coming examinations were constantly very important indeed--far more important than chestnut buds or maytime hazes'),(27055,5,67,428046,'it was all very well for anne who was sure of passing at least to have her moments of belittling them but when your whole future depended on them--as the girls truly thought theirs did--you could not regard them philosophically'),(27056,5,226572,4,'ive lost seven pounds in the last two weeks sighed jane'),(27057,5,153,200508,'its no use to say dont worry'),(27058,5,279,200508,'i will worry'),(27059,5,428068,428068,'worrying helps you some--it seems as if you were doing something when youre worrying'),(27060,5,67,29521,'it would be dreadful if i failed to get my license after going to queens all winter and spending so much money'),(27061,5,279,369127,'i dont care said josie pye'),(27062,5,257,5026,'if i dont pass this year im coming back next'),(27063,5,413,424,'my father can afford to send 