rt last sunday'),(27364,5,4328,71455,'chapter xxxviii'),(27365,5,51,5695,'the bend in the road marilla went to town the next day and returned in the evening'),(27366,5,30104,410,'anne had gone over to orchard slope with diana and came back to find marilla in the kitchen sitting by the table with her head leaning on her hand'),(27367,5,707,1308,'something in her dejected attitude struck a chill to annes heart'),(27368,5,729,163,'she had never seen marilla sit limply inert like that'),(27369,5,60,333265,'are you very tired marilla'),(27370,5,401965,1274,'yes--no--i dont know said marilla wearily looking up'),(27371,5,279,67,'i suppose i am tired but i havent thought about it'),(27372,5,153,163,'its not that'),(27373,5,991,429660,'did you see the oculist'),(27374,5,994,794,'what did he say'),(27375,5,7867,89575,'asked anne anxiously'),(27376,5,9487,247,'yes i saw him'),(27377,5,258,917,'he examined my eyes'),(27378,5,258,408346,'he says that if i give up all reading and sewing entirely and any kind of work that strains the eyes and if im careful not to cry and if i wear the glasses hes given me he thinks my eyes may not get any worse and my headaches will be cured'),(27379,5,295,36932,'but if i dont he says ill certainly be stone-blind in six months'),(27380,5,30104,67,'anne just think of it'),(27381,5,284,11760,'for a minute anne after her first quick exclamation of dismay was silent'),(27382,5,67,4634,'it seemed to her that she could not speak'),(27383,5,1502,67,'then she said bravely but with a catch in her voice marilla dont think of it'),(27384,5,2074,2068,'you know he has given you hope'),(27385,5,257,109,'if you are careful you wont lose your sight altogether and if his glasses cure your headaches it will be a great thing'),(27386,5,279,23063,'i dont call it much hope said marilla bitterly'),(27387,5,994,163,'what am i to live for if i cant read or sew or do anything like that'),(27388,5,279,22945,'i might as well be blind--or dead'),(27389,5,1,229079,'and as for crying i cant help that when i get lonesome'),(27390,5,295,67,'but there its no good talking about it'),(27391,5,257,19569,'if youll get me a cup of tea ill be thankful'),(27392,5,4348,976,'im about done out'),(27393,5,5853,129820,'dont say anything about this to any one for a spell yet anyway'),(27394,5,279,67,'i cant bear that folks should come here to question and sympathize and talk about it'),(27395,5,483,14397,'when marilla had eaten her lunch anne persuaded her to go to bed'),(27396,5,1502,1308,'then anne went herself to the east gable and sat down by her window in the darkness alone with her tears and her heaviness of heart'),(27397,5,1338,8866,'how sadly things had changed since she had sat there the night after coming home'),(27398,5,1502,9907,'then she had been full of hope and joy and the future had looked rosy with promise'),(27399,5,30104,1308,'anne felt as if she had lived years since then but before she went to bed there was a smile on her lips and peace in her heart'),(27400,5,729,119836,'she had looked her duty courageously in the face and found it a friend--as duty ever is when we meet it frankly'),(27401,5,139,333969,'one afternoon a few days later marilla came slowly in from the front yard where she had been talking to a caller--a man whom anne knew by sight as sadler from carmody'),(27402,5,30104,11135,'anne wondered what he could have been saying to bring that look to marillas face'),(27403,5,994,636,'what did mr'),(27404,5,433117,333265,'sadler want marilla'),(27405,5,333265,30104,'marilla sat down by the window and looked at anne'),(27406,5,74,67,'there were tears in her eyes in defiance of the oculists prohibition and her voice broke as she said he heard that i was going to sell green gables and he wants to buy it'),(27407,5,15674,67,'buy it'),(27408,5,15674,333242,'buy green gables'),(27409,5,30104,185582,'anne wondered if she had heard aright'),(27410,5,5037,333242,'oh marilla you dont mean to sell green gables'),(27411,5,30104,3873,'anne i dont know what else is to be done'),(27412,5,226572,554,'ive thought it all over'),(27413,5,257,1278,'if my eyes were strong i could stay here and make out to look after things and manage with a good hired man'),(27414,5,295,73318,'but as it is i cant'),(27415,5,279,314,'i may lose my sight altogether and anyway ill not be fit to run things'),(27416,5,5037,8866,'oh i never thought id live to see the day when id have to sell my home'),(27417,5,295,67,'but things would only go behind worse and worse all the time till nobody would want to buy it'),(27418,5,138,11932,'every cent of our money went in that bank and theres some notes matthew gave last fall to pay'),(27419,5,333254,302,'lynde advises me to sell the farm and board somewhere--with her i suppose'),(27420,5,67,2018,'it wont bring much--its small and the buildings are old'),(27421,5,295,37695,'but itll be enough for me to live on i reckon'),(27422,5,4348,30104,'im thankful youre provided for with that scholarship anne'),(27423,5,4348,9844,'im sorry you wont have a home to come to in your vacations thats all but i suppose youll manage somehow'),(27424,5,333265,23063,'marilla broke down and wept bitterly'),(27425,5,2074,88610,'you mustnt sell green gables said anne resolutely'),(27426,5,5037,40,'oh anne i wish i didnt have to'),(27427,5,295,7312,'but you can see for yourself'),(27428,5,279,8177,'i cant stay here alone'),(27429,5,130931,425306,'id go crazy with trouble and loneliness'),(27430,5,1,126,'and my sight would go--i know it would'),(27431,5,2074,333265,'you wont have to stay here alone marilla'),(27432,5,5886,2074,'ill be with you'),(27433,5,4348,426548,'im not going to redmond'),(27434,5,55,426548,'not going to redmond'),(27435,5,333265,30104,'marilla lifted her worn face from her hands and looked at anne'),(27436,5,4949,1577,'why what do you mean'),(27437,5,26,794,'just what i say'),(27438,5,4348,426481,'im not going to take the scholarship'),(27439,5,279,4602,'i decided so the night after you came home from town'),(27440,5,2074,424,'you surely dont think i could leave you alone in your trouble marilla after all youve done for me'),(27441,5,226572,20042,'ive been thinking and planning'),(27442,5,2792,28339,'let me tell you my plans'),(27443,5,340351,3855,'barry wants to rent the farm for next year'),(27444,5,1051,163,'so you wont have any bother over that'),(27445,5,1,58932,'and im going to teach'),(27446,5,226572,368686,'ive applied for the school here--but i dont expect to get it for i understand the trustees have promised it to gilbert blythe'),(27447,5,295,433574,'but i can have the carmody school--mr'),(27448,5,379805,140890,'blair told me so last night at the store'),(27449,5,53,873,'of course that wont be quite as nice or convenient as if i had the avonlea school'),(27450,5,295,309,'but i can board home and drive myself over to carmody and back in the warm weather at least'),(27451,5,1,396284,'and even in winter i can come home fridays'),(27452,5,43,163,'well keep a horse for that'),(27453,5,5037,333265,'oh i have it all planned out marilla'),(27454,5,1,1274,'and ill read to you and keep you cheered up'),(27455,5,2074,229079,'you shant be dull or lonesome'),(27456,5,1,279,'and well be real cozy and happy here together you and i'),(27457,5,333265,135860,'marilla had listened like a woman in a dream'),(27458,5,5037,515,'oh anne i could get on real well if you were here i know'),(27459,5,295,424,'but i cant let you sacrifice yourself so for me'),(27460,5,67,130739,'it would be terrible'),(27461,5,30104,202528,'anne laughed merrily'),(27462,5,74,19587,'there is no sacrifice'),(27463,5,1155,77,'nothing could be worse than giving up green gables--nothing could hurt me more'),(27464,5,1649,461,'we must keep the dear old place'),(27465,5,413,333265,'my mind is quite made up marilla'),(27466,5,4348,58932,'im not going to redmond and i am going to stay here and teach'),(27467,5,5853,5381,'dont you worry about me a bit'),(27468,5,295,784,'but your ambitions--and-- im just as ambitious as ever'),(27469,5,241,426719,'only ive changed the object of my ambitions'),(27470,5,4348,137448,'im going to be a good teacher--and im going to save your eyesight'),(27471,5,16049,1718,'besides i mean to study at home here and take a little college course all by myself'),(27472,5,5037,333265,'oh ive dozens of plans marilla'),(27473,5,226572,5027,'ive been thinking them out for a week'),(27474,5,279,6135,'i shall give life here my best and i believe it will give its best to me in return'),(27475,5,483,53024,'when i left queens my future seemed to stretch out before me like a straight road'),(27476,5,279,433869,'i thought i could see along it for many a milestone'),(27477,5,2530,67,'now there is a bend in it'),(27478,5,279,297,'i dont know what lies around the bend but im going to believe that the best does'),(27479,5,67,333265,'it has a fascination of its own that bend marilla'),(27480,5,279,407,'i wonder how the road beyond it goes--what there is of green glory and soft checkered light and shadows--what new landscapes--what new beauties--what curves and hills and valleys further on'),(27481,5,279,426481,'i dont feel as if i ought to let you give it up said marilla referring to the scholarship'),(27482,5,295,424,'but you cant prevent me'),(27483,5,4348,273,'im sixteen and a half obstinate as a mule as mrs'),(27484,5,333254,30104,'lynde once told me laughed anne'),(27485,5,5037,424,'oh marilla dont you go pitying me'),(27486,5,279,67,'i dont like to be pitied and there is no need for it'),(27487,5,4348,333242,'im heart glad over the very thought of staying at dear green gables'),(27488,5,7899,67,'nobody could love it as you and i do--so we must keep it'),(27489,5,2074,1873,'you blessed girl'),(27490,5,2257,3360,'said marilla yielding'),(27491,5,279,534,'i feel as if youd given me new life'),(27492,5,279,46472,'i guess i ought to stick out and make you go to college--but i know i cant so i aint going to try'),(27493,5,5886,30104,'ill make it up to you though anne'),(27494,5,483,67,'when it became noised abroad in avonlea that anne shirley had given up the idea of going to college and intended to stay home and teach there was a good deal of discussion over it'),(27495,5,387,13077,'most of the good folks not knowing about marillas eyes thought she was foolish'),(27496,5,389358,55,'allan did not'),(27497,5,729,917,'she told anne so in approving words that brought tears of pleasure to the girls eyes'),(27498,5,3483,273,'neither did good mrs'),(27499,5,729,194337,'she came up one evening and found anne and marilla sitting at the front door in the warm scented summer dusk'),(27500,5,1056,6950,'they liked to sit there when the twilight came down and the white moths flew about in the garden and the odor of mint filled the dewy air'),(27501,5,333253,16441,'rachel deposited her substantial person upon the stone bench by the door behind which grew a row of tall pink and yellow hollyhocks with a long breath of mingled weariness and relief'),(27502,5,279,4332,'i declare im getting glad to sit down'),(27503,5,226572,4732,'ive been on my feet all day and two hundred pounds is a good bit for two feet to carry round'),(27504,5,153,333265,'its a great blessing not to be fat marilla'),(27505,5,279,67,'i hope you appreciate it'),(27506,5,43,414164,'well anne i hear youve given up your notion of going to college'),(27507,5,279,67,'i was real glad to hear it'),(27508,5,226481,34,'youve got as much education now as a woman can be comfortable with'),(27509,5,279,5103,'i dont believe in girls going to college with the men and cramming their heads full of latin and greek and all that nonsense'),(27510,5,295,273,'but im going to study latin and greek just the same mrs'),(27511,5,333254,37476,'lynde said anne laughing'),(27512,5,4348,414164,'im going to take my arts course right here at green gables and study everything that i would at college'),(27513,5,333254,68347,'lynde lifted her hands in holy horror'),(27514,5,30104,7312,'anne shirley youll kill yourself'),(27515,5,55,67,'not a bit of it'),(27516,5,279,67,'i shall thrive on it'),(27517,5,5037,314,'oh im not going to overdo things'),(27518,5,42,434393,'as josiah allens wife says i shall be mejum'),(27519,5,295,83,'but ill have lots of spare time in the long winter evenings and ive no vocation for fancy work'),(27520,5,4348,515,'im going to teach over at carmody you know'),(27521,5,279,67,'i dont know it'),(27522,5,279,333528,'i guess youre going to teach right here in avonlea'),(27523,5,51,873,'the trustees have decided to give you the school'),(27524,5,4916,8294,'cried anne springing to her feet in her surprise'),(27525,5,4949,368686,'why i thought they had promised it to gilbert blythe'),(27526,5,1051,991,'so they did'),(27527,5,295,9637,'but as soon as gilbert heard that you had applied for it he went to them--they had a business meeting at the school last night you know--and told them that he withdrew his application and suggested that they accept yours'),(27528,5,258,165586,'he said he was going to teach at white sands'),(27529,5,53,994,'of course he knew how much you wanted to stay with marilla and i must say i think it was real kind and thoughtful in him thats what'),(27530,5,2033,414164,'real self-sacrificing too for hell have his board to pay at white sands and everybody knows hes got to earn his own way through college'),(27531,5,1051,2074,'so the trustees decided to take you'),(27532,5,279,424,'i was tickled to death when thomas came home and told me'),(27533,5,279,30104,'i dont feel that i ought to take it murmured anne'),(27534,5,279,424,'i mean--i dont think i ought to let gilbert make such a sacrifice for--for me'),(27535,5,279,2530,'i guess you cant prevent him now'),(27536,5,228518,393134,'hes signed papers with the white sands trustees'),(27537,5,1051,12054,'so it wouldnt do him any good now if you were to refuse'),(27538,5,53,873,'of course youll take the school'),(27539,5,104155,7913,'youll get along all right now that there are no pyes going'),(27540,5,369126,994,'josie was the last of them and a good thing she was thats what'),(27541,5,226864,8866,'theres been some pye or other going to avonlea school for the last twenty years and i guess their mission in life was to keep school teachers reminded that earth isnt their home'),(27542,5,68061,1308,'bless my heart'),(27543,5,994,1577,'what does all that winking and blinking at the barry gable mean'),(27544,5,4247,30104,'diana is signaling for me to go over laughed anne'),(27545,5,2074,1202,'you know we keep up the old custom'),(27546,5,9933,41887,'excuse me while i run over and see what she wants'),(27547,5,30104,79998,'anne ran down the clover slope like a deer and disappeared in the firry shadows of the haunted wood'),(27548,5,333254,136528,'lynde looked after her indulgently'),(27549,5,226864,1917,'theres a good deal of the child about her yet in some ways'),(27550,5,226864,357023,'theres a good deal more of the woman about her in others retorted marilla with a momentary return of her old crispness'),(27551,5,295,390,'but crispness was no longer marillas distinguishing characteristic'),(27552,5,42,273,'as mrs'),(27553,5,333254,9700,'lynde told her thomas that night'),(27554,5,333265,172429,'marilla cuthbert has got mellow'),(27555,5,228305,994,'thats what'),(27556,5,30104,374194,'anne went to the little avonlea graveyard the next evening to put fresh flowers on matthews grave and water the scotch rosebush'),(27557,5,729,322199,'she lingered there until dusk liking the peace and calm of the little place with its poplars whose rustle was like low friendly speech and its whispering grasses growing at will among the graves'),(27558,5,483,23386,'when she finally left it and walked down the long hill that sloped to the lake of shining waters it was past sunset and all avonlea lay before her in a dreamlike afterlight--a haunt of ancient peace'),(27559,5,74,343678,'there was a freshness in the air as of a wind that had blown over honey-sweet fields of clover'),(27560,5,8866,53546,'home lights twinkled out here and there among the homestead trees'),(27561,5,1653,140362,'beyond lay the sea misty and purple with its haunting unceasing murmur'),(27562,5,51,340101,'the west was a glory of soft mingled hues and the pond reflected them all in still softer shadings'),(27563,5,51,67,'the beauty of it all thrilled annes heart and she gratefully opened the gates of her soul to it'),(27564,5,2783,2074,'dear old world she murmured you are very lovely and i am glad to be alive in you'),(27565,5,207546,334375,'halfway down the hill a tall lad came whistling out of a gate before the blythe homestead'),(27566,5,67,30104,'it was gilbert and the whistle died on his lips as he recognized anne'),(27567,5,258,410,'he lifted his cap courteously but he would have passed on in silence if anne had not stopped and held out her hand'),(27568,5,368685,424,'gilbert she said with scarlet cheeks i want to thank you for giving up the school for me'),(27569,5,67,67,'it was very good of you--and i want you to know that i appreciate it'),(27570,5,368685,11618,'gilbert took the offered hand eagerly'),(27571,5,67,30104,'it wasnt particularly good of me at all anne'),(27572,5,279,16362,'i was pleased to be able to do you some small service'),(27573,5,60,37,'are we going to be friends after this'),(27574,5,254,8377,'have you really forgiven me my old fault'),(27575,5,30104,410,'anne laughed and tried unsuccessfully to withdraw her hand'),(27576,5,279,67,'i forgave you that day by the pond landing although i didnt know it'),(27577,5,994,473,'what a stubborn little goose i was'),(27578,5,226572,5543,'ive been--i may as well make a complete confession--ive been sorry ever since'),(27579,5,1649,435209,'we are going to be the best of friends said gilbert jubilantly'),(27580,5,1649,30104,'we were born to be good friends anne'),(27581,5,226481,566,'youve thwarted destiny enough'),(27582,5,279,1917,'i know we can help each other in many ways'),(27583,5,2074,2074,'you are going to keep up your studies arent you'),(27584,5,1051,279,'so am i'),(27585,5,3479,2074,'come im going to walk home with you'),(27586,5,333265,24345,'marilla looked curiously at anne when the latter entered the kitchen'),(27587,5,59,30104,'who was that came up the lane with you anne'),(27588,5,368685,17292,'gilbert blythe answered anne vexed to find herself blushing'),(27589,5,279,1850,'i met him on barrys hill'),(27590,5,279,4716,'i didnt think you and gilbert blythe were such good friends that youd stand for half an hour at the gate talking to him said marilla with a dry smile'),(27591,5,1649,34021,'we havent been--weve been good enemies'),(27592,5,295,24280,'but we have decided that it will be much more sensible to be good friends in the future'),(27593,5,798,8903,'were we really there half an hour'),(27594,5,67,6645,'it seemed just a few minutes'),(27595,5,295,333265,'but you see we have five years lost conversations to catch up with marilla'),(27596,5,30104,76968,'anne sat long at her window that night companioned by a glad content'),(27597,5,51,382,'the wind purred softly in the cherry boughs and the mint breaths came up to her'),(27598,5,51,152851,'the stars twinkled over the pointed firs in the hollow and dianas light gleamed through the old gap'),(27599,5,59486,67,'annes horizons had closed in since the night she had sat there after coming home from queens but if the path set before her feet was to be narrow she knew that flowers of quiet happiness would bloom along it'),(27600,5,51,142935,'the joy of sincere work and worthy aspiration and congenial friendship were to be hers nothing could rob her of her birthright of fancy or her ideal world of dreams'),(27601,5,1,53024,'and there was always the bend in the road'),(27602,5,7986,173697,'gods in his heaven alls right with the world whispered anne softly');
